const e=[{id:1,icon:"Snowflake",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Winter safety",subtitle:"Protecting yourself from the cold and the dangers of winter",summary:"Winter is a beautiful season, but it also has its dangers: cold, snow, ice, slipping and poisonous gas from heaters. We need to know how to protect ourselves and our families.",blocks:[{heading:"Heating your home safely",body:`Use gas, kerosene and electric heaters correctly and according to the manufacturer's instructions.
• Keep an opening for fresh air in the room, because gas and kerosene heaters produce the poisonous gas carbon monoxide (CO).
• Do not leave a heater switched on while you sleep.`},{heading:"Protection from the cold",body:"Wear warm clothes in layers. Cover your head, hands and feet well. When the weather is very cold, do not stay outside for a long time."},{heading:"Driving safely on snow and ice",body:"Drive slowly on snow and ice. Increase the distance between your car and the car in front. Use winter tires or snow chains."},{heading:"Preventing frozen pipes",body:"Water pipes can freeze and burst in the cold. Wrap and insulate outdoor pipes. On very cold nights, let a little water run slowly from the faucet."}],tips:["Install a CO (carbon monoxide) alarm at home.","Turn off the gas heater before you go to sleep.","Wear clothes in layers: they are better than one thick garment.","Keep a first aid kit and a blanket in the car.","Make sure your phone is fully charged when you go out."],warnings:["Do not place a heater near curtains or clothes.","Keep children away from heaters.","Do not let heavy snow build up on the roof of the house.","Do not overload one electrical outlet with several electric heaters."],activity:{prompt:"You are at home and the gas heater is on. A family member has a headache and feels dizzy. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I get everyone out into fresh air at once (I turn off the heater on the way only if it is quick and safe), call 122 even if they start to feel better, and nobody goes back in until the room is aired and the heater is checked",correct:!0,feedback:"Headache and dizziness near a gas heater can mean CO poisoning, so fresh air and a call to 122 come first."},{text:"I give them a headache tablet and leave the heater on",correct:!1,feedback:"A tablet does not help if the heater is giving off CO; get everyone out into fresh air and call 122."},{text:"I only turn off the heater and we stay in the room",correct:!1,feedback:"Turning off the heater is not enough: the poisonous gas stays in the room, so everyone must get out into fresh air."},{text:"I do nothing and wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting is dangerous: the gas keeps building up, so get everyone out into fresh air and call 122."}]},quiz:[{question:"Why should there be an opening for fresh air in the room when a gas heater is on?",options:[{text:"So that the room smells nice",correct:!1},{text:"To prevent the poisonous gas carbon monoxide (CO) from building up",correct:!0},{text:"So that the room does not get cold",correct:!1},{text:"So that no noise is made",correct:!1}],explanation:"Gas and kerosene heaters produce carbon monoxide (CO), a poisonous gas. An opening for fresh air keeps it from building up in the room."},{question:"Which clothes are best for protection from the cold?",options:[{text:"One thick garment",correct:!1},{text:"Clothes in layers",correct:!0},{text:"Thin clothes",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"Several layers of warm clothes protect better than one thick garment. Also cover your head, hands and feet well."},{question:"How do we prevent water pipes from freezing?",options:[{text:"We wrap the pipes and, on very cold nights, let a little water run slowly",correct:!0},{text:"We do nothing",correct:!1},{text:"We light a fire under the pipes",correct:!1},{text:"We break the pipes",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water pipes can freeze and burst in the cold. Wrap and insulate outdoor pipes, and on very cold nights let a little water run slowly from the faucet."}]},{id:2,icon:"Sun",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Summer safety",subtitle:"Protecting yourself from heat and wildfires",summary:"Summer is a hot season with its own dangers, such as heatstroke, crop fires, wildfires, drowning and water shortages. Let us learn how to protect ourselves.",blocks:[{heading:"Protection from heatstroke",body:"Drink water throughout the day, before you feel thirsty. Stay out of the sun during the hottest hours of the day. Wear light, light-colored clothes and a hat."},{heading:"Heat exhaustion or heatstroke?",body:`• Heat exhaustion: heavy sweating, cold, pale and clammy skin, weakness, dizziness, headache, nausea and thirst. Move the person to a cool place, loosen their clothing, cool them with water and give sips of water if they are fully alert. Get medical help if they vomit, if the symptoms get worse or last more than an hour, or if they become confused (that is heatstroke).
• Heatstroke: a very hot body with confusion, strange behavior, slurred speech, a seizure or collapse; the skin may be hot and dry or still sweating. This is an emergency: call 122, move the person to a cool place, remove extra clothing and cool them at once, before they are taken to the hospital. The best way is to put the body up to the neck in cold water for 15 minutes or until they are thinking clearly again, whichever comes first; if that is not possible, use a cold shower, cold wet towels or sheets and ice packs, and fan them. Give nothing to drink to a person who is confused.`},{heading:"Preventing wildfires",body:"Do not light fires in forests or among dry grass. Do not throw away cigarettes. Do not leave glass bottles outside, because they can act like a lens, focus the sun's rays and start a fire."},{heading:"Safety at lakes and rivers",body:"Never swim alone. Swim only in safe places where there is supervision. Wear a life jacket. Never take your eyes off children."},{heading:"Keeping food and water safe",body:"In summer, food spoils quickly. Keep food in the refrigerator. Drink clean water. Wash fruit and vegetables well."}],tips:["Drink water throughout the day: usually 2 liters or more.","Stay out of the sun at midday and in the afternoon, when the heat is at its highest.","Use sunscreen (SPF 30+).","Eat fruit with a high water content (such as watermelon and melon).","Never leave children or animals alone in a hot car."],warnings:["Heatstroke can kill: drink plenty of water and stay in the shade.","Fire spreads quickly in forests.","Drowning: never leave children near water without supervision.","Spoiled food causes food poisoning."],activity:{prompt:"You are outside and feel dizzy and weak. Your body has become very hot. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I move into the shade, drink water, cool my body with cool water and call 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Dizziness, weakness and a very hot body are warning signs of heat danger: get into the shade, cool down and call 122."},{text:"I carry on with my work",correct:!1,feedback:"Carrying on with work keeps heating your body; stop, move to a cool place, cool down and call 122."},{text:"I stay in the sun and only drink iced water",correct:!1,feedback:"Staying in the sun keeps heating your body; move into the shade and cool your body with water."},{text:"I lie down in the sun and go to sleep",correct:!1,feedback:"Lying in the sun makes your body even hotter; move to a cool place, cool down and call 122."}]},quiz:[{question:"Why should we not leave glass water bottles outside in the sun?",options:[{text:"Because the water gets hot",correct:!1},{text:"Because they can act like a lens and start a fire",correct:!0},{text:"Because the glass breaks",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"A glass bottle can act like a lens: it focuses the sun's rays and can start a fire. Do not leave glass bottles outside."},{question:"In summer, how much water should we drink?",options:[{text:"Half a liter is enough",correct:!1},{text:"1 liter is enough",correct:!1},{text:"2 liters or more, throughout the day",correct:!0},{text:"Only when we feel thirsty",correct:!1}],explanation:"Drink water throughout the day, usually 2 liters or more, and before you feel thirsty. This helps protect you from heatstroke."},{question:"In summer, at what time of day are the heat and the danger greatest?",options:[{text:"Early morning",correct:!1},{text:"Midday and afternoon",correct:!0},{text:"Evening",correct:!1},{text:"Night",correct:!1}],explanation:"The heat is at its highest at midday and in the afternoon, so stay out of the sun at those hours."}]},{id:3,icon:"Home",accent:"#059669",title:"Home safety",subtitle:"Protecting the family from dangers inside the home",summary:"The home should be a safe place for the family, but there are many dangers in the home, such as fire, electricity, gas, falls and poisoning. Let us learn how to make a home safe.",blocks:[{heading:"Fire safety and fire extinguishers",body:`Install a smoke alarm and test it every month. Keep a fire extinguisher near the kitchen. Types of fire extinguisher (NFPA classes):
• Water: for wood, paper and fabric (Class A).
• CO₂: for electrical equipment (Class C).
• ABC powder: for Classes A, B and C, but not for cooking oil and not for metals.
• Foam: for flammable liquids (Class B).
• Never pour water on burning oil!

Use an extinguisher only if all of this is true: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door. The PASS method: Pull the safety pin, Aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle firmly and Sweep the nozzle from side to side. Plan an emergency escape route from your home.`},{heading:"Electrical safety and generators",body:`Keep outlets and electrical cables in good condition. Repair or replace cut or damaged cables. Do not touch electrical appliances with wet hands. Do not connect many appliances to one power strip.

Generators: run a generator only outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from windows, doors and vents — never inside a house, garage, basement, shop or storeroom, even with the doors and windows open. Fit a battery-powered CO alarm in the home. Never plug a generator into a wall outlet or connect it to the house wiring: it can electrocute neighbors and electricity workers. Only a qualified electrician may connect it, through a transfer switch.`},{heading:"Gas safety",body:"Check gas pipes and cylinders regularly. If you smell gas: do not switch any electrical switch on or off, do not light any flame, do not use a phone inside and do not start a generator or a car nearby. Open the windows and doors on your way out if you can. Close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely, without going into the area where the gas has collected; if not, do not go near it. Leave the house, warn others and call 115 from outside."},{heading:"Preventing falls",body:"Fit handrails on stairs. Do not leave things on the floor or on the stairs. Make sure stairs are well lit. Clean up spilled water from the floor immediately."}],tips:["Test the smoke alarm every month.","Do not connect many appliances to power strips.","Keep medicines and chemicals in a locked place, away from children.","Always keep the emergency exit route clear.","Write the emergency numbers (115, 122, 104) in a visible place."],warnings:["Do not leave children alone in the kitchen.","Keep glass and sharp objects in a high place, away from children.","Make windows and balconies safe for children.","Playing with fire or electricity can kill."],activity:{prompt:"There is a smell of gas in the kitchen. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I open the windows on my way out, close the gas valve only if I can reach it safely, leave the house and call 115 from outside",correct:!0,feedback:"No switch or flame, windows opened on the way out, the valve closed only if safe, and 115 called from outside."},{text:"I switch on the light to find where the gas is leaking",correct:!1,feedback:"Switching on a light can ignite the gas; touch no switch and get out of the house."},{text:"I switch on my phone's flashlight inside the house",correct:!1,feedback:"Using a phone inside the house can ignite the gas; get out first and call 115 from outside."},{text:"I wait until the smell of gas goes away",correct:!1,feedback:"The gas keeps building up while you wait; get out, warn others and call 115 from outside."}]},quiz:[{question:"How often should we test the smoke alarm?",options:[{text:"Once every 5 years",correct:!1},{text:"Every month",correct:!0},{text:"Never",correct:!1},{text:"Only when we buy it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Test the smoke alarm every month, so you can be sure it will warn you if a fire starts."},{question:"When there is a smell of gas in the house, what do we do?",options:[{text:"We switch on the electric lights",correct:!1},{text:"We open the windows on our way out, close the gas valve only if it is safe to reach, and leave",correct:!0},{text:"We light a cigarette",correct:!1},{text:"We wait until the smell goes away",correct:!1}],explanation:"Any switch or flame can ignite the gas. Open windows on your way out, close the valve only if it is close and safe to reach, and call 115 from outside."},{question:"Why should we not touch electricity with wet hands?",options:[{text:"Because our hands get dirty",correct:!1},{text:"Because water conducts electricity and causes an electric shock",correct:!0},{text:"Because the electricity gets cut off",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water conducts electricity, so touching electrical appliances with wet hands can give you an electric shock."}]},{id:4,icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#dc2626",title:"First aid basics",subtitle:"Basic actions to save a life until the ambulance arrives",summary:"First aid can save a life. If someone is injured, their heart has stopped or they cannot breathe, you can help them until the ambulance arrives. The first thing to do: call 122.",blocks:[{heading:"Chest compressions (CPR) for adults",body:`When a person's heart stops (they become unconscious and are not breathing, or not breathing normally):
1. Make sure the area is safe for you.
2. Tap their shoulders and ask in a loud voice: "Are you all right?"
3. If there is no response, call 122 and send someone to bring an automated external defibrillator (AED).
4. Give 30 compressions in the center of the chest: depth 5–6 cm, rate 100–120 compressions per minute.
5. If you are trained, give 2 rescue breaths (tilt the head back, lift the chin and pinch the nose). If you are not trained, or not willing to give breaths, give chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR), without stopping.
6. Repeat 30:2 (or compressions only) until the ambulance team arrives.

• AED: if one is available, switch it on immediately and follow the device's voice instructions.`},{heading:"Bleeding: how to stop it",body:`• Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press firmly. Do not lift your hand until the bleeding stops.
• If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another cloth on top and keep pressing.
• Tourniquet: for very severe bleeding from an arm or leg, apply it 5–7 cm above the wound (not over the knee or elbow) and tighten it until the bleeding stops. Write down the time it was applied and never loosen it.
• If an object is stuck in the wound, do not pull it out! Apply pressure around it and call 122.`},{heading:"Burns: first aid",body:`• Minor burn: immediately cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes (not ice and not very cold water).
• Do not burst blisters, and do not put butter, oil or toothpaste on the burn.
• After cooling, cover it loosely with a clean cloth or plastic wrap.
• Severe or large burn: call 122. If clothing is stuck to the skin, do not pull it off. Keep the person warm.`},{heading:"Choking",body:`Signs: the person cannot speak, cough or breathe, and holds their throat with their hands.
• Adults and children (one year and older): give 5 blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. Then give 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich): stand behind the person, place your fist slightly above the navel and well below the breastbone, and press quickly inward and upward. Repeat 5+5.
• Infants (under one year): lay the infant along your forearm, face down with the head lower than the chest, and give 5 back blows. Then turn the infant onto their back and give 5 chest thrusts in the center of the chest with the heel of one hand. Never give abdominal thrusts to an infant.
• If the person becomes unconscious, call 122 and start CPR.`}],tips:["Always call 122 as the first step.","Use gloves if they are available.","Keep a first aid kit at home and in the car.","Take a first aid course.","Time matters: start helping quickly."],warnings:["Before anything else, check your own safety.","If someone has fallen from a height, do not move them (their neck or back may be injured), unless the place is unsafe or they need CPR.","Do not put anything in the mouth of an unconscious person.","Do not touch a wound with dirty hands."],activity:{prompt:"Someone at home is choking on something and cannot speak or breathe. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I give 5 blows to their back, then 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich) and repeat",correct:!0,feedback:"Unable to speak or breathe means full choking; 5 back blows, then 5 abdominal thrusts, repeated, can push the object out."},{text:"I give them water to drink",correct:!1,feedback:"Drinking does not treat choking: give 5 back blows, then 5 abdominal thrusts, and repeat."},{text:"I push my fingers deep into their mouth",correct:!1,feedback:"This is not what the lesson teaches: give 5 back blows, then 5 abdominal thrusts, and repeat."},{text:"I wait until they feel better",correct:!1,feedback:"A person who cannot breathe cannot wait: start back blows and abdominal thrusts at once."}]},quiz:[{question:"In adult CPR, what is the ratio of compressions to breaths?",options:[{text:"10 compressions and 1 breath",correct:!1},{text:"30 compressions and 2 breaths",correct:!0},{text:"15 compressions and 2 breaths",correct:!1},{text:"50 compressions and 5 breaths",correct:!1}],explanation:"Adult CPR is 30 chest compressions, then 2 breaths if you are trained. If you are not trained, give compressions only (Hands-Only CPR), without stopping."},{question:"What do we do for a minor burn?",options:[{text:"We put ice on it",correct:!1},{text:"We put butter on it",correct:!1},{text:"Cool running water for 20 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"We put kerosene on it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cool a minor burn under cool running water for 20 minutes. Do not put ice, butter, oil or any other substance on it."},{question:"What is the ambulance number?",options:[{text:"115",correct:!1},{text:"122",correct:!0},{text:"104",correct:!1},{text:"911",correct:!1}],explanation:"In the Kurdistan Region, 122 is the ambulance number; 115 is Civil Defense and 104 is the police."}]},{id:5,icon:"Backpack",accent:"#9333ea",title:"The emergency kit",subtitle:"Preparing a bag for emergencies",summary:"An emergency kit is a bag that holds the things you need in an emergency, such as an earthquake, a flood or any other incident. Every household should have one.",blocks:[{heading:"Water and food",body:`• Water: about 4 liters per person per day, for at least 3 days (for drinking and hygiene).
• Canned food or dry food that does not need cooking.
• Crackers, dates and candy.`},{heading:"Medical supplies",body:"Bandages, clean wound dressings, medical tape, gloves, scissors, tweezers and any medicines that family members need (if any)."},{heading:"Important equipment",body:"A flashlight and batteries, a small radio, a power bank to charge your phone, a change of clothes, a blanket and a whistle to call for help."},{heading:"Documents and money",body:"Copies of identity cards and passports, some cash, a list of important phone numbers and spare keys."}],tips:["Choose a light bag that is easy to carry.","Check the contents of the kit once every 6 months and replace any water or food that has expired.","Keep the kit in an easy-to-reach place near the door.","Include items for the particular needs of each family member (children, the elderly, the sick).","Do not forget your pets."],warnings:["Make sure there is enough water and food for every family member.","Do not forget your medicines.","Include a phone and a charger.","Cash is important, because bank cards may not work."],activity:{prompt:"After an earthquake, you must evacuate your home quickly. The emergency kit is ready at home. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I take the kit and go to the assembly point",correct:!0,feedback:"Taking the ready kit to the assembly point gives you water, food and medicines without losing time."},{text:"I spend time looking around to collect other things",correct:!1,feedback:"Searching for other things wastes time in a home that may be damaged; take the ready kit and leave."},{text:"I leave the kit behind and just run",correct:!1,feedback:"Leaving the kit means losing the water, food and medicines you prepared; it is ready, so take it with you."},{text:"I stay at home and wait",correct:!1,feedback:"You must evacuate: a home that may be damaged is dangerous, so take the kit and go to the assembly point."}]},quiz:[{question:"How much water should the emergency kit hold for each person?",options:[{text:"1 liter is enough",correct:!1},{text:"About 4 liters per day, for at least 3 days",correct:!0},{text:"Half a liter",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"Plan about 4 liters of water per person per day, for at least 3 days, for drinking and hygiene."},{question:"How often should we check the contents of the kit?",options:[{text:"Once every 10 years",correct:!1},{text:"Once every 6 months",correct:!0},{text:"Only when we buy it",correct:!1},{text:"It never needs checking",correct:!1}],explanation:"Check the kit once every 6 months and replace any water or food that has expired."},{question:"Which of these items is NOT needed in an emergency kit?",options:[{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"A flashlight",correct:!1},{text:"A television",correct:!0},{text:"Medicines",correct:!1}],explanation:"A television is not needed. The kit holds what you need in an emergency: water, food, medicines, a flashlight and a small radio."}]},{id:6,icon:"PhoneCall",accent:"#16a34a",title:"Emergency numbers",subtitle:"Knowing the numbers and how to call",summary:"Knowing the emergency numbers can save a life. Everyone should memorize these numbers and know when and how to call.",blocks:[{heading:"The main numbers in the Kurdistan Region",body:`• 115: Civil Defense (fire, rescue, building collapse, floods, gas leaks and hazardous materials).
• 122: Ambulance (medical emergencies).
• 104: Police.`},{heading:"How to make the call",body:"Stay calm and speak clearly. Say the exact location first: the area, the street, a nearby landmark and the floor. Then say what has happened, how many people are hurt or trapped, and any danger (fire spreading, gas, electricity, chemicals). Give your name and your phone number. Answer the questions, follow their instructions and do not hang up until they tell you to."},{heading:"Which number to call, and when",body:`• Fire, smoke, explosion, collapse, flood or a trapped person: 115.
• Injury, serious illness or an unconscious person: 122.
• Theft, threats and security incidents: 104.
• If there is a fire or incident and also injured people: 115 and 122.`},{heading:"The exact address",body:"Give the name of the neighborhood and the street, visible landmarks nearby (a mosque, a school, a shop), and the house number and its color."}],tips:["Save the numbers in your phone.","Put a list of the numbers in a visible place at home.","Teach the numbers to children.","Teach children the home address.","Memorize your home address exactly."],warnings:["Never make false or prank calls: they keep help away from people who need it.","Do not keep the emergency line busy without a reason: other people's lives are at risk.","Stay calm and speak clearly.","The exact address is very important."],activity:{prompt:"You saw a car hit a person on the street, and the person was injured. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I call 122, give the address and report the injured person's condition",correct:!0,feedback:"An injured person needs an ambulance: calling 122 with the exact address and the person's condition brings help quickly."},{text:"I continue on my way",correct:!1,feedback:"Walking away leaves the injured person without help; call 122 and give the exact address."},{text:"I record videos",correct:!1,feedback:"Filming does not help the injured person and does not respect them; call 122 first and give the exact address."},{text:"I just watch",correct:!1,feedback:"Just watching does not help and wastes time; call 122 at once and give the exact address."}]},quiz:[{question:"What is the Civil Defense number (fire and rescue)?",options:[{text:"122",correct:!1},{text:"104",correct:!1},{text:"115",correct:!0},{text:"911",correct:!1}],explanation:"115 is Civil Defense: fire, rescue, building collapse, floods, gas leaks and hazardous materials. 122 is the ambulance and 104 is the police."},{question:"When you call, what information should you give?",options:[{text:"Only your name",correct:!1},{text:"The exact location first, then what has happened, how many people are hurt or trapped, any danger, and your name and phone number",correct:!0},{text:"Only the address",correct:!1},{text:"No information is needed",correct:!1}],explanation:"Say the exact location first so that help can find you; then what has happened, how many people are hurt or trapped, any danger, and your name and phone number."},{question:"What is the ambulance number?",options:[{text:"115",correct:!1},{text:"122",correct:!0},{text:"104",correct:!1},{text:"100",correct:!1}],explanation:"122 is the ambulance number, for injuries, serious illness and unconscious people. 115 is Civil Defense and 104 is the police."}]},{id:7,icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire extinguishers: complete training",subtitle:"Types of extinguisher, fire classes and the PASS method",summary:"A fire extinguisher is a key piece of equipment for putting out small fires in their early stages. Every adult, at home and at work, should know how to use one quickly and correctly, because portable extinguishers only work for a few seconds. If the fire becomes large, leave the extinguisher, get out and call 115.",blocks:[{heading:"Fire classes (NFPA)",body:`• Class A: ordinary solid materials (wood, paper, fabric).
• Class B: flammable liquids and gases (gasoline, kerosene, gas). Water spreads a burning liquid.
• Class C: live electrical equipment. Do not use water or foam.
• Class D: combustible metals (such as magnesium). Water causes a violent reaction and an explosion.
• Class K: cooking oils and fats. Water spreads hot oil into a large fire.`},{heading:"Types of fire extinguisher",body:`Classes are given in NFPA letters:
• Water: Class A only.
• CO₂: Class B and live electrical equipment (NFPA B and C; under EN 2, C means gas and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires). It has a short range and is not for deep-seated wood or paper fires. Hold only the handle and do not touch the nozzle: it becomes extremely cold and burns the skin. In a small room, leave and ventilate after use, because CO₂ pushes out the oxygen.
• ABC powder: for A, B and C. Not for Class D and not for Class K.
• Foam: for A and B. Do not use it on electrical equipment.
• Clean agent: for sensitive equipment such as computers; its rating depends on the model, so read the label.
• Wet chemical: for cooking oil (Class K).
• Class D: only an extinguisher or agent whose label lists the burning metal.`},{heading:"The PASS method",body:`Before you use an extinguisher (every answer must be yes):
• The fire is small and just starting, like a fire in a wastebasket or a pan.
• The extinguisher is the right type for this fire.
• Your exit is behind you and the fire is not between you and the exit.
• The alarm has been raised: others are warned and 115 has been called.
• You are trained to use the extinguisher.
• No smoke or heat is blocking your way out.
• If the fire does not go out at once, stop, get out and close the door behind you.

1. P = Pull: pull out the safety pin.
2. A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top of the flames.
3. S = Squeeze: squeeze the extinguisher handle firmly.
4. S = Sweep: sweep the nozzle from side to side until the fire is out.

PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions.`},{heading:"Checking the extinguisher",body:`Visual check every month, by the person in charge:
• The extinguisher is in its place, visible and easy to reach.
• The safety pin and its seal are in place.
• The label is readable and faces out.
• The hose is not cracked or punctured, and the nozzle is clear.
• The body of the extinguisher is not rusted, damaged or leaking.
• If there is a pressure gauge, its needle is in the green zone. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full.

Service by a qualified technician:
• Inspection and maintenance: once a year.
• Recharge: after any use, even a short one.
• 6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years.
• Hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder): every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells.

A test date is not a replacement date. An extinguisher is taken out of service if it fails an inspection or test, is damaged, obsolete or recalled, has no readable label or is a non-rechargeable unit past the service life on its label. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed.`},{heading:"Particular risks at home and in the community",body:`• Kitchen: cooking oil is Class K. Use a wet chemical extinguisher or a large lid.
• Electricity: outlets and cables are Class C.
• Gas heaters and cylinders: gas is Class B. The priority is to stop the gas: a qualified person closes the gas valve only if it is safe to reach. Do not put out a gas flame if the gas cannot be stopped safely: get everyone out and call 115.
• Gasoline and kerosene: store them away from the house in a well-ventilated place.
• An ABC extinguisher is good for most home fires, but not for cooking oil (K) and not for metals (D).`},{heading:"Extinguisher colors (BS EN 3 and BS 7863)",body:`Color is not the fire class. Under the European standard BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent:
• Cream = foam.
• Blue = dry powder.
• Black = CO₂.
• Yellow = wet chemical.
• Water has no band.

Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols.`},{heading:"Class F (cooking oil): the same as Class K",body:`Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. So Class F (EN 2) and Class K (NFPA) are the same class: fires in cooking oils and fats.
• Very hot oil can catch fire by itself.
• A wet chemical extinguisher is the right choice: it forms a soap-like layer on the oil that cuts off the oxygen and cools the oil.
• Restaurants and large kitchens need a Class F/K extinguisher.`}],tips:["Every home should have at least one ABC extinguisher.","Place the extinguisher near the kitchen door, not inside the kitchen next to the stove.","Teach all adult family members how to use the extinguisher.","Check the extinguisher every month; if it has a pressure gauge, the needle must be in the green zone (CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full).","Do not pull the safety pin until you need to use the extinguisher.","Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.","Keep your back toward the exit door, so that your escape route always stays open.","Once a year, have the extinguisher serviced by a qualified person (Service).","A portable extinguisher only works for a few seconds: be ready before you start."],warnings:["Never pour water on burning oil: a large fire will spread quickly!","Never use water on electrical equipment: risk of electric shock!","Do not use the extinguisher if the fire is bigger than a wastebasket, is spreading or smoke is building up: get out and call 115!","Always aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top!","CO₂ extinguisher: do not touch the nozzle! It becomes as cold as about 78 degrees below zero and burns the skin.","ABC powder in a closed room reduces visibility and makes breathing difficult: after using it, leave the room and open the doors and windows."],activity:{prompt:"In the kitchen at home, the oil in a frying pan on the stove has caught fire! What will you do?",options:[{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Water turns to steam in hot oil and spreads the burning oil into a large fire; never pour water on it."},{text:"If it is safe, I turn off the stove and put a large lid on the pan to cut off the oxygen, or I use a wet chemical extinguisher",correct:!0,feedback:"Turning off the stove and covering the pan cuts off the oxygen; a wet chemical extinguisher is made for cooking oil."},{text:"I pick up the pan and carry it outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not pick up a burning pan; turn off the stove if it is safe and put a large lid on it."},{text:"I beat it with a cloth to put it out",correct:!1,feedback:"This is not what the lesson teaches: cover the pan with a large lid or use a wet chemical extinguisher."}]},quiz:[{question:"In NFPA letters, which fire classes is a CO₂ extinguisher rated for?",options:[{text:"A and D",correct:!1},{text:"B and C",correct:!0},{text:"A and K",correct:!1},{text:"D and K",correct:!1}],explanation:"In NFPA letters, CO₂ is rated for Class B (flammable liquids) and Class C (live electrical equipment); it is not for deep-seated wood or paper fires, metals (D) or cooking oil (K)."},{question:"In the PASS method, where should the nozzle be aimed?",options:[{text:"At the top of the fire",correct:!1},{text:"At the base of the fire",correct:!0},{text:"Around the fire",correct:!1},{text:"At the ceiling of the room",correct:!1}],explanation:"In PASS, aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top of the flames, and sweep from side to side until the fire is out."},{question:"Why is water not used on an oil fire (Class K)?",options:[{text:"Because it has no effect",correct:!1},{text:"Because the water instantly turns to steam and spreads the hot oil into a large fire",correct:!0},{text:"Because the oil absorbs the water",correct:!1},{text:"Because water itself burns",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water instantly turns to steam inside hot oil and throws the burning oil out into a large fire. Use a large lid or a wet chemical extinguisher instead."},{question:"Which extinguisher is suitable for most home fires (Classes A, B and C)?",options:[{text:"Water extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"CO₂ extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"ABC powder extinguisher",correct:!0},{text:"Foam extinguisher",correct:!1}],explanation:"ABC powder covers Classes A, B and C, so it suits most home fires. It is not for cooking oil (K) or metals (D)."},{question:"In the UK color code (BS EN 3 with BS 7863), what color is the band on a wet chemical extinguisher (for cooking oil)?",options:[{text:"Red",correct:!1},{text:"Black",correct:!1},{text:"Yellow",correct:!0},{text:"Blue",correct:!1}],explanation:"In the UK code, a yellow band marks wet chemical. Color is never the fire class, and the practice in Duhok is not confirmed, so always read the label."},{question:"Under NFPA 10, how often should a qualified person carry out maintenance on an extinguisher?",options:[{text:"Every week",correct:!1},{text:"Once a year, with a visual check every month",correct:!0},{text:"Once every 20 years",correct:!1},{text:"It does not need checking",correct:!1}],explanation:"The person in charge does a visual check every month; a qualified technician does inspection and maintenance once a year and recharges the extinguisher after any use."}]},{id:8,icon:"Heart",accent:"#db2777",title:"First aid and CPR",subtitle:"Complete training in first aid and cardiopulmonary resuscitation",summary:"First aid is the initial help given to an injured or sick person before the medical team arrives. Everyone in the community should know how to perform CPR, stop bleeding and protect an injured person until the ambulance arrives. This lesson can help protect your life and the lives of your family.",blocks:[{heading:"CPR: reviving the heart and lungs",body:`1. Check that the area is safe.
2. Tap the casualty's shoulders and ask: "Are you all right?"
3. If they do not respond and are not breathing normally, call 122 and ask for an automated external defibrillator (AED).
4. Give 30 compressions in the center of the chest: 100–120 compressions per minute, depth 5–6 cm for all adults.
5. If you are trained and willing, give 2 breaths, each lasting one second. If you are not trained, or not willing to give breaths, give chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR), without stopping.
6. Continue 30:2 (or compressions only) until the ambulance team arrives.
7. If an AED is available, use it immediately.`},{heading:"Stopping bleeding",body:`• Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press firmly.
• If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another cloth on top and keep pressing.
• Tourniquet: for very severe bleeding from an arm or leg, apply it 5–7 cm above the wound, write down the time it was applied and never loosen it.
• Do not pull out an object that is stuck in a wound!`},{heading:"Treating burns",body:`• Heat burns: cool running water for 20 minutes.
• Chemical burns: rinse with plenty of water for at least 20 minutes.
• Electrical burns: first cut off the electricity! Then call 122.
• Do not burst blisters, and do not use butter, oil or toothpaste.`},{heading:"Fractures and falls",body:`• Do not move the injured limb: keep it still with a stick or a board (splint).
• Do not move a person who has fallen from a height: their neck or back may be injured. Keep them still and call 122. Move them only if the place is unsafe (fire, traffic, collapse, gas, rising water), keeping the head, neck and back in line. If they need CPR, lay them on their back on a firm surface. Do not put them in the recovery position.
• Wrap ice in a cloth and place it on the swelling.`},{heading:"Electric shock",body:`• Cut off the electricity immediately! Do not touch the casualty until the power has been cut off.
• If you cannot cut off the power (household electricity only), use a dry piece of wood to move the cable away from the casualty.
• Call 122; anyone who has had an electric shock must be examined by a doctor.`},{heading:"Choking: the Heimlich maneuver",body:`If a person cannot speak, cough or breathe and is holding their throat with their hands, they are choking on something.
• Give 5 blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades.
• Give 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich): stand behind the person, place your fist slightly above the navel and well below the breastbone, grasp it with your other hand and press quickly inward and upward.
• Continue 5+5 until the object comes out or the person becomes unconscious; if they become unconscious, call 122 and start CPR.
• Infants (under one year): 5 back blows (head lower than the chest) and 5 chest thrusts. Never give abdominal thrusts to an infant.`},{heading:"The recovery position",body:`If a person is unconscious but breathing normally, and no neck or back injury is suspected, place them in the recovery position:
1. Place their arm nearest to you at a right angle, pointing toward their head.
2. Bring their far arm across their chest and place the back of that hand under their cheek.
3. Raise the knee of their far leg.
4. Holding the knee and the shoulder, roll them toward you onto their side.
5. Tilt their head back slightly so that the airway stays open.

• Call 122 and watch their breathing until the ambulance arrives.`}],tips:["Every home should have a first aid kit.","Keep the list of emergency numbers in a visible place.","Teach the whole family CPR and the Heimlich maneuver.","Keep clean cloths, a tourniquet and scissors in the kit.","122 is always available: do not be afraid to call.","The brain is quickly damaged without oxygen: start CPR quickly.","For all adults, including the elderly, the compression depth is 5–6 centimeters: do not reduce it.","For a pregnant woman or a very overweight person who is choking, give thrusts in the center of the chest, not on the abdomen."],warnings:["Do not touch the casualty if the electricity has not been cut off!","Do not pull out an object that is stuck in a wound!","Do not burst burn blisters!","Do not move a person who has fallen from a height, unless the place is unsafe or they need CPR!","Do not use abdominal thrusts (Heimlich) on infants under one year old!","Continue CPR until the ambulance team arrives: do not stop."],activity:{prompt:"A friend's hand caught fire and they have a large burn. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I put butter or oil on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Never put butter or oil on a burn; cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122."},{text:"I cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Cool running water for 20 minutes cools the burn, and a large burn needs an ambulance (122)."},{text:"I burst the blisters",correct:!1,feedback:"Never burst burn blisters; cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122."},{text:"I do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"A large burn needs help at once: cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122."}]},quiz:[{question:"In CPR, how many chest compressions should be given per minute?",options:[{text:"50–70",correct:!1},{text:"80–90",correct:!1},{text:"100–120",correct:!0},{text:"150 or more",correct:!1}],explanation:"Push hard and fast in the center of the chest: 100–120 compressions per minute, 5–6 cm deep for all adults."},{question:"If a sharp object is stuck in a wound, what will you do?",options:[{text:"I pull it out",correct:!1},{text:"I leave it in place and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"I leave the person and go on my way",correct:!1},{text:"I wash it with water and pull it out",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not pull out an object stuck in a wound: leave it in place, press around it and call 122."},{question:"If someone gets an electric shock, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"I take hold of their hand",correct:!1},{text:"I start CPR immediately",correct:!1},{text:"I cut off the electricity",correct:!0},{text:"I pour water on them",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cut off the electricity first: until the power is off, touching the casualty can give you a shock too. Then call 122."},{question:"What is the main sign of complete choking?",options:[{text:"A loud noise from the throat",correct:!1},{text:"Being unable to speak, cough or breathe, with the hands at the throat",correct:!0},{text:"A mild cough",correct:!1},{text:"A headache",correct:!1}],explanation:"In complete choking, the person cannot speak, cough or breathe and holds their throat. Give 5 back blows, then 5 abdominal thrusts."},{question:"Who is the recovery position used for?",options:[{text:"An unconscious person who is still breathing normally",correct:!0},{text:"An unconscious person who is not breathing",correct:!1},{text:"A conscious person with a minor wound",correct:!1},{text:"A healthy person with no problems",correct:!1}],explanation:"The recovery position is for an unconscious person who is breathing normally, with no suspected neck or back injury. A person who is not breathing needs CPR."}]},{id:9,icon:"CarFront",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Road safety",subtitle:"Protecting yourself and your family on the roads",summary:"Road traffic accidents are one of the biggest causes of death and injury. Learning the traffic laws and following them can protect your life. This lesson covers the safety of pedestrians, drivers and children.",blocks:[{heading:"Pedestrian safety",body:"Always walk on the sidewalk. Before crossing the road, look both ways and cross at a pedestrian crossing. Do not look at your phone while crossing. At night, wear light-colored clothes or clothes with reflective markings."},{heading:"Driving safety",body:"Always wear your seat belt. Do not use your phone while driving. Keep to a suitable, legal speed. In winter, use suitable tires."},{heading:"Children in the car",body:"Always put children in a child car seat that suits their age and weight. Children aged 12 and under should sit in the back seats. Never leave children alone in the car."},{heading:"Motorcycles and bicycles",body:"Always wear a protective helmet. At night, lights and reflective markings are essential. Wear gloves and suitable footwear."}],tips:["Always wear your seat belt.","Do not use your phone while driving.","Children should sit in the back seats.","Wear a protective helmet on motorcycles and bicycles."],warnings:["Phone + driving = danger of death.","Speed + snow or ice = accident."],activity:{prompt:"You are driving and your phone rings. An important message has arrived. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I keep driving and read the message",correct:!1,feedback:"Phone plus driving means danger of death; stop in a safe place first, then read the message."},{text:"I stop in a safe place at the side of the road and then reply",correct:!0,feedback:"Stopping in a safe place first means you can reply without putting anyone on the road in danger."},{text:"I use the phone with one hand",correct:!1,feedback:"Using the phone with one hand is still using it while driving; stop in a safe place first."},{text:"I type a quick reply while driving",correct:!1,feedback:"Typing while driving is one of the most dangerous things you can do; stop in a safe place, then reply."}]},quiz:[{question:"Up to what age should children sit in the back seats of a car?",options:[{text:"12 years",correct:!0},{text:"6 years",correct:!1},{text:"18 years",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"Children aged 12 and under should sit in the back seats, in a child car seat that suits their age and weight."},{question:"Which of these is one of the most dangerous things to do while driving?",options:[{text:"Using a phone",correct:!0},{text:"Wearing a seat belt",correct:!1},{text:"Driving at the legal speed",correct:!1},{text:"Stopping at a red traffic light",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not use a phone while driving: phone plus driving means danger of death. A seat belt and the legal speed help keep you safe."},{question:"May children be left alone in a parked car?",options:[{text:"No, not even for a short time",correct:!0},{text:"Yes, if the windows are open a little",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, for a few minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if the car is parked in the shade",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never leave children alone in the car, not even for a short time. Always seat them in a child car seat that suits their age and weight."}]},{id:10,icon:"LifeBuoy",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Water safety and drowning prevention",subtitle:"Protecting yourself and children near water",summary:"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), drowning is one of the leading causes of death among children and young people worldwide. In the Kurdistan Region, lakes, dams and rivers are very dangerous in summer. Every family needs to learn about water safety.",blocks:[{heading:"Children and water",body:"Never leave children alone near water: a small child can drown in very shallow water (a few centimeters, such as in a bucket or a basin). Near pools and water, always keep your eyes on them."},{heading:"Swimming safely",body:"Swim only in official places where there is supervision. Always have someone else with you. Before going into the water, find out how deep it is. Wear a life jacket at lakes and rivers."},{heading:"Rescue",body:`If someone is drowning:
1. Do not go into the water yourself!
2. Reach out or throw something to them (a rope, a stick, a life ring).
3. Call 115 and 122.
4. Once they are out of the water, check their breathing. If they are not breathing normally and you are trained, start CPR with breaths: drowning stops the breathing first, so breaths matter most. If you are not trained or cannot give breaths, give chest compressions only until help arrives.
5. Anyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor, even if they seem well: lung problems are common afterwards.`}],tips:["Do not leave children alone near water.","Wear a life jacket at lakes and rivers.","Do not go into the water to rescue someone: throw something to them.","Learn CPR."],warnings:["A few centimeters of water are enough to drown a small child.","The water in the region's rivers and dams can be very strong and cold, even if it looks calm on the surface."],activity:{prompt:"You and your family are near a lake. Your small child wants to go into the water. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I let them go: it is normal",correct:!1,feedback:"A small child can drown even in very shallow water; never let them go in alone."},{text:"I put a life jacket on them, go in with them and watch them closely",correct:!0,feedback:"A life jacket and an adult close by, watching all the time, keep a small child safer near water."},{text:"I tell them to stay at the edge and I move away",correct:!1,feedback:"Never leave children alone near water, even at the edge; stay close and keep your eyes on them."},{text:"I watch from a distance",correct:!1,feedback:"Watching from a distance is not enough: near water, stay close and always keep your eyes on them."}]},quiz:[{question:"If someone is drowning, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"I go into the water to rescue them",correct:!1},{text:"I throw something to them (a rope or a stick) and call 115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"I just shout",correct:!1},{text:"I do nothing",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not go into the water yourself. Reach out or throw something to them, such as a rope, a stick or a life ring, and call 115 and 122."},{question:"How much water is enough for a small child to drown?",options:[{text:"A few centimeters, as in a bucket",correct:!0},{text:"Only water deeper than the child is tall",correct:!1},{text:"Only deep lakes and dams",correct:!1},{text:"At least water up to the child's waist",correct:!1}],explanation:"A few centimeters of water are enough to drown a small child, for example in a bucket or a basin. Never leave children alone near water."},{question:"Someone pulled from the water is not breathing normally. You are trained in CPR. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Start CPR that includes breaths",correct:!0},{text:"Give chest compressions only",correct:!1},{text:"Wait for them to start breathing",correct:!1},{text:"Turn them over so the water drains out",correct:!1}],explanation:"If they are not breathing normally and you are trained, start CPR with breaths: drowning stops the breathing first, so breaths matter most. If you are not trained or cannot give breaths, give chest compressions only until help arrives."}]},{id:11,icon:"Activity",accent:"#d97706",title:"Earthquakes at home and in the community",subtitle:"Preparedness and response",summary:"Earthquakes come without warning. Family preparedness and knowing the correct actions during an earthquake save lives. Our area is not free from earthquake risk, so every family should be prepared.",blocks:[{heading:"During an earthquake",body:`1. Drop, Cover, Hold On: get under a sturdy table and hold on to its leg.
2. Stay away from windows, glass and heavy objects.
3. Do not run outside, do not stand in a doorway and do not use the elevator.
4. If you are outside: move away from buildings, trees and power lines.`},{heading:"The emergency kit",body:"Every family should prepare an emergency kit: water (about 4 liters per person per day, for at least 3 days), food that does not spoil, medicines, a battery-powered radio, a flashlight, blankets and important documents."},{heading:"After an earthquake",body:"If you smell gas or hear gas leaking: do not touch any switch or flame, open the windows on your way out, close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected and without making any spark, then get out and call 115 from outside. Do not go into damaged buildings. Expect aftershocks. If people are trapped under buildings or there are injured people, call 115 and 122."}],tips:["Drop, Cover, Hold On.","Always keep your emergency kit ready.","Shut off the gas only if you smell gas or hear a leak, and only if you can reach the valve without going into the gas and without making any spark.","Do not stay in damaged buildings."],warnings:["Do not run while the ground is shaking!","Do not go into damaged buildings!"],activity:{prompt:"A strong earthquake strikes. You are at home and the children are frightened. What will you do?",options:[{text:"We get under a sturdy table together with the children, cover our heads and hold on to the table leg",correct:!0,feedback:"Drop, Cover, Hold On under a sturdy table protects you and the children from falling objects until the shaking stops."},{text:"We run outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not run while the ground is shaking; drop, cover and hold on under a sturdy table."},{text:"We stand by the window",correct:!1,feedback:"Windows and glass can break during the shaking; stay away from them and take cover under a sturdy table."},{text:"We take the elevator down",correct:!1,feedback:"Never use the elevator in an earthquake; drop, cover and hold on under a sturdy table."}]},quiz:[{question:"During an earthquake at home, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Get under a sturdy table and cover your head",correct:!0},{text:"Run outside",correct:!1},{text:"Stand by the window",correct:!1},{text:"Use the elevator",correct:!1}],explanation:"During the shaking, drop, cover and hold on under a sturdy table. Do not run outside, stand by windows or use the elevator."},{question:"You are outside when an earthquake starts. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Move away from buildings, trees and power lines",correct:!0},{text:"Run into the nearest building and get under a table",correct:!1},{text:"Stand close to a tall wall for protection",correct:!1},{text:"Shelter under a big tree until it stops",correct:!1}],explanation:"If you are outside during an earthquake, move away from buildings, trees and power lines. Do not run while the ground is shaking."},{question:"After the earthquake, may you go back into a damaged building?",options:[{text:"No, do not go into damaged buildings",correct:!0},{text:"Yes, to fetch important documents and medicines",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, as soon as the shaking has stopped",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if it is your own home",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not go into damaged buildings, and expect aftershocks. If people are trapped or injured, call 115 and 122."}]},{id:12,icon:"Siren",accent:"#dc2626",title:"The work of Civil Defense",subtitle:"The duties of the 115 teams and how you can help them",summary:"Civil Defense is an important government agency that protects the lives of citizens and private and public property. In the Kurdistan Region, 115 is the Civil Defense number for fire, rescue and disasters, and its teams are ready day and night. Citizens should know how to call 115 and how to help the teams, not hinder them.",blocks:[{heading:"The main duties of Civil Defense",body:`• Firefighting: in homes, offices, factories, farmland and forests.
• Rescue: from vehicles in accidents, from under rubble and from water.
• Floods and disasters: evacuating people, pumping out water and helping those affected.
• Hazardous materials (HazMat): gas leaks and chemicals.
• Training and awareness: lessons in schools and government offices.
• Safety inspections: in markets, restaurants, halls and high-rise buildings.`},{heading:"Official structure",body:`• In the Kurdistan Region, Civil Defense comes under the Ministry of Interior.
• Each governorate has a Civil Defense Directorate, such as the Duhok Civil Defense Directorate, with stations in the city and the districts.
• Departments: firefighting, rescue, first aid, training and technical departments.
• In major disasters, Civil Defense works together with the ambulance service (122), the police (104) and other agencies.`},{heading:"Their equipment and vehicles",body:`• Fire engines: carry water, hoses and pumps.
• Ladder trucks: reach high floors.
• Rescue equipment: hydraulic tools for cutting through the metal of vehicles and freeing trapped people (Jaws of Life).
• Breathing apparatus (BA): gives the firefighter clean air inside smoke.
• Special clothing for protection from heat.
• Radios for communication between teams.`},{heading:"How to call 115",body:`1. Dial 115.
2. Stay calm and speak clearly.
3. Say: the exact address (neighborhood, street and a visible landmark nearby), what has happened (a fire? an accident? is someone trapped?), how many people are injured or trapped, any particular hazard (gas, chemicals, electricity), and your name and phone number so that they can call you back.
4. Do not hang up until they tell you to.
5. If it is safe, keep the road clear for the fire engines.
6. Send someone out to the street to show the team the way.`},{heading:"How the community helps 115",body:`• On the road: when you hear a siren, calmly move to the right and stop so that the road is clear.
• Do not make false calls: every false call takes a team away from a real emergency.
• Keep access routes clear: do not park cars in front of gates or in narrow alleys.
• Witnesses: if you saw the incident, stay and give your information to the team.
• Do not crowd around the team or take photos, so that you do not get in their way.
• If the team asks for help, help them.
• If you see that 115 is needed, do not delay: call.
• Stay calm and do not spread fear and confusion.`},{heading:"Working and volunteering with Civil Defense",body:`• Applications for employment are made through official Civil Defense announcements.
• Requirements for age, education, physical fitness and tests are set by the Directorate: for up-to-date information, contact the Duhok Civil Defense Directorate.
• New staff receive basic training in firefighting, rescue and first aid.
• Citizens can take part in awareness lessons and training and become helpers in their own neighborhood during emergencies.`}],tips:["Memorize 115, and make sure every family member does too.","Know your home address exactly (neighborhood, street, house number).","When you hear a siren, move to the right and stop, not in the middle of the road.","Use 115 only for emergencies, so that the line stays open for people who need it.","If you suspect a fire or another danger, call: it is better for the team to come and find no problem.","Display the house number and the neighborhood name on your door so that the team can reach you easily.","Write a home escape plan and practice it with your family."],warnings:["Do not make false calls to 115: they waste the teams' time and put lives at risk.","Do not crowd around 115 vehicles or take photos: you get in the way of their work.","Do not film or share videos of victims: respect them.","When you hear a siren, do not drive behind emergency vehicles, and keep the road clear for them."],activity:{prompt:"A car accident has happened in front of your house. 2 people are trapped inside the car and no one else is there. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I immediately call 115 and 122 and give the exact address. If there is no fire, I do not pull out the trapped casualties, so that their neck and back are not injured. I stay in a safe place and follow the instructions of 115.",correct:!0,feedback:"Calling 115 and 122 brings rescue and medical help, and not moving trapped casualties protects their neck and back."},{text:"I quickly try to pull out the trapped casualties",correct:!1,feedback:"Pulling trapped casualties out can injure their neck and back; unless there is a fire, call 115 and 122 and follow instructions."},{text:"I record a video and post it on social media",correct:!1,feedback:"Filming and posting victims does not help and does not respect them; call 115 and 122 first."},{text:"I go to find someone else to help and do not call",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not delay: you are the only one there, so call 115 and 122 yourself, immediately."}]},quiz:[{question:"In the Kurdistan Region, what is the Civil Defense emergency number?",options:[{text:"991",correct:!1},{text:"107",correct:!1},{text:"115",correct:!0},{text:"122",correct:!1}],explanation:"In the Kurdistan Region, 115 is the Civil Defense number for fire, rescue and disasters. 122 is the ambulance."},{question:"When you see a fire engine with its siren on, what do you do?",options:[{text:"I calmly move to the right and stop so that the road is clear",correct:!0},{text:"I drive quickly behind it",correct:!1},{text:"I block its way",correct:!1},{text:"I record videos",correct:!1}],explanation:"Moving calmly to the right and stopping keeps the road clear for the fire engine. Never drive behind it, block it or stop to film."},{question:"When you call 115, what is the most important thing to say first?",options:[{text:"Only your name",correct:!1},{text:"The address and what has happened",correct:!0},{text:'I say "Come quickly" and hang up',correct:!1},{text:"I ask when they will arrive",correct:!1}],explanation:"The team must know where to go and what has happened. Then give the number of people hurt, any hazards and your name and number, and do not hang up until told."},{question:"What does a BA (Breathing Apparatus) do?",options:[{text:"It is a fire extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"It is a breathing device that gives the firefighter clean air inside smoke",correct:!0},{text:"It is a radio",correct:!1},{text:"It is a siren",correct:!1}],explanation:"A breathing apparatus (BA) gives the firefighter clean air to breathe inside smoke."},{question:"If there is a fire and also injured people, which number do you call?",options:[{text:"Only 104",correct:!1},{text:"Only 122",correct:!1},{text:"115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"I do not call and I wait",correct:!1}],explanation:"A fire needs Civil Defense (115) and injured people need an ambulance (122), so call both."}]},{id:13,icon:"FlameKindling",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Types of fire",subtitle:"Not all fires are the same: each type needs its own method",summary:"Under the American system (NFPA 10), there are five classes of fire: A, B, C, D and K. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. Each class involves a different burning material and needs a suitable extinguisher. Choosing the wrong extinguisher can make the fire bigger. This lesson will teach you how to recognize the type of fire and what to use.",blocks:[{heading:"Class A: solid materials (wood, paper, fabric)",body:`Very common at home: paper, cardboard, curtains, fabric, plastic and wooden furniture. Extinguisher: water or ABC powder. Aim at the base of the fire, starting from the distance shown on the extinguisher label, with your exit behind you.
• If your clothes catch fire: stop, drop and roll.`},{heading:"Class B: flammable liquids and gases (gasoline, kerosene, alcohol)",body:`These liquids catch fire quickly, and their vapors can ignite from a distance. Do not use water: water spreads the burning liquid and makes the fire bigger. Extinguisher for liquids: foam, CO₂ or ABC powder. Spread the foam gently over the surface of the liquid so that it covers it.
• Alcohol, acetone and other polar solvents: CO₂, ABC powder or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam; ordinary foam breaks down on them.
• Gas fire (LPG, natural gas): the priority is to stop the gas. A qualified person closes the gas valve only if it is safe to reach. Do not put out a gas flame if the gas cannot be stopped safely: unburned gas can build up and explode. If it is not safe, get everyone out, raise the alarm and call 115. Foam is not the general choice for gas fires. Under EN 2, gas fires are Class C.
• BLEVE: if a fire heats a gas cylinder or a tank, it can burst in a large explosion. Move away from it and call 115.`},{heading:"Class C: electrical equipment (NFPA)",body:`Computers, televisions, refrigerators, outlets and electrical cables.
• Never use water! An electric shock can kill.
• First cut off the electricity (pull out the plug or switch off the main power supply to the house), then use CO₂, a clean agent or ABC powder.
• ABC powder damages electronic equipment; CO₂ is better for sensitive equipment.
• In the European system (EN 2), electrical fire is not a separate class.`},{heading:"Class D: combustible metals (magnesium, titanium, sodium, lithium metal)",body:`These metals burn at very high temperatures.
• Do not use water or CO₂ on burning metals: they cause a violent reaction and a bigger fire.
• Use only a Class D extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal; the agent differs from metal to metal (lithium, for example, needs an agent listed for lithium). Never use water, ordinary powder or foam. Dry sand only if the site fire plan or the metal's safety data sheet (SDS) names it and it is completely dry. Call 115.
• The lithium-ion (Li-ion) batteries in phones, laptops, power banks and electric bicycles contain no metallic lithium and are not Class D: if they catch fire, cool them with plenty of water and call 115. Class D is only for lithium-metal batteries.`},{heading:"Class K (NFPA) / F (EN 2): cooking oil",body:`A fire in hot oil in a frying pan or a pot on the stove.
• Never use water! Water turns to steam instantly inside hot oil and spreads the burning oil in an explosion.
• ABC powder is not suitable for this class.
• Use a wet chemical extinguisher, or turn off the stove and put a large lid or a fire blanket over the pan to cut off the oxygen.
• Do not pick up the pan or carry it outside.`},{heading:"Stages of fire development",body:`1. Incipient: the fire is small and there is little smoke. Only at this stage is it safe to use an extinguisher.
2. Growth: the heat increases and the fire spreads.
3. Fully developed: the whole room is on fire.
4. Decay: the fire dies down, but if air suddenly enters the room, there is a risk of an explosive ignition (backdraft).

• Flashover: when the hot smoke layer under the ceiling reaches about 500–600 degrees Celsius, everything in the room catches fire at the same time.`}],tips:["Before buying, read the fire classes written on the extinguisher label.","Keep at least one ABC extinguisher at home, and a wet chemical extinguisher or a fire blanket for the kitchen.","Under NFPA 10, in workplaces the travel distance to a Class A extinguisher must not exceed 23 meters.","According to NFPA, cooking is the leading cause of home fires: do not leave the kitchen while cooking.","Electrical equipment fires: never use water. The first step is to cut off the electricity.","Lithium-ion batteries: if one gets hot, swells or gives off smoke, move it away from flammable materials, move away from it and call 115.","Cooking oil: turn off the stove and put a large lid over the pan. This is the first step in putting out that kind of fire."],warnings:["Water on burning oil = steam explosion and a large fire.","Water on electrical equipment = fatal shock.","ABC powder on a computer = damage to the equipment.","Cool a burning lithium-ion battery with plenty of water and call 115: the fire can flare up again.","A fire bigger than a trash can = get out immediately, do not use an extinguisher."],activity:{prompt:"You are in the kitchen. The oil in a frying pan has caught fire and the stove is still on. Your family is in the bedroom. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I turn off the stove, put a large lid on the pan and warn my family; if the fire does not go out, we leave and call 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Turning off the stove and the lid cut off heat and oxygen, and warning your family lets everyone get out if needed."},{text:"I pour water on it to cool it down",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on burning oil causes a steam explosion and a large fire; never pour water on it."},{text:"I pick up the pan and carry it outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not pick up the pan or carry it outside; turn off the stove and put a large lid on it."},{text:"I run out alone and do not warn my family",correct:!1,feedback:"Your family is in danger too; warn them, and if the fire does not go out, leave together and call 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"A computer in your room has caught fire. Which method is correct?",options:[{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"I first cut off the electricity, then use CO₂, a clean agent or ABC powder",correct:!0},{text:"AFFF foam",correct:!1},{text:"Wet chemical",correct:!1}],explanation:"A computer is electrical equipment: cut off the electricity first, then use CO₂, a clean agent or ABC powder. Water can cause a fatal electric shock."},{question:"The oil in a frying pan on a gas stove has caught fire. What is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1},{text:"I close the gas valve and put a large lid on the pan",correct:!0},{text:"I pour ABC powder on it",correct:!1},{text:"I beat it with a cloth",correct:!1}],explanation:"Turning off the gas stops the heat, and a large lid cuts off the oxygen. Water causes a steam explosion, and ABC powder is not suitable for cooking oil."},{question:"The lithium-ion battery of a phone has caught fire. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I cover it with a blanket and leave it in the room",correct:!1},{text:"I just pour ABC powder on it and leave",correct:!1},{text:"If it is safe, I move it away from flammable materials, cool it with plenty of water and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"I just spray CO₂ on it and leave it",correct:!1}],explanation:"If it is safe, move the battery away from flammable materials, cool it with plenty of water and call 115. ABC powder and CO₂ do not fully stop this fire."},{question:"Flashover happens when the hot smoke layer under the ceiling reaches about what temperature?",options:[{text:"200 degrees Celsius",correct:!1},{text:"300 degrees Celsius",correct:!1},{text:"500–600 degrees Celsius",correct:!0},{text:"1000 degrees Celsius",correct:!1}],explanation:"Flashover happens when the hot smoke layer under the ceiling reaches about 500–600°C: everything in the room catches fire at the same time."},{question:"According to NFPA, which activity is the leading cause of home fires?",options:[{text:"Reading books",correct:!1},{text:"Washing clothes by hand",correct:!1},{text:"Cooking (the kitchen)",correct:!0},{text:"Sleeping",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to NFPA, cooking is the leading cause of home fires, so do not leave the kitchen while cooking."}]},{id:14,icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#0891b2",title:"How to use a fire extinguisher",subtitle:"Types of extinguisher, the PASS method and when to leave the extinguisher and get out",summary:"Fire extinguishers are found in homes, offices and public places, but many people do not know how to use them. A portable extinguisher only works for a few seconds, so you must be prepared before a fire starts. This lesson teaches you the PASS method, how to choose the right extinguisher and, most important of all, when you should not use the extinguisher and should get out.",blocks:[{heading:"Main types of extinguisher",body:`Classes in NFPA letters:
• Water: Class A only.
• Foam: A and B.
• CO₂: Class B and live electrical equipment (NFPA B and C; under EN 2, C means gas and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires). Short range; not for deep-seated wood or paper fires; in a small room, leave and ventilate after use.
• ABC powder: A, B and C, the most common type; not for D and not for K.
• Wet chemical: K, cooking oil.
• Clean agent: for sensitive electronic equipment; its rating depends on the model, so read the label.

Color is not the fire class. Under BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent: cream = foam, blue = dry powder, black = CO₂, yellow = wet chemical; water has no band. Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols.

Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F.

For the home: an ABC extinguisher, plus a wet chemical extinguisher or a fire blanket for the kitchen.`},{heading:"PASS: step by step",body:`Before you start, every answer must be yes: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out.

1. P = Pull: pull out the safety pin.
2. A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top. Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.
3. S = Squeeze: squeeze the handle firmly and steadily.
4. S = Sweep: sweep slowly from side to side until the fire is out.

• If the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door.
• Small extinguishers empty quickly, often in a few seconds; the exact discharge time is on the label.
• PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions.`},{heading:"Where to stand: safety comes first",body:`Always keep your back toward the door (your exit route). Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller. If the heat becomes too great, move back. Never let the fire get between you and the door.
• Smoke and heat can kill quickly: if the room fills with smoke, get out immediately.`},{heading:"When not to use an extinguisher",body:`• The fire is bigger than a trash can or is spreading quickly.
• The room is filling with smoke.
• You do not know what material is burning.
• Your exit route is not behind you.
• No one has called 115 yet (call first).
• The fire is getting close to a gas cylinder or a liquid fuel tank.
• Flammable liquids have spread over a large area.

In all of these situations: first get out, then call 115.`},{heading:"Checking the extinguisher at home",body:`Every month (visual check, by the person in charge):
1. The extinguisher is in its place and visible, and the way to it is clear.
2. The safety pin and its seal are intact.
3. The body of the extinguisher is clean, free of rust, undamaged and not leaking; the hose and nozzle are clear.
4. The instruction label is easy to read and faces out.
5. If there is a pressure gauge, its needle is in the green zone. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full.

Service by a qualified technician:
• Inspection and maintenance: once a year, recorded on the extinguisher's tag.
• Recharge: after any use, even a short one.
• 6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years.
• Hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder): every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells.

A test date is not a replacement date. An extinguisher is taken out of service if it fails an inspection or test, is damaged, obsolete or recalled, has no readable label or is a non-rechargeable unit past the service life on its label. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed.`},{heading:"Where should extinguishers be placed?",body:`• At home (at least one): near the kitchen door and, if available, one on every floor.
• In workplaces (NFPA 10): the travel distance to an extinguisher must not exceed 23 meters for Class A, and 9 or 15 meters for Class B (depending on the size of the extinguisher).
• The extinguisher sign must be visible from a distance.
• Hang the extinguisher on the wall at a height that is easy to reach (its top no higher than about 1.5 meters), not on the floor and not inside closed cupboards.`}],tips:["When buying: an ABC extinguisher you can lift and use (the weight is on the label) for the home, and a wet chemical extinguisher or a fire blanket for the kitchen.","Before buying, check the label for the fire classes and the standard mark (such as EN 3).","Practice PASS in a training session with a training extinguisher.","Read on the label how many seconds your extinguisher works for: most small extinguishers empty quickly.","Take part in Civil Defense training when it is held in your neighborhood or workplace.","Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.","Always keep your back toward the door."],warnings:["A small extinguisher only works for a few seconds: be ready.","The nozzle of a CO₂ extinguisher becomes very cold: do not touch it.","ABC powder on a computer can damage the equipment.","A fire bigger than a trash can = get out, do not use an extinguisher.","Lithium-ion batteries: ABC powder and CO₂ do not fully stop the fire. Cool the battery with plenty of water and call 115."],activity:{prompt:"You are at home. In the bedroom, a phone charger plugged into an outlet has caught fire. The fire is small and you have 2 extinguishers within reach: a 2 kg ABC extinguisher and a wet chemical extinguisher. What will you do?",options:[{text:"First I cut off the electricity (I pull out the plug if it is safe, or I switch off the main power). I warn my family and call 115. Then, with my back toward the door, I take the ABC extinguisher and use PASS, starting from the distance shown on its label; if the fire does not go out at once, I get out and close the door.",correct:!0,feedback:"Power off, family warned, 115 called, exit behind you and the right extinguisher: every check is yes before PASS."},{text:"I use the wet chemical extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Wet chemical is for cooking oil (Class K); for this electrical fire, cut the power, then use the ABC extinguisher."},{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Never use water on electrical equipment: it can cause a fatal electric shock."},{text:"I run away and do not tell anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Leaving without warning anyone puts your family in danger; warn them and call 115, even if you do not fight the fire."}]},quiz:[{question:"In PASS, what does the letter P stand for?",options:[{text:"Push",correct:!1},{text:"Pull (pull out the safety pin)",correct:!0},{text:"Point",correct:!1},{text:"Press",correct:!1}],explanation:"P means Pull: pull out the safety pin. Then Aim at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle and Sweep from side to side."},{question:"Roughly how long does a small ABC powder extinguisher (2 kg) work for?",options:[{text:"3–5 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Only a few seconds; the exact time is on its label",correct:!0},{text:"1 minute",correct:!1},{text:"Without limit",correct:!1}],explanation:"A small extinguisher empties in only a few seconds, so be ready before you start. The exact discharge time is on its label."},{question:"How do you know how far from the fire to start?",options:[{text:"Always start half a meter away",correct:!1},{text:"From the extinguisher label: start at the distance shown there, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller",correct:!0},{text:"Always start 7–10 meters away",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"There is no fixed distance: start from the distance on the extinguisher label, keep your exit behind you and move closer only as the fire gets smaller."},{question:"When should you not use the extinguisher, and get out instead?",options:[{text:"Never: always use it",correct:!1},{text:"When the fire is bigger than a trash can or the room is filling with smoke",correct:!0},{text:"Only in the kitchen",correct:!1},{text:"Only in the bedroom",correct:!1}],explanation:"If the fire is bigger than a trash can, is spreading, or the room is filling with smoke, do not use the extinguisher: get out and call 115."},{question:"Which extinguisher is suitable for a cooking oil fire?",options:[{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"ABC powder",correct:!1},{text:"Wet chemical (Class K/F)",correct:!0},{text:"CO₂",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cooking oil fires are Class K (NFPA) or F (EN 2). A wet chemical extinguisher forms a layer that cuts off the oxygen and cools the oil; never use water."}]},{id:15,icon:"HeartHandshake",accent:"#e11d48",title:"First aid for citizens",subtitle:"Primary assessment, CPR, AED, bleeding, burns and choking",summary:"A person's life can be saved by an ordinary citizen who knows what to do. After cardiac arrest, every minute without CPR and without an AED reduces the chance of survival. Following the AHA 2025 guidelines, this lesson explains the primary assessment (DRABC), cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), the automated external defibrillator (AED), the Heimlich maneuver, stopping bleeding, burns and some other emergencies. Every citizen should learn this information.",blocks:[{heading:"DRABC: the primary assessment",body:`• D = Danger: is there any danger, such as electricity, fire or traffic? Before anything else, make sure you are safe.
• R = Response: tap their shoulders and ask loudly: "Are you all right?" If there is no response, call 122 and ask for an AED.
• A = Airway: tilt the head back and lift the chin.
• B = Breathing: for no more than 10 seconds, look to see whether the chest is moving and whether breathing is normal.
• C = CPR: if they are not breathing normally, start chest compressions immediately. Members of the public do not need to check for a pulse.`},{heading:"CPR: 30:2 or Hands-Only",body:`• Position: the center of the chest, one hand on top of the other, arms straight.
• Adults (including the elderly): depth 5–6 cm, rate 100–120 compressions per minute.
• 30 compressions + 2 breaths if you are trained; if not, give compressions only (Hands-Only). Children, infants and people who drowned especially need breaths: if you are trained and able, give CPR with breaths; if you cannot, compressions alone are better than nothing.
• Do not stop until the AED arrives, the ambulance team takes over or you can no longer continue.
• Children (from one year old to puberty): with one or both hands, depth about 5 cm (one third of the depth of the chest).
• Infants (under one year): with both thumbs and the hands encircling the chest, or with the heel of one hand; depth about 4 cm. The two-finger method is no longer taught (AHA 2025).
• For children and infants, when two rescuers perform CPR together: 15:2.`},{heading:"AED: automated external defibrillator",body:`AEDs are found in public places such as government offices, hospitals and large shopping centers. Anyone can use one, because the device gives spoken instructions. Using it early greatly increases the chance of survival.
1. Switch the device on.
2. Place the 2 pads on the bare chest, as shown in the picture on the pads.
3. Listen to the device's voice instructions.
4. Call out: "Nobody touch the patient!"
5. If the device tells you to, press the shock button.
6. Immediately continue CPR for 2 minutes and follow the device's instructions.

• During the shock, no one should touch the casualty!`},{heading:"Heimlich: choking",body:`Signs: the person cannot speak, cough or breathe, and holds their throat with their hands.
1. Give 5 blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades (back blows).
2. Then give 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich): place your fist slightly above the navel and well below the breastbone, and press quickly inward and upward.
3. Repeat 5+5 until the object comes out; if the person becomes unconscious, call 122 and start CPR.

• Infants (under one year): 5 back blows (head lower than the chest) + 5 chest thrusts. Never give abdominal thrusts.
• Pregnant women or very overweight people: give thrusts in the center of the chest, not on the abdomen.`},{heading:"Bleeding: Stop the Bleed",body:`1. Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press firmly and continuously. If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another cloth on top.
2. Wound packing: if the wound is deep, pack clean wound gauze into the wound and then press firmly.
3. Tourniquet: for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg, apply it 5–7 cm above the wound (not over the knee or elbow) and tighten it until the bleeding stops. Write down the time it was applied, and never loosen or remove it: only the medical team removes it.

• Do not pull a sharp object out of a wound!
• Eye injury: do not press on the eye.
• Head injury: if a skull fracture is suspected, press only gently.
• Call 122.`},{heading:"Burns and other emergencies",body:`• Burns: 20 minutes of cool running water (not hot water and not ice).
• Fainting: lay the person on their back and raise their legs slightly.
• Person with diabetes: if they are conscious and able to swallow, give them something sweet.
• Panic attack: stay calm and help them to breathe slowly.
• Head or neck injury: do not move the person; keep them still and call 122. Move them only if the place is unsafe or they need CPR, keeping the head, neck and back in line. Do not put them on their side (recovery position).
• Fracture: do not move the limb; keep it still and place wrapped ice on the swelling.
• Electric shock: first cut off the electricity.
• Snake bite: move away from the snake and keep the person calm and still. Keep the bitten arm or leg still with a splint, and remove rings, watches and tight clothing before it swells. Call 122 and get the person to a hospital quickly. Do not cut or suck the wound, do not tie a tight band or tourniquet, do not put ice on it and do not use traditional or herbal remedies.
• Poisoning: call 122 and keep the container or label to show the doctors. Do not make the person vomit, and do not give milk, oil, salt water or other home remedies; give nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor tells you to. If kerosene, gasoline or lamp oil was swallowed, vomiting is especially dangerous because it can go into the lungs. If the poison is on the skin or in the eyes, rinse with plenty of running water and take off soiled clothing. If the person is drowsy or unconscious but breathing normally, lay them on their side.`}],tips:["122 for an ambulance and 115 for fire and rescue: memorize them.","Take a CPR and first aid course, and renew it every few years.","Know where the AEDs are near your home and your workplace.","After cardiac arrest, every minute counts: start CPR quickly.",`The song "Stayin' Alive" helps you remember the rhythm of 100–120 compressions per minute.`,"Keep a first aid kit at home, in the car and at work.","In the first aid kit: clean cloths, gloves, a tourniquet, scissors and a list of emergency numbers."],warnings:["Never touch a person who has had an electric shock until the electricity has been cut off.","If a back or neck injury is suspected, do not move a person who has fallen, unless the place is unsafe or they need CPR.","Tourniquet: write down the time it was applied, and never loosen or remove it.","Do not put hot water, ice, butter or toothpaste on a burn.","AED pads must not overlap; for children under 8 years old, use child pads if available."],activity:{prompt:"On the street, you see a 55-year-old person collapse. They are not responding and not breathing. There is an AED at the entrance of a nearby government office, and another person is there. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I make sure the area is safe and check for a response. I call 122 and send the other person to bring the AED. I start CPR (30:2) immediately and use the AED as soon as it arrives.",correct:!0,feedback:"Calling 122, sending for the AED and starting CPR at once matter, because every minute without CPR lowers the chance of survival."},{text:"I only send someone to bring the AED and wait without doing CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting without CPR wastes minutes that matter; start compressions at once while the other person brings the AED."},{text:"I give them water",correct:!1,feedback:"Never give anything by mouth to an unconscious person; call 122 and start CPR."},{text:"I record a video from a distance",correct:!1,feedback:"Filming does not help; this person needs CPR and an AED now, so call 122 and start compressions."}]},quiz:[{question:"In CPR, what is the compression depth for adults?",options:[{text:"2 cm",correct:!1},{text:"5–6 cm",correct:!0},{text:"10 cm",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"For all adults, including the elderly, press 5–6 cm deep at 100–120 compressions per minute. Do not reduce the depth."},{question:"Who can use an AED?",options:[{text:"Only doctors",correct:!1},{text:"Anyone: the device gives spoken instructions",correct:!0},{text:"Only firefighters",correct:!1},{text:"No one outside a hospital",correct:!1}],explanation:"Anyone can use an AED: the device gives spoken instructions, and using it early greatly increases the chance of survival."},{question:"How is CPR performed on infants (under one year)?",options:[{text:"With both hands, as for adults",correct:!1},{text:"With both thumbs (hands encircling the chest) or the heel of one hand, depth about 4 cm",correct:!0},{text:"With two fingers, 2 cm",correct:!1},{text:"CPR is not performed on infants",correct:!1}],explanation:"For infants, use both thumbs with the hands encircling the chest, or the heel of one hand, about 4 cm deep. The two-finger method is no longer taught (AHA 2025)."},{question:"After applying a tourniquet, what should you do?",options:[{text:"I loosen it after 10 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"I write down the time it was applied and never loosen it before the medical team arrives",correct:!0},{text:"I release it every half hour and tighten it again",correct:!1},{text:"I remove it as soon as the bleeding stops",correct:!1}],explanation:"Write down the time the tourniquet was applied and never loosen or remove it; only the medical team removes it."},{question:"An infant under one year old is choking on something and is still conscious. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Abdominal thrusts (Heimlich) immediately",correct:!1},{text:"5 back blows + 5 chest thrusts",correct:!0},{text:"CPR immediately",correct:!1},{text:"I leave them alone",correct:!1}],explanation:"For a conscious choking infant: 5 back blows with the head lower than the chest, then 5 chest thrusts. Never give abdominal thrusts to an infant."}]},{id:16,icon:"DoorOpen",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Surviving a fire at home",subtitle:"Smoke alarms, the family escape plan and what to do if you are trapped",summary:"A fire in a home can spread very quickly: once the smoke alarm sounds, you may have as little as two minutes to get out safely. Working smoke alarms, an escape plan that the whole family has practiced and a few simple rules save lives: get out, stay out, close the doors behind you and call 115 from outside.",blocks:[{heading:"Smoke alarms: the early warning",body:`A smoke alarm can wake you when there is smoke, even in the middle of the night.
• Install smoke alarms in every bedroom, outside the sleeping areas and on every floor of the home, on the ceiling or high on a wall, following the manufacturer's instructions.
• Keep them away from the cooking stove and the bathroom door, so that cooking fumes and steam do not set them off.
• Test every alarm once a month with its test button.
• Change replaceable batteries at least once a year, and at once if the alarm keeps giving a short chirp (a low battery).
• Replace the whole alarm when it is 10 years old, or earlier if the manufacturer's instructions say so.
• Never remove the battery or switch an alarm off because cooking sets it off.`},{heading:"The family escape plan",body:`Make the plan together and practice it with everyone who lives in the home.
1. Draw a simple map of the home that shows every door and window.
2. Find two ways out of every room, if possible: the door and a second way, such as a window that opens.
3. Keep corridors, stairs and exits clear. Doors and window bars on the way out must open quickly from the inside; keep any key in a fixed place next to the door.
4. Choose a meeting point outside, in front of the home and a safe distance away, such as a tree or an electricity pole, where everyone is counted.
5. Decide who will help children, older people and people with disabilities to get out.
6. Practice the plan at least twice a year, at night and in the daytime.`},{heading:"When the alarm sounds: get out and stay out",body:`• Shout "Fire!" to warn everyone and leave at once by the nearest safe way. Do not stop to collect belongings.
• If there is smoke, get low and go under it to your way out: the cleaner air is near the floor.
• Before you open a closed door, feel the door and its handle with the back of your hand. If it is hot, or smoke is coming in around it, keep it closed and use your second way out.
• Close the doors behind you as you leave: a closed door slows the spread of smoke, heat and fire.
• Use the stairs, never the elevator.
• Go to the meeting point and call 115 from outside. Give the address and a nearby landmark first.
• Never go back inside for people, pets or belongings. If anyone is missing, tell the firefighters at once where they may be.`},{heading:"If you are trapped: close the door, seal it, call and signal",body:`If smoke or fire blocks both ways out:
1. Stay in a room with a window and close the door between you and the fire.
2. Put towels, blankets or clothes around the door and over air vents to keep the smoke out.
3. Call 115 and say exactly where you are: the address, the floor and the room. Do not hang up until you are told to.
4. Signal at the window with a light-colored cloth or a flashlight, so that the firefighters can see you. If you open the window for air, close it again if smoke comes in.
5. Stay low and wait for the firefighters. Do not jump from an upper floor.`},{heading:"Clothes on fire and burns",body:`• If your clothes catch fire: stop, drop to the ground, cover your face with your hands and roll over and over until the flames are out. Do not run.
• If someone cannot drop and roll, put out the flames with a heavy blanket or coat.
• Cool the burn at once under cool running water for 20 minutes. Do not put ice, butter, toothpaste or creams on it, and do not burst blisters.
• Remove rings, watches and clothing near the burn, but not anything stuck to the skin. Then lay clean plastic wrap or a clean cloth loosely over it.
• Call 122 if the burn is large or deep, is on the face, hands, feet or genitals, was caused by electricity or a chemical, or if the person has trouble breathing.`}],tips:["At night, close the doors of the bedrooms and the kitchen: a closed door slows the spread of smoke and fire.","Keep a flashlight and your phone next to your bed.","Make sure your house number or a clear landmark can be seen from the street, day and night.","Write 115, 122 and your full address with a landmark in a place where everyone can see them.","Teach children the escape plan and the meeting point: they get out, tell an adult and never try to fight a fire."],warnings:["Never go back into a burning building, not even for a family member: tell the firefighters where the person is.","Do not walk upright through smoke, and never open a door that feels hot.","Never use the elevator in a fire."],activity:{prompt:"At night the smoke alarm wakes you. You touch your bedroom door with the back of your hand and it is hot. Your bedroom is on the third floor and has a window. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I open the door quickly and run through the corridor to the stairs.",correct:!1,feedback:"A hot door means fire or very hot smoke on the other side; opening it can let them into your room. Keep it closed and use your second way out or wait at the window."},{text:"I keep the door closed, seal the gaps with towels or clothes, call 115 with the address, floor and room, and signal at the window with a light-colored cloth.",correct:!0,feedback:"The closed, sealed door protects you from smoke and fire, and 115 needs your exact location to reach you quickly."},{text:"I jump from the window at once, before the smoke reaches the room.",correct:!1,feedback:"A jump from the third floor can kill or badly injure you. Seal the door, call 115 and signal at the window: the firefighters can reach you there."},{text:"I hide under the bed and wait quietly for the smoke to pass.",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke does not pass by itself, and the firefighters must be able to find you. Stay by the window, signal and tell 115 where you are."}]},quiz:[{question:"Where should smoke alarms be installed in a home?",options:[{text:"Only in the kitchen, directly above the cooking stove",correct:!1},{text:"In every bedroom, outside the sleeping areas and on every floor",correct:!0},{text:"Only in the living room, next to the front door, where everyone can hear them",correct:!1},{text:"Inside a cupboard, where dust and children cannot reach them",correct:!1}],explanation:"Smoke alarms belong in every bedroom, outside the sleeping areas and on every floor. Directly above the stove, cooking fumes set them off and people are tempted to switch them off."},{question:"How often should you test each smoke alarm with its test button?",options:[{text:"Once every five years",correct:!1},{text:"Once a month",correct:!0},{text:"Only when you change the battery",correct:!1},{text:"Never: testing wears the alarm out",correct:!1}],explanation:"Test every alarm once a month. Change replaceable batteries at least once a year, and replace the whole alarm at 10 years or as the manufacturer's instructions say."},{question:"You have escaped from the burning house, but a family member is still missing. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stay outside, call 115 and tell the firefighters where the person may be",correct:!0},{text:"Go back inside quickly to look for them before the firefighters arrive at the house",correct:!1},{text:"Wait silently at the meeting point until the fire has gone out",correct:!1},{text:"Send a child back in, because children are small and quick",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never go back into a burning building. Firefighters have protective clothing and breathing equipment; telling them at once where the person may be is the fastest help."},{question:"Smoke and fire block both ways out of your room. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Open the door and the window wide to let the smoke out of the room",correct:!1},{text:"Jump from the window at once, even from an upper floor",correct:!1},{text:"Close the door, seal the gaps and call 115 with your location",correct:!0},{text:"Hide in the wardrobe, where the smoke cannot reach you",correct:!1}],explanation:"A closed and sealed door keeps smoke out longer. Tell 115 the address, floor and room, then signal at the window so that the firefighters can find you."},{question:"Your clothes catch fire. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Run outside quickly so that the wind blows the flames out",correct:!1},{text:"Stop, drop, cover your face and roll until the flames are out",correct:!0},{text:"Stand still and wait for someone to bring water from the kitchen",correct:!1},{text:"Take off the burning clothes while running to the bathroom",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stop, drop and roll smothers the flames; do not run. Afterwards, cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122 for a serious burn."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_home_escape_plan.webp",title:"A family practices its home escape plan",description:"Parents and children leave the home low under the smoke, close the door behind them and meet at the meeting point outside."}]},{id:17,icon:"Fuel",accent:"#b45309",title:"Heaters, gas cylinders, carbon monoxide and generators",subtitle:"Safe heating, LPG cylinders, the invisible gas CO and generators at home and in the neighborhood",summary:"In winter and during power cuts, many homes use gas and kerosene heaters, LPG cylinders and generators. Used wrongly, they can cause fires, explosions and carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning; CO is a gas that you cannot see or smell. This lesson gives the safe rules for each of them and explains what to do if you smell gas or if someone shows signs of CO poisoning.",blocks:[{heading:"Gas and kerosene heaters",body:`• Use a heater in good condition and follow the manufacturer's instructions. Use only the fuel the manufacturer names: never put gasoline in a kerosene heater.
• Keep anything that can burn (curtains, bedding, clothes, furniture) at least 1 meter (3 feet) from the heater, and keep children away from it.
• Put the heater on a flat, firm floor, away from doors and from places where people walk.
• A heater without a flue (a pipe that takes the exhaust outside) releases its exhaust into the room: keep the room door open to the rest of the house and open a window a little for fresh air.
• Turn portable gas and kerosene heaters off before you sleep and when you leave the room.
• Refuel a kerosene heater outside, and only after it has been turned off and has cooled down.
• If a heater flares up or catches fire, do not carry it: switch it off with its shut-off switch if that is safe; otherwise get everyone out, close the door and call 115.`},{heading:"LPG cylinders",body:`• Use cylinders, regulators and hoses in good condition; do not use any part that is damaged, cracked or leaking.
• Keep the cylinder upright, in a ventilated place, away from heaters, stoves, direct sun and other heat.
• LPG is heavier than air: leaking gas collects near the floor and in low places. Do not keep cylinders in a basement, and do not store spare cylinders in bedrooms or near the way out.
• Have the regulator, hose and connections fitted and checked by a qualified technician, and replace a hose that is cracked, hard or old.
• Never look for a leak with a flame, a match or a lighter. A technician checks connections with soapy water: bubbles show a leak.
• Close the cylinder valve when you are not using the gas.`},{heading:"If you smell gas",body:`1. Do not light any flame and do not switch lights or appliances on or off. Do not use a phone inside, and do not start a generator or a car nearby.
2. Open doors and windows on your way out, if you can do it quickly.
3. Close the gas valve only if it is close to you and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected and without making any spark; otherwise do not go near it.
4. Get everyone out and warn the neighbors.
5. Call 115 from outside, and do not go back in until the specialists say it is safe.`},{heading:"Carbon monoxide (CO): the invisible poison",body:`Carbon monoxide (CO) is made when fuel burns: in heaters, stoves, water heaters, generators, car engines and charcoal. It has no color and no smell.
• Signs of CO poisoning: headache, dizziness, weakness, nausea or vomiting, chest pain and confusion. If several people in the same place feel ill at the same time, think of CO.
• People who are asleep can die from CO before they notice any sign.
• Install a CO alarm near the sleeping areas, test it every month and follow the manufacturer's instructions.
• Have heaters, water heaters and chimneys checked every year by a qualified technician.
• Never heat the home with a gas oven, and never bring a charcoal brazier or a barbecue indoors.`},{heading:"If the CO alarm sounds or people feel ill",body:`• Get everyone out into fresh air at once; turn the heater off on the way only if it is quick and safe.
• From outside, call 122 for anyone with signs, even if they start to feel better, and call 115.
• Do not go back inside to rescue anyone: wait for the trained teams.
• Nobody goes back in until the specialists say it is safe and the appliance has been checked.`},{heading:"Generators at home and in the neighborhood",body:`• Run a portable generator only outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from windows, doors and vents, with the exhaust pointing away from buildings. Never run it inside a house, garage, basement, shop or storeroom, or on a closed balcony, even with the doors and windows open.
• Before refueling, turn the generator off and let it cool down: fuel spilled on hot parts can catch fire. Keep fuel outside the living areas in a proper closed container.
• Never plug a generator into a wall outlet or connect it to the house wiring (backfeeding): it can kill neighbors and electricity workers. Only a qualified electrician may connect a generator, through a transfer switch.
• Neighborhood generators: do not touch their cables or switches yourself. Report loose or damaged cables, sparks or a smell of burning to the operator, and call 115 in an emergency. Only a qualified electrician should fit the switch that changes your home between the neighborhood generator and the public grid.`}],tips:["Install a smoke alarm and a CO alarm in the home and test them every month.","Have heaters and gas appliances checked every year by a qualified technician.","Keep a safe zone of at least 1 meter (3 feet) around heaters, especially for children.","Close the cylinder valve when you are not using the gas.","Keep heater fuel and generator fuel outside the living areas, in proper closed containers away from heat."],warnings:["Never run a generator, a charcoal brazier or a barbecue indoors, even with the doors and windows open.","Never look for a gas leak with a flame.","Never plug a generator into a wall outlet or connect it to the house wiring."],activity:{prompt:"On a cold night, the kerosene heater has been burning for hours in the closed sitting room. Your mother and your son both have a headache and feel dizzy and sick. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I give them headache tablets, open the window a little and we stay in the warm room.",correct:!1,feedback:"Tablets do not remove CO, and a small opening is not enough once people have signs. Everyone must get out into fresh air, and you must call 122."},{text:"I get everyone out into fresh air at once, turn the heater off on the way only if it is quick and safe, and call 122 and 115 from outside; nobody goes back in until it is checked.",correct:!0,feedback:"Two people with the same signs near a burning heater point to CO poisoning: fresh air and a call to 122 come first, and the heater must be checked before it is used again."},{text:"I turn the heater off and let them sleep, because rest helps a headache.",correct:!1,feedback:"People who are asleep can die from CO without waking up. Get everyone out into fresh air and call 122."},{text:"I add more kerosene so that the heater burns better and makes less smoke.",correct:!1,feedback:"More fuel does not stop CO. The people need fresh air and medical help now: get them out and call 122."}]},quiz:[{question:"How far should curtains, bedding and other things that burn be kept from a heater?",options:[{text:"About 10 centimeters is enough",correct:!1},{text:"At least 1 meter (3 feet)",correct:!0},{text:"Any distance, if the heater is new",correct:!1},{text:"No distance, if the heater is on low",correct:!1}],explanation:"Keep anything that can burn at least 1 meter (3 feet) from heaters, and keep children away from them too."},{question:"What is the safe way to check an LPG hose and its connections for a leak?",options:[{text:"Hold a lit match near the connection and watch for a flame",correct:!1},{text:"Turn on the stove and listen to the sound of the flame",correct:!1},{text:"A qualified technician checks them with soapy water",correct:!0},{text:"Smell the hose closely with the cylinder valve fully open",correct:!1}],explanation:"A flame can ignite leaking gas and cause a fire or an explosion. A qualified technician checks connections with soapy water: bubbles show a leak."},{question:"Which of these can be a sign of carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning?",options:[{text:"A strong smell of gas coming from the heater",correct:!1},{text:"Headache and dizziness in several people at once",correct:!0},{text:"Blue smoke that you can see clearly near the floor",correct:!1},{text:"A skin rash that appears about a week later",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO has no color and no smell. Headache, dizziness, weakness and nausea, especially in several people at once near fuel-burning equipment, are warning signs. A smell of gas means a gas leak, not CO."},{question:"Where can a portable generator be run safely?",options:[{text:"Outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from windows, doors and vents",correct:!0},{text:"In the garage, as long as the big garage door stays wide open",correct:!1},{text:"On a closed balcony, with the window next to it open for fresh air",correct:!1},{text:"Inside the house, next to an open window, with a fan blowing the smoke out",correct:!1}],explanation:"Generator exhaust contains CO, which can kill within minutes indoors. Run a generator only outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from windows, doors and vents, with the exhaust pointing away from buildings."},{question:"How may a generator be connected to the electrical wiring of a home?",options:[{text:"By plugging it into a wall outlet with a cable that has two plugs",correct:!1},{text:"Only by a qualified electrician, through a transfer switch",correct:!0},{text:"By the owner, directly to the fuse box, during a power cut",correct:!1},{text:"By any neighbor who has connected a generator before",correct:!1}],explanation:"Plugging a generator into a wall outlet or the house wiring (backfeeding) can electrocute neighbors and electricity workers. Only a qualified electrician may connect it, through a transfer switch."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_heaters_gas_generators.webp",title:"Safe heating and a generator outdoors",description:"A heater with a clear safe zone around it, an upright gas cylinder with a checked hose, a CO alarm on the wall and a generator running outdoors, away from the windows."}]},{id:18,icon:"Building",accent:"#4f46e5",title:"Living in apartments and high-rise buildings",subtitle:"Exits, stairs, fire doors and what to do when there is a fire in your building",summary:"In an apartment building, a fire in one apartment can put everyone in danger: smoke spreads through corridors and stairs, and the way out is longer. Knowing your exits, keeping the stairs and fire doors working and knowing when to leave and when to stay in your apartment can save your family. Always follow your building's plan and the instructions of Civil Defense.",blocks:[{heading:"Know your building before a fire",body:`• Learn where all the exit stairs on your floor are, in case the nearest one is blocked by fire or smoke. Count the doors from your apartment to each stairway, so that you can find it in the dark or in smoke.
• Ask the building management what fire safety the building has (fire alarm, extinguishers, emergency lighting, evacuation plan) and how residents are told to respond to an alarm.
• Make an escape plan for your apartment and agree on a meeting point outside the building.
• Find out which neighbors on your floor will need help to go down the stairs, such as older people and people with disabilities, and agree with the management and the neighbors who will help them.`},{heading:"Stairs, corridors and fire doors",body:`• Never store anything in corridors, on the stairs or in front of exit doors: no furniture, shoes, bicycles, gas cylinders or fuel.
• Fire doors and stair doors must close by themselves and stay closed. Never wedge them open or tie them back, and report damaged doors, closers or seals to the management.
• Exit and stairway doors must not be locked or blocked, and must open from the inside without a key.
• Do not run generator cables, extension cords or hoses through stair doors or fire doors: they stop the doors from closing.`},{heading:"Use the stairs, never the elevator",body:`In a fire, use the stairs to get out. Do not use the elevator unless the firefighters tell you to: it can stop and trap you between floors in the smoke.

If someone cannot use the stairs, do not take them into the elevator. Tell 115 and the firefighters at once exactly where the person is: the floor and the apartment number.`},{heading:"If the fire is in your apartment",body:`1. Warn everyone in the apartment and get out at once.
2. Close the apartment door behind you: the closed door keeps the smoke and fire inside.
3. Warn the neighbors: knock on their doors and shout "Fire!", and press the fire alarm if the building has one.
4. Go down by the stairs, not by the elevator.
5. Call 115 from outside: give the address, the floor and the apartment number, and go to the meeting point.`},{heading:"If the fire is elsewhere in the building",body:`• When the alarm sounds or you smell smoke, feel your apartment door with the back of your hand before you open it.
• If the door is cool and the corridor and stairs are clear, leave by the nearest stairway, close the doors behind you and go to the meeting point.
• If the door is hot, or the corridor or stairs are full of smoke, stay in your apartment: keep the door closed, put wet towels or sheets around it and over the vents, and call 115 with the address, the floor and the apartment number.
• Go to a window or balcony away from the smoke and signal with a light-colored cloth or a flashlight. Open a window a little for fresh air, but close it if smoke comes in.
• Listen to announcements and follow your building's plan and the instructions of Civil Defense and the firefighters: rescue from a tall building can take a long time.`},{heading:"Balconies, gas cylinders, generators and cables",body:`• Do not store spare gas cylinders, fuel, old furniture or rubbish on balconies. Where a gas cylinder is kept for cooking, keep it upright, away from heat and direct sun, with the valve closed when it is not in use.
• Never run a generator on a balcony: its exhaust carries CO into your home and your neighbors' homes through windows and doors.
• Generator and neighborhood power cables must be fitted by a qualified electrician. Report loose, damaged or hot cables in the building to the management.
• Put cigarettes out completely in a deep ashtray; never throw them from a balcony or a window.`}],tips:["Know at least two exit stairways from your floor.","Keep your keys, phone and a flashlight next to your bed, and take them with you when you leave.","Take part in the building's fire drills as if they were real.","Ask the management to check the fire alarm, emergency lighting, extinguishers and fire doors regularly.","Install smoke alarms inside your apartment and test them every month."],warnings:["Never use the elevator during a fire unless the firefighters tell you to.","Never wedge a fire door or a stair door open.","Do not go into a corridor or stairway full of smoke: stay in your apartment, seal the door and call 115."],activity:{prompt:"You live on the seventh floor. The fire alarm sounds. Your apartment door feels cool, but when you open it a little, the corridor is full of thick smoke. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take the elevator down quickly, because it is faster than seven floors of stairs.",correct:!1,feedback:"Never use the elevator in a fire: it can stop and trap you in the smoke. Stay in your apartment and call 115."},{text:"I run through the smoke to the stairs while holding my breath.",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke is poisonous, and you can lose your way in it. Close the door, seal the gaps and call 115 from your apartment."},{text:"I close the door again, seal the gaps with wet towels, call 115 with the address, floor and apartment number, and signal from a window away from the smoke.",correct:!0,feedback:"Thick smoke in the corridor blocks the way out. Your closed apartment door protects you while 115 sends help to your floor."},{text:"I leave the apartment door open so that I can hear the firefighters arrive.",correct:!1,feedback:"An open door lets smoke into the apartment. Keep it closed and sealed, and tell 115 where you are."}]},quiz:[{question:"Why must fire doors and stair doors in a building stay closed?",options:[{text:"They keep the corridors warm in winter and save electricity for everyone",correct:!1},{text:"They slow the spread of smoke and fire and protect the escape stairs",correct:!0},{text:"They stop strangers and children from walking between the floors at night",correct:!1},{text:"They are mainly for decoration, so it does not matter if they stay open",correct:!1}],explanation:"A closed fire door slows smoke, heat and fire and keeps the stairway usable for escape. Never wedge it open, and report a door that does not close by itself."},{question:"The fire is inside your apartment. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Get out, close the door behind you, warn the neighbors and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Leave the apartment door open so that the smoke can escape into the corridor",correct:!1},{text:"Stay inside and try to fight a large fire with buckets of water",correct:!1},{text:"Take the elevator down, then ask the building guard to deal with it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Closing the door of the burning apartment keeps smoke and fire out of the corridor and stairs that your neighbors need. Warn them, use the stairs and call 115 from outside."},{question:"What should be done with shoes, bicycles or gas cylinders left in the corridor or on the stairs?",options:[{text:"Keep them neatly against one wall so that people can still pass",correct:!1},{text:"Leave them there in summer and move them only in winter",correct:!1},{text:"Cover them with a cloth so that they do not catch fire",correct:!1},{text:"Remove them: corridors and stairs must stay clear for escape",correct:!0}],explanation:"In a fire, people escape through smoke and darkness, and anything in a corridor or on the stairs can block them or burn. Nothing is stored in corridors, on stairs or in front of exits."},{question:"When may you use the elevator during a fire in your building?",options:[{text:"When you live above the fifth floor",correct:!1},{text:"Only when the firefighters tell you to use it",correct:!0},{text:"When the smoke is only on the lower floors",correct:!1},{text:"When you are carrying children or heavy belongings",correct:!1}],explanation:"Use the stairs. An elevator can stop and trap people in smoke; the firefighters decide whether an elevator may be used."},{question:"Smoke in the corridor traps you in your apartment. What do you tell 115?",options:[{text:"Only your name, because they will find the building themselves",correct:!1},{text:"The address, floor, apartment number and how many people are with you",correct:!0},{text:"Only that there is smoke; they will call you back later to ask for the details",correct:!1},{text:"Nothing yet: you wait quietly until someone knocks on the door",correct:!1}],explanation:"The firefighters need to know exactly where you are: the address, floor and apartment, and how many people are there. Do not hang up until you are told to."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_apartment_fire_safety.webp",title:"Escaping safely from an apartment building",description:"Residents go down the stairs past a closed fire door, while a family in another apartment waits by the window and signals with a light-colored cloth."}]},{id:19,icon:"Leaf",accent:"#c2410c",title:"Crop, stubble and vegetation fires",subtitle:"Preventing fires at harvest time and staying safe when a field fire comes near",summary:"In summer, dry wheat and barley fields, stubble and grass catch fire easily, and the wind can spread a field fire very quickly. These fires are often started by people: a cigarette, a spark or a hot exhaust from machinery, or a fire lit to burn stubble. This lesson explains how to prevent them and what to do when a fire comes near your field, your village or your home.",blocks:[{heading:"Why field fires are dangerous",body:`• Dry crops, stubble and grass catch fire easily, especially on hot, dry and windy days.
• The wind pushes the fire forward and carries burning embers ahead of the flames, where they can start new fires.
• Fire spreads fastest uphill and in the direction the wind is blowing.
• Smoke hides the way, and a fast fire can surround people, animals and vehicles in the field.`},{heading:"Prevention at harvest time",body:`• Before and during the harvest, clean chaff, straw and dust off harvesters and tractors every day, especially around the engine, the exhaust and the turbocharger: dust and straw that reach these hot parts can catch fire.
• Check machines for worn bearings, slipping belts and fuel or oil leaks, and repair faults before you start work.
• Keep water and a suitable fire extinguisher on the harvester and with the team.
• Plow or clear a firebreak (a strip of bare soil with nothing that can burn) around the field and between the fields and the village.
• Stop working with machines when it is very hot, dry and windy.
• Do not drive or park cars and tractors on dry grass or stubble: a hot exhaust can set it on fire.`},{heading:"Do not light fires near fields",body:`• Burning stubble or rubbish in the open can escape with the wind into neighboring fields, orchards and villages. Before you burn anything, check with Civil Defense and the local authorities whether and when burning is allowed; never burn on a dry or windy day, and never leave a fire alone.
• Never throw cigarettes from cars or drop them in fields or grass, and do not smoke while you work in the harvest.
• Do not light cooking fires or barbecues near crops or dry grass, and put any fire out completely with water before you leave.
• Take glass bottles and rubbish home: glass left in a field can focus sunlight on dry grass.`},{heading:"When a fire comes near",body:`• Leave early. Do not wait until the fire is close: smoke and flames can block the road. If the authorities tell you to leave, go at once.
• Only try to put out a very small fire that has just started, with water or soil, if your way out is behind you and the wind is not blowing the fire toward you. If it does not go out at once, leave.
• Move out of the fire's path: never in the direction the wind is pushing it and never uphill above it. Use roads and paths, and go toward ground with little or nothing to burn, such as plowed land.
• Call 115 from a safe place and give the exact location (the village, the road, the field or a landmark) and say whether the fire is moving toward houses or people.
• Warn other farmers and the village by phone, not by running in front of the fire.`},{heading:"Protecting villages and homes",body:`• Keep the area around houses, barns and animal pens clear of dry grass, straw, firewood and rubbish for at least 9 meters (30 feet).
• Keep stacks of straw and hay away from houses and roads, and do not store fuel next to them.
• Agree in the village on who calls 115, where people gather and how older people, children and animals will be moved.
• Keep roads and gates clear so that fire engines can reach the village and the fields.
• When a fire is near, follow the instructions of Civil Defense and do not return until they say it is safe.`}],tips:["Clean harvesters and tractors every day of the harvest, especially around the engine and the exhaust.","Keep water and a fire extinguisher on every harvester.","Watch the weather: hot, dry and windy days are the most dangerous.","Save 115 in your phone and learn a clear landmark for each of your fields.","Tell the farmers around you as soon as you see smoke."],warnings:["Never throw a cigarette into dry grass or stubble, or from a car window.","Never try to stop a large field fire yourself: leave and call 115.","Never run uphill or in the direction the wind is blowing to escape a fire."],activity:{prompt:"You are harvesting wheat with a harvester on a hot, windy afternoon. You see smoke and small flames in the stubble behind the machine, and the wind is blowing them toward the next field. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop, move the machine and the people out of the fire's path, call 115 with the exact location and warn the nearby farmers; I use water or the extinguisher only while the fire is still very small and my way out is clear.",correct:!0,feedback:"A field fire grows fast in the wind. Getting people out of its path and calling 115 early come first; only a very small fire may be attacked, and only with a safe way out."},{text:"I drive the harvester through the flames to reach the other side of the field.",correct:!1,feedback:"Driving through fire can trap you and set the machine on fire. Move out of the fire's path and call 115."},{text:"I keep harvesting, because the stubble fire will burn itself out.",correct:!1,feedback:"The wind can carry the fire into crops, orchards and villages. Stop, get people out of its path and call 115 at once."},{text:"I run ahead of the fire, in the direction the wind is blowing, to warn the next farm.",correct:!1,feedback:"Running where the wind is pushing the fire puts you in its path. Warn others by phone and move out of the fire's path."}]},quiz:[{question:"Which part of a harvester is the most important to keep clean of chaff and dust?",options:[{text:"The driver's seat, the cabin windows and the mirrors",correct:!1},{text:"The engine area, around the hot exhaust and turbocharger",correct:!0},{text:"The tires and the steps that lead up to the cabin door",correct:!1},{text:"The grain tank, after it has been emptied at the end of the day",correct:!1}],explanation:"Chaff and dust blown onto the hot exhaust or turbocharger can catch fire. Clean the engine area every day of the harvest, and more often on hot days."},{question:"A field fire is coming toward you, pushed by the wind. Which way do you escape?",options:[{text:"Straight ahead in the direction the wind is blowing, as fast as possible",correct:!1},{text:"Up the nearest hill, so that you can see where the fire is going",correct:!1},{text:"Out of its path along a road, not uphill and not with the wind",correct:!0},{text:"Into the standing wheat, where the tall crop hides you from the smoke",correct:!1}],explanation:"Fire runs fastest with the wind and uphill. Move out of its path along roads or paths toward ground with little to burn, and call 115."},{question:"What should you tell 115 when you report a field fire?",options:[{text:"Only that there is smoke somewhere, and then hang up quickly to save time",correct:!1},{text:"The exact place (village, road or landmark) and where the fire is heading",correct:!0},{text:"The name of the field's owner, the type of crop and how much it is worth",correct:!1},{text:"Nothing more, because Civil Defense can see every field fire from far away",correct:!1}],explanation:"Civil Defense needs to find the fire quickly: give the village, the road and a landmark, say whether houses or people are in danger, and do not hang up until you are told to."},{question:"Why is burning stubble dangerous?",options:[{text:"Stubble does not burn well, so the fire always goes out at once by itself",correct:!1},{text:"It is only dangerous at night, when people cannot see the smoke",correct:!1},{text:"The fire can escape with the wind into nearby fields and villages",correct:!0},{text:"It is dangerous only for the person who lights it, not for others",correct:!1}],explanation:"Fires lit for farming can spread out of control, especially on dry and windy days. Check with Civil Defense and the local authorities before any burning, and never leave a fire alone."},{question:"Which of these can start a field fire?",options:[{text:"A cigarette thrown from a car window into dry grass",correct:!0},{text:"A green tree standing in the shade at the edge of the field",correct:!1},{text:"Rain falling on the stubble after the harvest",correct:!1},{text:"A closed water tank parked beside the field",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cigarettes, sparks and hot exhausts from vehicles and machines, and fires lit in the open, start many field fires. Never throw cigarettes out, and do not park on dry grass."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_crop_fire_prevention.webp",title:"Preventing a fire in a wheat field",description:"A farmer cleans the engine area of a harvester next to a plowed firebreak, with a water tank and a fire extinguisher ready at the edge of a dry wheat field."}]},{id:20,icon:"Bomb",accent:"#b45309",title:"Mines and unexploded ordnance: stay safe",subtitle:"Recognize the danger, keep away, report",summary:"Mines, cluster munitions and other unexploded ordnance can stay dangerous for decades, even when they look old, rusty or harmless. Everyone can stay safe by following a few rules: do not touch, keep away, keep others away and report.",blocks:[{heading:"What can be dangerous",body:"Explosive ordnance comes in many sizes, shapes and colors. It can lie on the ground, be buried in soil, hide in grass, rubble or old buildings, or hang in trees. Treat these as dangerous:",points:["Mines: often buried or hidden, and very hard to see.","Small bomblets from cluster munitions: some are brightly colored and can look like toys.","Unexploded shells, rockets, mortar bombs and grenades.","Any metal or plastic object of unknown shape in a place where there was fighting.","Old, rusty or broken items: they can still explode."]},{heading:"If you see a suspicious object",points:["Stop and do not go closer.","Do not touch, move, kick or pick it up; do not throw anything at it and never try to burn it.","Warn the people around you and keep everyone away, especially children.","Remember the place and nearby landmarks.","Move well away before you use a phone, then call 115 and follow the instructions of Civil Defense.","Never carry the object to your home, a police station or a Civil Defense station."]},{heading:"Warning signs and fenced areas",body:'Warning signs for explosive hazards are usually red or orange, often with a skull and crossbones and the word "Danger" or "Mines". A dangerous area can also be marked with red-painted stones, fences, wire, string or tape. Signs in your area may look different.',points:["Respect every sign and fence, and never cross them.","Never remove, move or take a sign, even an old or broken one.","No sign does not mean safe: most dangerous areas are not marked.","Be careful near overgrown fields, unused paths, ruins and abandoned military positions; use main roads and well-used paths."]},{heading:"If you think you are in a mined area",body:`You see a mine, a warning sign or a strange object near your feet, or there has been an explosion:
1. Stop at once. Stay still and do not move your feet.
2. Call out for help, and warn others not to come to you.
3. Wait for a trained team, even if it takes a long time.

Never try to walk back in your own footprints, test the ground with a stick or place marks: these are not safe ways out. If someone is injured, do not run to them: call for help and wait for trained rescuers.`},{heading:"Advice for people at higher risk",points:["Farmers: if you find a strange object while plowing or digging, stop work, keep everyone away and report it; do not farm land that is marked or that others avoid.","Shepherds: do not take animals into marked or suspected areas, and never follow an animal that has gone in.","Builders and well diggers: if digging uncovers a strange object, stop at once, get everyone away and report it.","Families returning home after fighting: before you use a house, field or road, ask the authorities whether it has been checked.","Scrap collectors: never collect, cut, open or sell military scrap; it can explode and endanger you, the buyer and the neighbors."]},{heading:"Talking with children",body:"Children are curious, and some objects look like toys. Talk with them calmly and often, without frightening them, and praise them when they tell you about something they saw. Teach them:",points:["If you did not drop it, do not pick it up.","Never touch, kick or throw stones at a strange object, and never go closer.","Stay away from signs, fences, ruins and old military places.","Tell an adult at once.","If you think you are in a dangerous place: stop, stand still, shout for help and wait."]}],tips:["Save 115 in your phone and teach it to your family.","Find out which places near your home, fields and grazing land are dangerous; ask the authorities when you are not sure.","When you report, give the place, nearby landmarks and whether people are close to the object.","Do not trust rumors that an area has been cleared; ask the authorities.","Use main roads and well-used paths, especially in areas where there was fighting."],warnings:["Never touch, move, collect or sell explosive ordnance or military scrap, even if it looks old, rusty or already used.","In a mined area, never walk back in your footprints, test the ground or place marks: stop, stay still, call for help and wait.","Do not run to an injured person in a mined area: call for help and wait for trained rescuers."],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_uxo_keep_away.webp",title:"Keep away and report",description:"A farmer keeps his family well away from a suspicious object in a field before calling 115."}],activity:{prompt:"While plowing a field, a farmer sees a rusty metal object in the soil. His son wants to pick it up and sell it as scrap. What should they do?",options:[{text:"They stop work, keep everyone away, remember the place and call 115 once they are well away",correct:!0,feedback:"Old or rusty ordnance can still explode. Stopping work, keeping everyone away and reporting from a safe place protects the family and lets trained teams deal with it."},{text:"They lift it carefully onto the tractor and take it to the Civil Defense station",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving or carrying it can make it explode, on the way or at the station. Leave it where it is and report it from a safe place."},{text:"They plow around it and carry on, because it is old and rusty",correct:!1,feedback:"Age and rust do not make ordnance safe, and plowing nearby can set it off. Stop work, keep away and report it."},{text:"They cover it with soil and put a stone next to it as a mark",correct:!1,feedback:"Covering or marking it means staying next to the danger. Keep away, remember the place and report it to 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"There are no warning signs near an overgrown field where there was fighting. Is it safe to enter?",options:[{text:"No: most dangerous areas are not marked, so stay on used paths and ask the authorities",correct:!0},{text:"Yes: every dangerous area in the region has clear red signs, so a field without them is completely safe",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, once the grass has been burned, because the fire destroys all the mines hidden in the soil",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if older people in the village say the field was cleared of mines many years ago",correct:!1}],explanation:"A missing sign does not mean safe: most affected areas are not marked. Burning does not make an area safe, and rumors are not a check. Use main roads and well-used paths and ask the authorities."},{question:"You realize you may have walked into a mined area. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Stop, keep your feet still, call for help and wait for a trained team",correct:!0},{text:"Walk back slowly and carefully in your own footprints to the road you came from",correct:!1},{text:"Push a stick into the ground in front of you to find a safe way out of the area",correct:!1},{text:"Run as fast as you can to the nearest road so you spend less time in the area",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stopping and waiting keeps you from stepping on another mine. Footprints are hard to see and walking back on them is not safe, testing the ground is a job for specialists, and you cannot outrun an explosion."},{question:"Your neighbor collects old shells and sells them to a scrap dealer. What is the safest advice?",options:[{text:"Stop collecting at once, do not move the items himself and report them to 115",correct:!0},{text:"Keep collecting, but take only the items that look empty, old or rusty and leave the rest",correct:!1},{text:"Open the items first to take out anything dangerous, then sell the metal",correct:!1},{text:"Keep them in a cool, closed room at home until the dealer comes for them",correct:!1}],explanation:"Military scrap can explode at any time, even if it looks empty or rusty, and it endangers the collector, the buyer and the neighbors. Do not move it: report it so that trained teams can deal with it."},{question:"Children find a strange, colorful object near the school. What should adults teach them?",options:[{text:"Do not touch it or go closer, keep friends away and tell an adult at once",correct:!0},{text:"Pick it up slowly and carefully and take it to the teacher to check what it is",correct:!1},{text:"Throw small stones at it from far away to see whether it is dangerous",correct:!1},{text:"Take a clear close-up photo of it and send it to the family group on the phone",correct:!1}],explanation:"Some explosive items look like toys. Touching, carrying, throwing things at it or going closer for a photo can make it explode; keeping away and telling an adult keeps everyone safe."},{question:"Where should you be when you call 115 about a suspicious object?",options:[{text:"Well away from it, in a safe place, after warning others to keep away",correct:!0},{text:"Right next to it, so that you can describe its shape, size and color exactly",correct:!1},{text:"Close to it with the phone camera on, so that you can send a clear picture",correct:!1},{text:"Holding it in your hand, so that you can read any writing or numbers on its side",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never go closer to look or take pictures. Phones and radios should not be used close to explosive ordnance, so move well away, then report the place and nearby landmarks."}]},{id:21,icon:"CloudFog",accent:"#6d28d9",title:"Hazardous release near you: shelter in place or evacuate",subtitle:"Go in, stay in, tune in",summary:"A fire, a factory accident or a damaged tanker can release toxic gas, smoke or dust. Often the safest place is inside a building. This lesson explains how to shelter in place, when to evacuate and what to do if a substance gets on you.",blocks:[{heading:"Go in, stay in, tune in",body:"If the authorities announce a hazardous release, or you see a strange cloud or smoke, smell a strong chemical odor or hear a warning siren near you:",points:["Go in: get inside the nearest solid building at once, and bring children and pets in with you.","Stay in: stay inside until the authorities say it is safe to go out, even to help others.","Tune in: follow official announcements on radio, television or your phone, and do what they say.","Some dangerous gases have no smell: follow the instructions even if you notice nothing."]},{heading:"How to shelter in place",points:["Close and lock all outside doors and windows.","Switch off air conditioners, air coolers, fans and ventilation, and close the vents.","Go to an inner room with few windows, above ground level if you can (not a basement), and close the inner doors.","If you have them, seal gaps around doors, windows and vents with tape and plastic sheeting.","Do not eat or drink anything that may have been exposed.","In a car: close the windows and vents, switch off the air conditioner and heater, and go into the nearest building."]},{heading:"When to evacuate",body:"Evacuate when the authorities tell you to, or when the substance is inside your building and you can get out without passing through it.",points:["If you are told to evacuate, leave at once and follow the instructions you are given.","Move upwind (toward the side the wind is coming from) and, if you can, to higher ground: some gases are heavier than air and collect in low places.","Never pass through the cloud, the smoke or the spilled substance.","If you cannot reach clean air without passing through it, stay as far from it as you can and shelter in place."]},{heading:"If a substance gets on you: remove, remove, remove",body:`1. Remove yourself: move away from the substance into fresh air.
2. Remove outer clothing: take off clothing the substance touched; try not to pull it over your head; do not pull off clothing stuck to the skin; put it in a closed plastic bag.
3. Remove the substance: blot or brush it off the skin with dry tissue or cloth; if the skin itches or burns, rinse it with plenty of water. If it gets into your eyes, rinse them with clean water.

Do not eat, drink, smoke or touch your face. Tell the responders that you were exposed and follow their instructions for a medical check. If someone has trouble breathing or is badly hurt, call 115 and 122.`},{heading:"Never go to look",points:["Do not go toward the scene to look, film or take photos: the substance can reach you without warning.","Do not go outside to help others until the authorities say it is safe; call 115 and 122 instead.","Report a release, a strange cloud or a spill to 115 from a safe place.","Share only official announcements; rumors spread fear and wrong advice."]}],tips:["Choose your shelter room now: an inner room with few windows, above ground level.","Keep tape, scissors, plastic sheeting and a battery-powered radio in your emergency kit.","Learn how to switch off your air conditioner, air cooler and ventilation quickly.","Agree with your family where you will shelter and how you will reach each other.","Phone neighbors who may not have heard the announcement; do not go outside to tell them."],warnings:["Never go toward a cloud, smoke or a spill to look or take photos!","Do not leave your shelter until the authorities say it is safe, even to help others.","When you evacuate, never walk or drive through the cloud or the spilled substance."],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_shelter_in_place.webp",title:"Shelter in place",description:"A family shelters in an inner room with the doors and windows closed and follows official announcements."}],activity:{prompt:"A fire at a nearby warehouse is sending thick, strange-smelling smoke toward your neighborhood, and an official announcement tells people to stay indoors. Your children are playing in the yard. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I bring the children inside, close the doors and windows, switch off the cooler and ventilation, go to an inner room and follow the announcements",correct:!0,feedback:"This is shelter in place: indoors, with outside air shut out, the family breathes much less smoke, and the announcements tell you when it is safe or whether you must leave."},{text:"I drive the family toward the warehouse road to see how big the fire is, then decide",correct:!1,feedback:"Going toward the scene takes you into the smoke. Stay indoors and follow official announcements."},{text:"I open all the windows and switch on the fans so the smoke passes through the house quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Open windows and fans bring the smoke inside. Close everything and switch off fans, coolers and ventilation."},{text:"I walk with the children through the smoke to a relative's house in another neighborhood",correct:!1,feedback:"Walking through the smoke exposes the whole family. Evacuate only when you are told to, and never through the cloud."}]},quiz:[{question:'What does "go in, stay in, tune in" mean?',options:[{text:"Get inside and stay inside until it is declared safe; follow official announcements",correct:!0},{text:"Go into the affected area, stay there to help the injured and keep calling your relatives for news",correct:!1},{text:"Go up to the roof, stay there to watch the cloud and switch on all the fans to clear the air",correct:!1},{text:"Get into your car, stay in it with the engine and air conditioner running and listen to music",correct:!1}],explanation:"Indoors you breathe in much less of the substance. Stay inside until the authorities say it is safe, and follow official announcements on radio, television or your phone."},{question:"While you shelter at home, what should you do with the air conditioner or air cooler?",options:[{text:"Switch it off, together with fans and ventilation, and close the vents",correct:!0},{text:"Run it at full speed so that it pushes the gas back out through the windows and doors",correct:!1},{text:"Leave it on, because its filter removes chemicals from the indoor air",correct:!1},{text:"Keep it on only in the room where the family is sheltering together",correct:!1}],explanation:"Air conditioners, coolers, fans and ventilation can pull outside air, and the substance with it, into the house. Switch them off and close the vents."},{question:"When should you evacuate during a hazardous release?",options:[{text:"When the authorities tell you to, or when it is inside your building and you can get out safely",correct:!0},{text:"As soon as you hear about the release, even if the only way out of the area passes through the cloud",correct:!1},{text:"Only after you have gone outside to see for yourself which way the cloud is moving",correct:!1},{text:"Whenever your neighbors leave, whatever the official announcements are saying",correct:!1}],explanation:"Often staying inside is safer than going out. Leave when you are told to, or when the substance has reached you inside and you can get out without passing through it."},{question:"If you have to evacuate, which way is safest?",options:[{text:"Upwind and, if possible, to higher ground, without passing through the cloud",correct:!0},{text:"Downwind, so that the wind helps to carry you and your family quickly away from the cloud",correct:!1},{text:"Down into a valley or an underpass, where the air is calm and still",correct:!1},{text:"Toward the source, because the fire there burns up the dangerous gas",correct:!1}],explanation:"Upwind, the wind carries the substance away from you, and some gases are heavier than air and collect in low places. Never walk through the cloud."},{question:"A liquid chemical splashes on your arm and sleeve. What should you do first?",options:[{text:"Get to fresh air, remove the contaminated outer clothing and blot the liquid off",correct:!0},{text:"Wipe it with your bare hand and carry on, because a small amount cannot cause harm",correct:!1},{text:"Cover it with oil or toothpaste to stop it from soaking into the skin",correct:!1},{text:"Keep the clothing on so that it protects your skin until you reach the hospital for treatment",correct:!1}],explanation:"Remove yourself, remove outer clothing, remove the substance. Taking off contaminated clothing removes most of the contamination; rinse with plenty of water if the skin itches or burns, and never put home remedies on the skin."}]},{id:22,icon:"FlaskConical",accent:"#7e22ce",title:"Poisoning at home",subtitle:"Safe storage, swallowed poison, chemical splashes and carbon monoxide",summary:"Medicines, cleaning products, kerosene, petrol and pesticides are found in most homes, and each of them can poison a person. Young children are most at risk, because they put things in their mouths and cannot read labels. This lesson explains how to store these products safely, what to do if someone swallows something harmful, how to rinse chemicals from the eyes and skin, and how to act when carbon monoxide (CO) is suspected.",blocks:[{heading:"Store dangerous products safely",body:"Safe storage is the best protection:",points:["Keep medicines, cleaning products and pesticides locked away or high up, out of children's sight and reach.","Keep every product in its original container with its label, so that it is never mistaken for a drink.","Never put kerosene, petrol, chemicals or medicines in drink bottles, cups or food containers.","Close the container after each use and put it away at once; do not leave it on the floor or a table, even for a moment.","Never mix cleaning products with each other (for example, bleach with another cleaner): the mixture can release dangerous fumes."]},{heading:"If someone swallows something harmful",body:`1. Take the product away from the person and keep it away from children.
2. Call 122 at once, even if the person seems well: some poisons cause harm only later. Keep the container or label to show the ambulance team and the doctors.
3. Do not make the person vomit.
4. Do not give milk, oil, salt water or other home remedies, and give nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor tells you to.
5. If the person becomes drowsy or unconscious but is breathing normally, lay them on their side (recovery position) and keep checking their breathing. If they stop breathing normally, start CPR (see the lesson "First aid and CPR").`},{heading:"Kerosene, petrol and lamp oil",body:`These liquids are especially dangerous if swallowed. They are thin and slippery and can easily go down into the lungs, even in a small amount, and cause serious breathing problems and pneumonia. That is why making the person vomit is especially dangerous. Coughing, choking or difficult breathing after swallowing them can mean that the liquid has reached the lungs: call 122 at once.

• Buy and keep fuel only in proper fuel containers, locked away from children.`},{heading:"Chemicals in the eyes or on the skin",points:["Protect yourself first: do not touch the chemical with bare hands, and wear gloves if you have them.","Eyes: rinse at once with plenty of clean running water for at least 15 minutes, holding the eyelids open. Do not let the water run into the other eye.","Skin: brush off any dry powder, take off soiled clothing, watches and jewelry, then rinse the skin with plenty of running water for at least 15 minutes.","Call 122 for advice and keep the product label. Call at once if the person has trouble breathing, feels unwell or a large area of the body was affected."]},{heading:"Carbon monoxide (CO)",body:`CO is a poisonous gas with no color and no smell. It comes from heaters, stoves, generators and engines that burn fuel. Headache, dizziness, nausea, weakness or confusion near such equipment can be warning signs.

• Get everyone out into fresh air at once and call 115 and 122 from outside.
• Do not go back inside until you are told that it is safe.
• Never run a generator or a car engine inside a house or garage, even with the doors open, and install a CO alarm near the bedrooms.`}],tips:["Write 122 (ambulance) and 115 (Civil Defense) next to your phone, and teach them to everyone in the family.","Walk through your home at a small child's eye level and remove anything poisonous that a child could reach.","When you call 122 about a poisoning, have the container in your hand so that you can read the product name.","Read the label before you use any cleaning product, and follow its instructions.","Keep children away while you use cleaning products, pesticides or fuel."],warnings:["Never make a person who has swallowed poison vomit, and never give them milk, oil or salt water.","Never store kerosene, petrol or chemicals in drink bottles: a child may drink them.","Do not go into a room full of fumes or suspected carbon monoxide to rescue someone; get out and call 115."],activity:{prompt:"Your three-year-old nephew is coughing and crying. Next to him is an open drink bottle in which the family kept kerosene for the heater. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take the bottle away, call 122 at once and keep the bottle to show the ambulance team. I do not make him vomit and I give him nothing to drink.",correct:!0,feedback:"Coughing after swallowing kerosene can mean that it reached the lungs. Calling 122 quickly, keeping the bottle and not causing vomiting protect the child."},{text:"I put my finger in his throat to make him vomit the kerosene out.",correct:!1,feedback:"Vomiting is especially dangerous with kerosene, because the liquid can go into the lungs. Call 122 instead."},{text:"I give him a glass of milk to weaken the poison.",correct:!1,feedback:"Milk does not make kerosene harmless and may make him vomit. Give nothing by mouth unless a doctor says so; call 122."},{text:"I wait to see whether the coughing stops before I call anyone.",correct:!1,feedback:"Coughing is a warning sign that kerosene may be in the lungs, and waiting wastes time. Call 122 now."}]},quiz:[{question:"Where should medicines and cleaning products be kept?",options:[{text:"Locked away or high up, in their original containers",correct:!0},{text:"Under the kitchen sink, where the whole family can find them quickly",correct:!1},{text:"In clean drink bottles, so that they take up less space",correct:!1},{text:"On a low bathroom shelf, with the caps left loose",correct:!1}],explanation:"Locked or high storage and the original labeled container keep children away from these products and stop anyone from mistaking them for a drink."},{question:"Someone has swallowed a cleaning product. Which action is correct?",options:[{text:"Call 122, keep the container and give nothing by mouth",correct:!0},{text:"Make them vomit at once, so that the product leaves the stomach quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Give them a glass of milk or oil to protect the stomach from the product",correct:!1},{text:"Give them salt water to drink, then wait to see whether they feel better",correct:!1}],explanation:"Vomiting and home remedies can cause more harm. Call 122, keep the container and give nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor tells you to."},{question:"Why is swallowed kerosene or petrol especially dangerous?",options:[{text:"It can slip into the lungs and cause serious breathing problems",correct:!0},{text:"It harms only the stomach, so the person is safe once they have vomited",correct:!1},{text:"It is dangerous only when a whole cup is swallowed, never a small sip",correct:!1},{text:"It becomes harmless after a few minutes, once it mixes with water",correct:!1}],explanation:"Kerosene and petrol can go down into the lungs, even in small amounts, and vomiting makes this more likely. Call 122 at once."},{question:"A chemical has splashed into someone's eye. How long should you rinse it with running water?",options:[{text:"At least 15 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"About 30 seconds",correct:!1},{text:"One quick splash is enough",correct:!1},{text:"Only until it stops stinging",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rinse at once with plenty of running water for at least 15 minutes, holding the eyelids open, then call 122 for advice."},{question:"While a heater is running indoors, several family members have a headache and feel dizzy. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Get everyone out, then call 115 and 122 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"Give everyone a painkiller and let them rest in the same warm room",correct:!1},{text:"Open one window a little and keep the heater running all night",correct:!1},{text:"Turn the heater up so the room warms quickly and the dizziness passes",correct:!1}],explanation:"These can be signs of carbon monoxide poisoning. CO has no color or smell: get everyone out first, call from outside and do not go back in until you are told it is safe."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_poisoning_home.webp",title:"Safe storage at home",description:"Medicines and cleaning products locked away in their original containers, out of children's reach"}]},{id:23,icon:"Ambulance",accent:"#be123c",title:"Medical emergencies: recognize and call",subtitle:"Stroke, heart attack, seizure, low blood sugar, severe allergy, shock and unconsciousness",summary:"In a sudden illness, the most important things a citizen can do are to recognize the warning signs, call 122 at once and stay with the person. This lesson teaches the signs of a stroke (FAST), a heart attack, a seizure, low blood sugar, a severe allergic reaction and shock, what to do for an unconscious person, and what to say when you call 122. It does not replace a first aid course.",blocks:[{heading:"Stroke: think FAST",body:`A stroke happens when part of the brain suddenly stops getting enough blood. Treatment works best when it starts early, so act at once:
• F = Face: ask the person to smile. Does one side of the face droop?
• A = Arms: ask them to raise both arms. Does one arm drift down or feel weak?
• S = Speech: is their speech slurred or strange, or do they not understand you?
• T = Time: if you see any of these signs, call 122 at once.

Note the time the signs began, or when the person was last seen well, and tell the ambulance team. Call even if the signs go away. Do not give food or drink, because a stroke can make swallowing difficult.`},{heading:"Heart attack",body:`Warning signs can include chest pain, pressure or squeezing; pain that spreads to an arm, the neck, the jaw or the back; shortness of breath; sweating; and nausea.
• Call 122 at once. Let the ambulance take the person to hospital; do not let them drive.
• Stop all activity: help the person sit or rest in the position that is most comfortable for them, and stay with them.
• If they become unresponsive and stop breathing normally, start CPR and send someone to bring an AED.`},{heading:"Seizure",body:`During a seizure the person may fall, stiffen and jerk, or stare and not respond. Most seizures stop by themselves within a few minutes.
• Stay with the person and note the time the seizure starts.
• Move hard or sharp objects away and put something soft and flat, such as a folded jacket, under their head.
• Do not hold them down and put nothing in their mouth. Give no food, drink or medicine until they are fully awake.
• When the jerking stops, turn them onto their side (recovery position) if no neck or back injury is suspected, and check their breathing.

Call 122 if:`,points:["It is the person's first seizure.","It lasts more than 5 minutes, or another seizure starts before the person recovers.","It happens in water, or the person is injured or has trouble breathing.","The person is pregnant, or is a baby under 6 months old.","The person does not wake up properly within 5–10 minutes after the seizure stops."]},{heading:"Low blood sugar, severe allergy and shock",points:["Low blood sugar (often in a person with diabetes): sudden confusion, shaking, sweating or strange behavior. If the person is awake and able to swallow, give them sugar, sweets or a sweet drink. Call 122 if they cannot swallow, have a seizure or are not better within 10 minutes.","Severe allergic reaction (anaphylaxis): swelling of the lips, face or throat, difficulty breathing, wheezing, a rash, dizziness or fainting, often minutes after a food, a medicine or an insect sting. Call 122 at once. If the person has their own adrenaline auto-injector, help them use it as its instructions show.","In a severe allergic reaction, let the person sit up if breathing is hard; otherwise let them lie down. Do not let them stand up or walk, even if they feel better.","Shock (after serious bleeding, burns or a severe illness): pale, cold and clammy skin, a fast heartbeat, dizziness or confusion. Call 122, lay the person on their back, keep them warm and give nothing to eat or drink. If they vomit or become drowsy, turn them onto their side unless a neck or back injury is suspected."]},{heading:"Unconscious: is the person breathing normally?",body:'Tap their shoulders and ask loudly: "Are you all right?" If there is no response, call 122 (or have someone else call) and look at the chest for no more than 10 seconds.',points:["Breathing normally, and no fall, crash or blow to the head, neck or back: put the person on their side in the recovery position, give nothing by mouth and keep checking their breathing.","Breathing normally, but a neck, back, hip or pelvis injury is possible: do not move them unless the place is unsafe; keep them still and keep watching their breathing.",'Not breathing, or only gasping: start CPR at once and send someone for an AED (see the lesson "First aid and CPR").']},{heading:"Calling 122: what to say",body:`Stay calm and answer the call-taker's questions:
1. Where you are: area, street, a nearby landmark, building and floor.
2. What happened and what you see, for example: "A man of about 60; one side of his face is drooping."
3. How many people are ill or hurt, and whether there is any danger.
4. Your name and phone number.

Do not hang up until you are told to. Stay with the person, and keep their medicines and medical papers ready for the ambulance team.`}],tips:["Learn FAST and teach it to your family: face, arms, speech, time.","Take a first aid and CPR course, and renew it every few years.","If someone in your family has diabetes, epilepsy, a heart condition or a severe allergy, learn their emergency plan and where they keep their medicines and devices.","Keep a written list of each family member's illnesses, medicines and allergies, and give it to the ambulance team.","Stay with the person until the ambulance team takes over."],warnings:["Never put anything in the mouth of a person who is having a seizure or who is not fully awake.","Do not give medicines on your own; only help a person use their own prescribed adrenaline auto-injector or inhaler.","Do not wait to see whether the signs of a stroke or a heart attack go away: call 122 at once."],activity:{prompt:"At a family gathering, your 62-year-old uncle suddenly cannot lift his right arm and his speech is slurred. He says he only needs to lie down for a while. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I call 122 at once, tell them what I see and the time the signs began, and stay with him until the ambulance arrives.",correct:!0,feedback:"Arm weakness and slurred speech are stroke signs (FAST). Treatment works best when it starts early, and the time the signs began helps the doctors."},{text:"I let him lie down and rest, and we call a doctor tomorrow if he is not better.",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting loses time: a stroke needs treatment as early as possible. Call 122 now, even if he feels better."},{text:"I drive him to a pharmacy so that he can buy a medicine for blood pressure.",correct:!1,feedback:"A pharmacy cannot treat a stroke, and medicines taken without a doctor may harm him. Call 122 so that the ambulance takes him to hospital."},{text:"I give him sweet tea and wait half an hour to see whether his speech improves.",correct:!1,feedback:"Sweet tea does not treat a stroke, and waiting loses time that matters. Call 122 at once."}]},quiz:[{question:"What does the T in FAST mean?",options:[{text:"Time: call 122 at once and note when the signs began",correct:!0},{text:"Temperature: check first whether the person has a fever",correct:!1},{text:"Tablets: give the person their usual medicine first",correct:!1},{text:"Tea: give the person a warm drink to calm them down",correct:!1}],explanation:"FAST stands for face, arms, speech and time. Time means calling 122 at once; the time the signs began helps the doctors choose the treatment."},{question:"A person is having a seizure on the floor. What is correct?",options:[{text:"Move hard objects away, time it and put nothing in their mouth",correct:!0},{text:"Hold their arms and legs firmly so that the jerking stops sooner",correct:!1},{text:"Put a spoon between their teeth so that they do not bite their tongue",correct:!1},{text:"Pour cold water on their face to wake them and stop the seizure",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not hold the person down or put anything in their mouth. Protect them from injury, time the seizure and turn them on their side when the jerking stops."},{question:"When must you call 122 for a seizure?",options:[{text:"If it lasts more than 5 minutes or it is the person's first seizure",correct:!0},{text:"Only if the person asks you to call once the seizure has ended",correct:!1},{text:"Never, because seizures always stop by themselves without help",correct:!1},{text:"Only if the seizure goes on for longer than half an hour without stopping",correct:!1}],explanation:"Call 122 for a first seizure, a seizure longer than 5 minutes, repeated seizures, a seizure in water, an injury, breathing trouble, pregnancy, a baby under 6 months, or if the person does not wake up properly."},{question:"A person with diabetes is confused and sweating but is awake and able to swallow. What helps?",options:[{text:"Give them sugar or a sweet drink; call 122 if they do not improve",correct:!0},{text:"Give them an extra dose of their insulin to bring their sugar back",correct:!1},{text:"Let them sleep it off in a quiet room without anything to eat",correct:!1},{text:"Pour a sweet drink into their mouth even if they become drowsy",correct:!1}],explanation:"Low blood sugar is treated with sugar by mouth only if the person is awake and can swallow. Call 122 if they cannot swallow, have a seizure or are not better within 10 minutes."},{question:"An unconscious person is breathing normally, and there is no sign of a fall or a blow to the neck or back. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Call 122, turn them on their side and keep checking breathing",correct:!0},{text:"Sit them up and give them water so that they wake up more quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Start chest compressions at once, even though they are breathing",correct:!1},{text:"Leave them alone on their back and go to look for their family",correct:!1}],explanation:"The recovery position keeps the airway open in an unconscious person who breathes normally and has no suspected neck, back, hip or pelvis injury. Give nothing by mouth; if breathing stops, start CPR."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_medical_emergencies.webp",title:"Stroke signs: FAST",description:"Face, arms, speech, time: recognize the signs and call 122"}]},{id:24,icon:"Handshake",accent:"#0369a1",title:"Evacuating people who need help",subtitle:"Children, older people, people with disabilities, pregnant women and people who are ill",summary:"In a fire, an earthquake or a flood, some people cannot get out quickly or on their own: young children, older people, people with disabilities, pregnant women and people who are ill. Their safety depends on a plan made before the emergency. This lesson explains how to make a personal evacuation plan, how to communicate with people who are deaf or blind, why ordinary elevators are not used in a fire, and how to help without putting yourself or the person in danger.",blocks:[{heading:"Who may need help?",body:"Think about everyone in your home, your building and your street who may not be able to get out quickly or alone:",points:["Babies and young children, who must be woken and taken out by an adult.","Older people who walk slowly, use a walker or cannot hear the alarm well.","People who use a wheelchair, crutches or a cane, and people with a visual, hearing, intellectual or mental health disability.","Pregnant women, people who are ill or recovering from an operation, and anyone who depends on medicines or medical equipment."]},{heading:"A personal evacuation plan",body:"Make the plan before an emergency, together with the person:",points:["The person knows their own abilities and needs best: ask them, and plan with their family and the building manager.","Choose a helper (a buddy) and a backup helper for each person, and make sure both agree to this role.","Write down the person's needs: medicines, medical devices, mobility aids, how they communicate and whom to call.","At night, keep glasses, hearing aids, the walking aid, a phone and a flashlight next to the bed.","If possible, the person sleeps on the ground floor, close to an exit.","Practice the plan together regularly."]},{heading:"Communicating with people who are deaf or blind",points:["Deaf or hard of hearing: the person may not hear the alarm or a shout. Go right up to them and get their attention with a wave or a flashing light, then face them and speak clearly without shouting.","Explain with simple gestures, point to the exit or write a short note. In the dark, shine a light on your face and hands so that they can see you.","Blind or low vision: say who you are and what is happening, and ask how you can help. Do not grab the person or push them.","Offer your arm and let them hold it just above the elbow. Pause before steps, curbs and doorways and say what is ahead.","At the assembly point, tell them where they are and who is with them."]},{heading:"Elevators and refuge areas",points:["Never use an ordinary elevator in a fire. When the fire alarm sounds, elevators usually go to one floor and can then be operated only by firefighters.","A person who cannot use the stairs waits with their helper at the safe place named in the building's emergency plan, such as a refuge area, away from the fire and smoke.","If you cannot reach a person who needs help, get out and call 115. Tell the firefighters at once where the person is waiting: the floor, the room and how many people.","Agree on these arrangements in advance with the building manager, and follow the instructions of Civil Defense."]},{heading:"Helping without danger",points:["Ask before you help, and follow the person's own instructions on how to help them.","Do not lift or carry a person if you are not trained, unless there is immediate danger to life: you can injure them and yourself.","Do not move a wheelchair, walker or cane away from the person; take the mobility aid along if you can.","Take the person's medicines and devices only if they are at hand; never go back inside for them.","At the assembly point, stay with the person and tell whoever is counting people that they are safe."]}],tips:["Make a list of the neighbors in your building who may need help, and agree with them who will help whom.","Keep a copy of each person's needs list in your emergency kit.","Smoke alarms with a flashing light or a vibrating pad help people who are deaf or hard of hearing.","If someone uses a walker or a wheelchair, check that they can get through every exit door of your home.","Include the person who needs help in your drills, so that the plan really fits them."],warnings:["Never use an ordinary elevator during a fire.","Never go back into a burning building to rescue someone: tell the firefighters exactly where the person is."],activity:{prompt:"The fire alarm sounds in your apartment building. Your neighbor on the fourth floor is an older woman who uses a walker and cannot manage the stairs quickly. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell her what is happening, help her at her own pace down the stairs or to the agreed safe waiting place, and tell the firefighters exactly where she is.",correct:!0,feedback:"Helping her follow the plan and telling the firefighters exactly where she is gets trained rescuers to her quickly, without putting either of you at extra risk."},{text:"I take her down in the elevator, because it is faster than the stairs.",correct:!1,feedback:"Ordinary elevators must not be used in a fire: they go to one floor and are then controlled by firefighters. Use the stairs or the agreed waiting place."},{text:"I leave without her, because rescuing people is only the firefighters' job.",correct:!1,feedback:"Leaving her without telling anyone delays her rescue. Warn her, help as your plan says, and tell the firefighters where she is."},{text:"I lift her onto my back by myself and hurry down the stairs.",correct:!1,feedback:"Lifting her without training can injure you both, and a fall on the stairs blocks the way for others. Carry a person only if you are trained or there is immediate danger to life."}]},quiz:[{question:"Who should make a person's personal evacuation plan?",options:[{text:"The person, with their family, helpers and the building manager",correct:!0},{text:"Only the building manager, without asking the person what they need",correct:!1},{text:"Only the firefighters, on the day that an emergency actually happens",correct:!1},{text:"Nobody: it is better to decide what to do when the alarm goes off",correct:!1}],explanation:"The person knows their own needs best, so the plan is made with them, their family, their helpers and the building manager, and it is practiced before an emergency."},{question:"The fire alarm sounds. How do you get the attention of a neighbor who is deaf?",options:[{text:"Go up to them, wave or flash a light and face them",correct:!0},{text:"Shout loudly from the end of the corridor until they hear you",correct:!1},{text:"Grab their arm from behind and pull them quickly toward the exit",correct:!1},{text:"Send a message to their phone and carry on toward the exit alone",correct:!1}],explanation:"A deaf person may not hear the alarm or a shout. Go right up to them, get their attention, face them and explain with gestures or a written note."},{question:"How do you guide a person who is blind out of a building?",options:[{text:"Say who you are, offer your arm and describe steps and obstacles",correct:!0},{text:"Hold both of their shoulders and steer them from behind toward the exit",correct:!1},{text:"Take their hand and pull them along quickly without saying anything",correct:!1},{text:"Point toward the exit and tell them to find the way on their own",correct:!1}],explanation:"Say who you are and ask how to help. Let the person hold your arm just above the elbow, and say what is ahead, such as steps, curbs and doorways."},{question:"Why must ordinary elevators not be used in a fire?",options:[{text:"In a fire they go to one floor and only firefighters control them",correct:!0},{text:"They are fine to use as long as the fire is on another floor",correct:!1},{text:"They are safe for wheelchair users but not for anyone else in a fire",correct:!1},{text:"They may be used if someone holds the doors open with a chair",correct:!1}],explanation:"Ordinary elevators are not used in an emergency unless they are specially designed for it. When the fire alarm sounds they usually go to a set floor and only firefighters can operate them. A person who cannot use the stairs waits at the agreed place, and the firefighters are told where they are."},{question:"You are not trained to carry people, and there is no immediate danger. A man in a wheelchair cannot use the stairs. What is best?",options:[{text:"Help him to the agreed place and tell firefighters where he is",correct:!0},{text:"Lift him out of the chair and carry him down the stairs on your own",correct:!1},{text:"Leave him in the corridor and go outside without telling anybody",correct:!1},{text:"Take him down in the elevator, because the stairs are too narrow",correct:!1}],explanation:"Lifting without training can injure both of you. Unless there is immediate danger, help him to the agreed safe place and make sure the firefighters know exactly where he is."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_assisted_evacuation.webp",title:"Helping a neighbor evacuate",description:"A helper guides a blind man by the arm, while a wheelchair user waits with a buddy at the refuge area"}]},{id:25,icon:"TrafficCone",accent:"#ea580c",title:"At a crash or vehicle fire",subtitle:"Protect yourself first, then call and help safely",summary:"Most crashes do not end in fire, but a crash scene is still dangerous: moving traffic, leaking fuel and smoke can hurt the people who stop to help. This lesson shows how to protect yourself first, how to call 115 and 122 with a location the teams can find, how to help injured people without making their injuries worse and what to do if a vehicle catches fire.",blocks:[{heading:"Protect yourself first",points:["Stop only where it is safe, off the road if you can, and do not block the way for ambulances and fire engines.","Switch on your hazard lights, switch off your engine and ask other drivers to switch off theirs.","Nobody smokes or lights a flame near the vehicles: fuel and most other vehicle fluids catch fire easily.","Put on a high-visibility vest if you have one, and never stand between the vehicles and oncoming traffic.","If you carry a warning triangle, place it only where you can do so without walking into traffic. The local rules on its use and distance are set by the traffic authorities.","Move uninjured people, especially children, away from the vehicles and the road to a safer place."]},{heading:"Call 115 and 122",points:["Call at once: after a crash, minutes can make the difference between life and death. Call 115 for fire, trapped people or leaking fuel, 122 for injured people, and both when both are needed.","Where: the name of the road or the two towns it connects, your direction of travel and a landmark nearby, such as a bridge, a gas station, a junction or a road sign.","If you are not sure where you are, read the nearest road signs or use the location shown on your phone's map.","What: how many vehicles, how many people are hurt or trapped, and any danger such as fire, smoke, leaking fuel or a truck with hazard plates.","Give your name and phone number and do not hang up until you are told to. If no one is hurt and there is no fire, leak or other danger, report the crash to the traffic police on 104."]},{heading:"Help the injured without making things worse",points:["Do not move an injured person unless they are in immediate danger, such as fire, heavy smoke or traffic that cannot be stopped: after a crash, the neck or back may be injured.","If the person is awake, talk to them calmly, ask them to keep their head and neck still and tell them that help is coming.","Do not remove a motorcyclist's helmet. It comes off only if the person is not breathing or their airway is blocked, and then only by trained people, ideally two, who keep the head and neck still.","Press firmly on heavy bleeding with a clean cloth and follow the instructions of the 122 call-taker.","Stay with the injured until the teams arrive, and then follow their instructions."]},{heading:"If a vehicle catches fire",points:["Warning signs: smoke or flames, or a smell of burning rubber or plastic. Act at once.","If you are driving, pull over as soon as it is safe and switch off the engine.","Get everyone out and move at least 30 meters (100 feet) from the burning vehicle, well away from traffic and out of the smoke, upwind if you can.","Never go back to a burning vehicle, not for a phone, a bag or documents.","Do not open the hood or the trunk if you think the fire is inside: fresh air can make the fire grow.","Do not try to fight the fire yourself. Call 115 from a safe distance."]},{heading:"Leaking fuel and dangerous loads",points:["Fuel vapor can catch fire from a spark, a cigarette or a hot engine: no smoking, no flames and engines off.","Keep people away from leaking fuel and do not walk through it.","A truck or tanker with orange plates or hazard signs: stay well away, do not try to rescue people from it yourself, tell 115 what the signs show and do not use your phone close to it.","Do not crowd around the scene or film the injured, and if you are only passing, keep your eyes on the road."]}],tips:["Keep a high-visibility vest, a flashlight and a first aid kit in your car.","Always notice the name of the road, your direction and the landmarks you pass.","Have your vehicle serviced regularly by a trained mechanic and get any fluid leak checked.","Never carry fuel cans or gas cylinders inside the passenger compartment.","Take a first aid course so that you know what to do while you wait for help."],warnings:["A burning vehicle can trap people quickly: get out at once and never go back.","Do not move injured people unless they are in immediate danger, and do not remove a motorcyclist's helmet.","Smoking, flames and running engines near leaking fuel can start a fire."],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_crash_scene_safety.webp",title:"Safe help at a crash scene",description:"A driver has stopped off the road with hazard lights on and calls for help, while everyone waits well away from a smoking car."}],activity:{prompt:"At night on a road between two towns, the car in front of you hits the side barrier. Smoke starts coming from under its hood. The driver is awake and is trying to open the door. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop safely behind it, off the road, with hazard lights on and my engine off, help the driver get out and away because of the smoke, call 115 and 122, and we wait at least 30 meters from the car.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: smoke means immediate danger, so helping the awake driver out is justified. You protect yourself first, call both numbers and keep everyone at least 30 meters away."},{text:"I open the hood to see where the smoke is coming from, so that I can tell 115 exactly what is burning.",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening the hood lets fresh air in, and the fire can grow into your face. Keep it closed, get everyone away and call 115."},{text:"I stop right next to the car in the same lane and leave my engine running, so that we can drive away quickly.",correct:!1,feedback:"Stopping in the lane puts you in the path of other traffic, and a running engine near a smoking car is one more source of ignition. Stop off the road with your engine off."},{text:"I wait a few minutes to see whether the smoke stops before I call, so that I do not make a false call.",correct:!1,feedback:"A real crash with smoke is never a false call. Minutes matter after a crash, so call 115 and 122 at once."}]},quiz:[{question:"A car is on fire. How far away should everyone move?",options:[{text:"At least 30 meters (100 feet), away from traffic",correct:!0},{text:"About 5 meters, close enough to watch the fire",correct:!1},{text:"Right beside the car, to take the bags out first",correct:!1},{text:"In the middle of the road, where drivers can see you",correct:!1}],explanation:"Fire safety guidance (USFA and NFPA) advises moving everyone at least 30 meters (100 feet) from a burning vehicle and well away from traffic. Never go back for belongings."},{question:"Smoke is coming from under a car's hood. What should you do with the hood?",options:[{text:"Keep it closed: air would make the fire grow",correct:!0},{text:"Open it wide so that the smoke can get out faster",correct:!1},{text:"Lift it a little and pour water onto the engine",correct:!1},{text:"Open it to check the engine before calling anyone",correct:!1}],explanation:"USFA advises: if you think there is a fire under the hood or in the trunk, do not open it, because fresh air could make the fire grow. Get away and call 115."},{question:"When may you move an injured person out of a crashed car?",options:[{text:"Only if they are in immediate danger, such as fire or traffic",correct:!0},{text:"Always at once, so that they can lie flat on the ground beside the car",correct:!1},{text:"Whenever there are two strong people to lift them",correct:!1},{text:"When the ambulance has not arrived after ten minutes",correct:!1}],explanation:"After a crash the neck or back may be injured. First aid guidelines say not to move an injured person unless the place is unsafe, for example because of fire, smoke or traffic."},{question:"A motorcyclist lies on the road after a crash. He is awake and breathing. What do you do about his helmet?",options:[{text:"Leave it on and ask him to keep his head still",correct:!0},{text:"Take it off at once so that he can breathe better",correct:!1},{text:"Loosen it and turn his head to check his neck",correct:!1},{text:"Pull it off quickly before the ambulance arrives",correct:!1}],explanation:"The helmet stays on while the rider is breathing. It is removed only if he is not breathing or his airway is blocked, and then by trained people, ideally two, who keep the head and neck still."},{question:"What does the 115 or 122 team need to hear from you first?",options:[{text:"The road, your direction and a nearby landmark",correct:!0},{text:"The make, model and color of every car involved",correct:!1},{text:"Your opinion about who caused the crash and why",correct:!1},{text:"The hospital you would like the injured taken to",correct:!1}],explanation:"Teams can only help if they find you. Give the location first, then what happened, how many are hurt, any dangers and your phone number, and do not hang up until told."}]},{id:26,icon:"Waves",accent:"#0e7490",title:"Floods for families",subtitle:"Warnings, flash floods, leaving in time and returning home safely",summary:"Floods and flash floods can come quickly, sometimes with little or no warning, and most people who die in floods drown. This lesson helps your family get ready, act on warnings, stay out of floodwater, leave in time and return home safely.",blocks:[{heading:"Warnings and getting ready",points:["Follow official announcements from Civil Defense and the authorities on radio, television and their official pages, and do not act on rumors.","Ask Civil Defense and your local authorities how flood warnings and evacuation orders are announced where you live.","Make a family plan: where you will go on higher ground, which way you will go and how you will contact each other.","Prepare an emergency kit: drinking water, food that does not spoil, medicines, a flashlight, a radio, batteries, a first aid kit and copies of important documents in a waterproof bag.","Plan for children, older people, people with disabilities and anyone who needs help to move."]},{heading:"Flash floods in valleys and dry riverbeds",points:["A flash flood usually comes within 6 hours of heavy rain, sometimes within minutes, and the rain may have fallen far away from you.","A dry riverbed or valley can fill with fast water in minutes, even when it is not raining where you are.","Do not camp, picnic or park in low ground next to a stream or in a dry riverbed when rain is forecast or storms are near.","If the water starts to rise, climb to higher ground at once: walk up, do not try to drive out.","At night a flood is hard to see coming, so take warnings seriously before dark."]},{heading:"Turn around, don't drown",points:["Never walk, swim or drive into floodwater: 15 cm of moving water can knock an adult down and 30 cm can carry most cars away.","Turn around and find another way: the road under the water may have collapsed. Never drive around barriers that close a flooded road.","Stay off bridges over fast-moving water.","Floodwater can hide holes, debris and electrical dangers, and it is often contaminated.","If someone is in the water, do not go in after them: call 115 and 122, then reach out with a pole or rope or throw something that floats from a safe place."]},{heading:"When and how to leave",points:["Leave at once if the authorities tell you to, and leave early enough not to be trapped by the water.","Take your emergency kit, follow the recommended routes and do not take shortcuts: they may be blocked or flooded.","Switch off the electricity before leaving only if you are told to and can do it safely. Never touch switches or electrical equipment while you are wet or standing in water.","Gas: if you smell gas, do not touch any switch; go out, warn others and call 115 from outside. Close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected.","If water traps you inside a building, go to the highest safe level and call 115 with your exact location. Do not go into a closed room under the roof where the water could trap you; go onto the roof only if you must."]},{heading:"Returning home safely",points:["Return only when the authorities say it is safe. If the house is damaged, do not go in until a building expert has checked it.","Keep the electricity off until an electrician has checked the house. If you would have to step into water to reach the main switch, call an electrician instead.","Do not touch electrical equipment if it is wet or if you are standing in water.","Follow local advice on whether the tap water is safe to drink, and throw away any food that may have touched floodwater: when in doubt, throw it out.","Air out and dry the house. If it stayed closed and wet for several days, expect mold, and wear rubber boots and gloves when you clean.","Run a generator only outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from doors, windows and vents, and never inside the house, even with the doors open."]}],tips:["Find out whether your home, work or school is in a low area or near a valley that can flood.","Agree on a meeting place on higher ground and a relative outside the area whom everyone can contact.","When heavy rain is forecast, keep your phone charged and a battery radio ready.","Check on older neighbors and people with disabilities before and after a flood.","Practice your family plan together."],warnings:["Never walk, swim or drive into floodwater: turn around and find another way.","Electricity and water together can kill: never touch switches or electrical equipment while you are wet or in water.","Children must never play in floodwater, streams or drainage channels after rain."],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_flood_family_higher_ground.webp",title:"Families move to higher ground",description:"A family with an emergency bag walks up a hill, away from a valley stream that is rising after rain."}],activity:{prompt:"Heavy rain fell in the mountains all night. Your family is having a picnic in a valley next to a dry riverbed, and it is not raining where you are. Suddenly the riverbed starts to fill with muddy water. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We leave our things and climb at once on foot to higher ground away from the riverbed, then call 115 if anyone is in danger.",correct:!0,feedback:"A flash flood can rise within minutes. Leaving everything and walking up to higher ground at once is the safest choice; belongings can be replaced, lives cannot."},{text:"We quickly pack everything into the car and drive out along the road at the bottom of the valley.",correct:!1,feedback:"Packing wastes minutes, and the valley road may flood first: 30 cm of moving water can carry a car away. Walk up, do not drive out."},{text:"We wait and watch, because it is not raining here, so the water will not rise much.",correct:!1,feedback:"Flash floods often come from rain that fell far away. A dry riverbed can become a torrent within minutes, so go to higher ground now."},{text:"We cross the riverbed quickly to the other side before the water gets deep.",correct:!1,feedback:"Never walk into moving water: just 15 cm can knock an adult down. Climb to higher ground on your own side."}]},quiz:[{question:"How deep does moving floodwater need to be to knock an adult down?",options:[{text:"About 15 cm",correct:!0},{text:"About 1 meter",correct:!1},{text:"About 50 cm",correct:!1},{text:"Only above the waist",correct:!1}],explanation:"The US National Weather Service (NWS) warns that about 15 cm (6 inches) of fast-moving water can knock over an adult and about 30 cm (12 inches) can carry away most cars."},{question:"The road ahead is covered with moving water. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Turn around and take another route",correct:!0},{text:"Drive through slowly in a low gear",correct:!1},{text:"Walk across first to test the depth",correct:!1},{text:"Follow the car in front through it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Turn around, don't drown: you cannot see how deep or fast the water is, or whether the road under it has collapsed. Never walk or drive into floodwater."},{question:"Before you leave home in a flood, when should you switch off the electricity?",options:[{text:"Only if you are told to and can stay dry while doing it",correct:!0},{text:"Always, even if you must stand in water to reach it",correct:!1},{text:"Never: leave every appliance on so the house stays lit",correct:!1},{text:"Only once water has already covered the floor",correct:!1}],explanation:"Switch off the power before leaving only if you are told to and can do it safely. Never touch switches or electrical equipment while you are wet or standing in water."},{question:"After a flood, when can your family go back home?",options:[{text:"When the authorities say it is safe",correct:!0},{text:"As soon as the rain has stopped",correct:!1},{text:"When the water outside is below your knees",correct:!1},{text:"The next morning, whatever the damage",correct:!1}],explanation:"Return only when the authorities say it is safe. A damaged house must be checked by a building expert, and the electricity by an electrician, before you use them."},{question:"Where may a generator run after a flood?",options:[{text:"Outdoors, at least 6 meters from doors and windows",correct:!0},{text:"In the garage, if the garage door is left open",correct:!1},{text:"Inside the house, right next to an open window",correct:!1},{text:"In the hallway, as long as all the doors are open",correct:!1}],explanation:"Generators give off carbon monoxide (CO), a poisonous gas with no color or smell. Run them only outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from doors, windows and vents, and never inside, even with the doors open."}]},{id:27,icon:"CloudLightning",accent:"#7c3aed",title:"Severe weather for the public",subtitle:"Heat, lightning, dust storms, strong wind, snow and ice",summary:"Severe weather can hurt people in a short time. In our region we face heat waves, thunderstorms with lightning, dust storms, strong winds, snow and ice. This lesson gives the main safe actions for each one. Always follow the weather warnings of the authorities and the instructions of Civil Defense.",blocks:[{heading:"Heat waves",points:["Older people, young children and people with long-term illnesses are at greater risk. Check on them often during a heat wave.","Drink water regularly through the day, even before you feel thirsty, and avoid hard work and sport in the hottest hours.","Heat exhaustion: heavy sweating, weakness, dizziness, headache, nausea and thirst. Move the person to a cool place, cool them with water and give sips of water if they are fully alert. Get medical help if they vomit, if the symptoms get worse or if they last more than an hour.","Heat stroke: confusion, strange behavior, slurred speech, a seizure or collapse in the heat; the skin may be hot and dry or still sweating. Call 122 and cool the person at once: the best way is cold water up to the neck for 15 minutes or until they are thinking clearly again, whichever comes first; otherwise use a cold shower, cold wet towels, ice packs and fanning. Give nothing to drink to a person who is confused.","Never leave a child, or anyone, in a parked car, not even for a minute: the inside can quickly become dangerously hot."]},{heading:"Lightning",points:["When thunder roars, go indoors: if you can hear thunder, lightning can reach you. There is no safe place outside during a thunderstorm.","Shelter in a solid building or in a car with a metal roof and the windows closed, and stay inside until 30 minutes after the last thunder.","If you cannot reach shelter, keep away from hilltops, open fields, lone tall trees, water and metal fences. A tent gives no protection.","Indoors, keep away from windows and doors, do not use corded phones and avoid showers and washing dishes until the storm has passed."]},{heading:"Dust storms",points:["At home, stay indoors and close windows and doors.","Infants, older people and people with asthma or other breathing problems need extra care: they stay indoors, and people with asthma follow their own asthma action plan and keep their own prescribed inhaler with them.","Driving: do not drive into a dust storm. If visibility drops, pull off the road as far as you can, stop, switch off all your lights, set the parking brake and take your foot off the brake pedal. Never stop on the road itself.","Your lights must be off while you are parked: drivers behind you may follow your lights and crash into you."]},{heading:"Strong winds",points:["Before the wind, tie down or bring in loose things on roofs, balconies and yards so that they do not blow into people or windows.","During the wind, go inside a sturdy building and move to an inner room away from windows.","Fallen power lines may be live: do not go near them or anything touching them, keep others away and call 115.","If you are driving, hold the wheel with both hands, slow down and keep your distance from trucks and buses."]},{heading:"Snow, ice and heating",points:["Heaters: keep fresh air coming into the room, install a CO alarm, never heat the house with a gas oven and never use a generator or a charcoal fire indoors.","Headache, dizziness, nausea or confusion near a heater can mean carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning: get everyone into fresh air at once and call 115 and 122 from outside.","On ice, wear shoes with a good grip, take short steps and walk slowly.","In snow and ice, avoid driving if you can. If you must drive, slow down, even if the road only looks wet, and keep blankets, water, a flashlight and a charged phone in the car."]}],tips:["Follow the weather forecast and the warnings of the authorities and Civil Defense.","In very hot or very cold weather, check on older neighbors and relatives often.","Secure loose objects on roofs and balconies as soon as strong wind is forecast.","Keep a flashlight, a battery radio and a charged phone ready for storms.","Have heaters and stoves checked and keep anything that can burn away from them."],warnings:["Never leave a child in a parked car, not even for a minute.","When thunder roars, go indoors: no place outside is safe.","Never run a generator or a charcoal fire indoors, and never heat a home with a gas oven."],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_severe_weather_actions.webp",title:"Safe actions in severe weather",description:"Four panels: cooling a person in the shade during a heat wave, a family going indoors as a thunderstorm comes, a car parked far off the road with its lights off in a dust storm and a person keeping away from a fallen power line."}],activity:{prompt:"On a very hot afternoon, your older neighbor has been working in his garden. Now he is confused, speaks strangely and his skin is very hot. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I call 122, move him into the shade or a cool room and start cooling him at once with cold water, wet towels and fanning, and I give him nothing to drink.",correct:!0,feedback:"Confusion in the heat means heat stroke, an emergency. Calling 122 and cooling him at once can save his life, and a confused person must not be given drinks."},{text:"I give him a big glass of cold water to drink and let him rest on a chair in the garden.",correct:!1,feedback:"A confused person must not be given drinks, and the garden keeps him in the heat. Call 122, move him to a cool place and cool him at once."},{text:"I help him to bed indoors so that he can sleep it off, and I check on him again in the evening.",correct:!1,feedback:"Heat stroke can kill if cooling is delayed. Do not leave him to sleep: call 122 and start cooling now."},{text:"I drive him to the hospital myself straight away, without stopping to cool him first.",correct:!1,feedback:"With heat stroke, cooling comes first and transport second. Call 122 and start cooling at once; the call-taker will guide you."}]},quiz:[{question:"During a thunderstorm, when should you go indoors?",options:[{text:"As soon as you hear thunder",correct:!0},{text:"Only when the rain gets heavy",correct:!1},{text:"Only when lightning is right overhead",correct:!1},{text:"Once the wind starts to blow hard",correct:!1}],explanation:"If you can hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck by lightning. When thunder roars, go indoors: there is no safe place outside during a thunderstorm."},{question:"After the last thunder, how long should you wait before going back outside?",options:[{text:"30 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"5 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Until the rain stops",correct:!1},{text:"There is no need to wait",correct:!1}],explanation:"Lightning can still strike after the rain eases. The US National Weather Service (NWS) advises waiting 30 minutes after the last lightning or thunder before going back outside."},{question:"While you are driving, a dust storm suddenly cuts visibility. What is the safest action?",options:[{text:"Pull far off the road, stop and switch off all lights",correct:!0},{text:"Stop in your lane and switch on the hazard lights",correct:!1},{text:"Speed up to get out of the dust as fast as you can",correct:!1},{text:"Follow the taillights of the car in front of you",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never stop on the road itself. Pull off as far as you can, switch off all lights, set the parking brake and keep your foot off the brake, so that drivers behind do not follow your lights into you (NWS, Pull aside, stay alive)."},{question:"Which of these points to heat stroke rather than heat exhaustion?",options:[{text:"Confusion or strange behavior in the heat",correct:!0},{text:"Thirst after a long day of working in the sun",correct:!1},{text:"Heavy sweating and feeling tired after work",correct:!1},{text:"A mild headache while sitting in a hot room",correct:!1}],explanation:"Confusion, strange behavior, a seizure or collapse in the heat mean heat stroke, a life-threatening emergency: call 122 and cool the person at once. Thirst, heavy sweating and a mild headache fit heat exhaustion."},{question:"After a storm you see a power line lying on the ground. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stay away, keep others away and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Move it aside with a dry wooden stick",correct:!1},{text:"Step over it carefully if it is not sparking",correct:!1},{text:"Drive slowly over it to get past",correct:!1}],explanation:"A fallen power line, and anything or any water touching it, may be live even if it is not sparking. Never touch it, move it or drive over it: keep everyone away and call 115."}]},{id:28,icon:"Truck",accent:"#4d7c0f",title:"Agriculture and rural safety",subtitle:"Pesticides, tractors and machinery, grain and hay, and dangers hidden in the soil",summary:"Farm work has dangers of its own: poisons used to protect crops, heavy machines that can overturn or catch clothing, stores of grain and hay that can bury a person or catch fire, and old explosive ordnance in the ground. Children who play or ride near the work are in special danger. This lesson explains how farming families and farm workers can protect themselves and what to do when something goes wrong.",blocks:[{heading:"Pesticides: store them safely",body:"Many poisonings happen because a pesticide was within reach or looked like a drink:",points:["Keep pesticides locked away, out of children's reach, and never with or near food, animal feed or medicines.","Keep every pesticide in its original container with its label. Never pour it into a drink bottle, a cup or a food container: someone may drink it.","Do not keep highly toxic pesticides or concentrates in the house; keep them in a separate locked store.","Never use empty pesticide containers for water, food or animal feed. Get rid of them as the label and the local authorities say.","Treated seed and rodent bait can look like ordinary grain: keep them locked away from children and animals."]},{heading:"Using pesticides safely",points:["Read the whole label before you open the container, and follow it exactly: the amount, the mixing, the protective equipment and how long people must stay out of the treated area.","Wear the protective clothing that the label asks for, such as waterproof gloves, long pants and long sleeves.","Keep children, other people and animals away while you mix and spray, and until the time on the label has passed.","After spraying, change your clothes and wash your hands at once. Do not leave work clothes or spraying equipment where children play."]},{heading:"If someone is poisoned by a pesticide",body:`Dizziness, tiredness, blurred vision, nausea, a dry throat, difficulty breathing, stinging eyes, itchy skin or weak muscles during or after work with pesticides can be signs of poisoning.

1. Protect yourself first: wear gloves and do not touch the pesticide with bare hands. Take the person away from it, into fresh air.
2. Call 122 at once, even if the person seems well, and keep the container or its label to show the ambulance team and the doctors.
3. If it was swallowed: do not make the person vomit, and give nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor tells you to.
4. On the skin or in the eyes: take off soiled clothing and rinse the skin, or the open eye, with plenty of running water for at least 15 minutes.
5. If the person is unconscious but breathing normally and has no injury to the neck, back, hip or pelvis, lay them on their side and keep checking their breathing. If they stop breathing normally, start CPR (see the lesson "Poisoning at home").`},{heading:"Tractors and machinery",body:"Overturns, people being run over and clothing caught in a turning power take-off (PTO) shaft cause most serious tractor injuries.",points:["Use a tractor with a rollover protective structure (a roll bar or safety cab, ROPS) and always fasten the seat belt: together they give the driver the best chance of surviving an overturn. A qualified dealer can fit a frame to many older tractors.","Keep away from ditches, banks and holes, stay off slopes that are too steep, and slow down when you turn and on rough, slippery or muddy ground.","Keep every guard on the PTO shaft and the machines in place. Tie back long hair and do not wear loose clothing near moving parts.","Before you clean, adjust or unblock a machine, stop the engine and wait until the shaft and every moving part have stopped.","Nobody rides on a tractor unless it has a seat and a seat belt for that person. Children never ride on tractors or machines and never play near them.","Before you start the engine or the PTO, make sure that everyone, especially children, is well away from the machine."]},{heading:"Grain, hay and straw",points:["Never go into a grain bin, silo or trailer while grain is moving or being emptied: flowing grain acts like quicksand and can bury a person in seconds. Never walk on grain to make it flow.","Grain that has formed a crust or a steep pile can collapse suddenly on anyone standing on it or next to it.","Children never play in grain stores, grain trailers or haystacks.","Bale and store hay only when it is fully dry: damp or green hay can heat up inside the stack and catch fire by itself, from two weeks to three months or more after baling.","Warning signs: steam rising from the stack, mold, or a burning, musty or sweet caramel smell. If you see or smell smoke, or part of the stack is very hot, do not pull it apart: keep people away and call 115.","Keep hay and straw stacks away from houses, machinery, fuel, chemicals and power lines."]},{heading:"Explosive ordnance and field fires",points:['If plowing or digging uncovers a strange object, stop work at once. Do not touch or move it, keep everyone away, move well away before you use a phone, and report it to 115 (see the lesson "Mines and unexploded ordnance: stay safe").',"Do not farm or graze animals on land that is marked as dangerous or that other people avoid. A place without warning signs is not necessarily safe: an unused, overgrown field next to farmed fields may hide mines.","If you think you are in a mined area: stop, stay still, call for help and wait for a trained team.",'Do not park tractors or cars on dry grass or stubble: a hot exhaust can set it on fire. Harvest fires are covered in the lesson "Crop, stubble and vegetation fires".']}],tips:["Lock pesticides away in their original, labeled containers.","Read the label before every use and wear the protective clothing it asks for.","Fasten the seat belt on a tractor with a roll bar, and keep every guard in place.","Stop the engine and wait until everything has stopped before you clean or unblock a machine.","Agree as a family where children may play: far from machines, grain stores, haystacks and sprayed fields."],warnings:["Never put pesticides or fuel in drink bottles or food containers.","Never let a child ride on a tractor or play near working machines.","Never go into a grain bin or silo while grain is moving, and never walk on grain to make it flow."],activity:{prompt:"Your neighbor is about to plow a field on a slope. His 8-year-old son asks to ride on the tractor's fender, and the father says it is only for a few minutes. What do you advise?",options:[{text:"The boy stays off the tractor and away from the field; the father fastens his seat belt and keeps off any slope that is too steep.",correct:!0,feedback:"A child can fall under the wheels or the plow, and a tractor can overturn on a slope in seconds. Children keep well away from working machines, and the driver wears the seat belt."},{text:"The boy can ride if he sits on the fender and holds on tightly with both hands.",correct:!1,feedback:"Children fall from fenders and are run over by the wheels or the plow. Nobody rides without their own seat and seat belt, and children never ride."},{text:"The boy can ride as long as his father drives very slowly on the slope.",correct:!1,feedback:"An overturn can happen in seconds, even at low speed, and a slope adds to the danger. The boy stays off the tractor."},{text:"The boy can walk behind the plow to watch how it turns the soil.",correct:!1,feedback:"A child walking near a moving plow can be run over or caught by it. Children stay well away from working machines."}]},quiz:[{question:"Where should pesticides be kept?",options:[{text:"Locked away in the original labeled container, far from food",correct:!0},{text:"In a clean drink bottle, with the name written on it in pen",correct:!1},{text:"On a high kitchen shelf, where the family can always find them",correct:!1},{text:"In an open box in the yard, where fresh air carries the fumes away",correct:!1}],explanation:"In a drink bottle or a kitchen a pesticide can be mistaken for a drink or reached by children. Keep it locked away in its original container with its label, away from food and animal feed."},{question:"A farm worker feels dizzy, cannot see clearly and feels sick after spraying. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Move him away into fresh air and call 122, keeping the label",correct:!0},{text:"Give him a glass of milk to wash the poison out of his stomach",correct:!1},{text:"Make him vomit at once, then let him rest in the shade",correct:!1},{text:"Let him finish the spraying, then take a shower at home",correct:!1}],explanation:"These can be signs of pesticide poisoning. Take the person away from the pesticide, call 122 and keep the container or label for the doctors; never make the person vomit and do not give milk or home remedies."},{question:"Why must the driver of a tractor with a roll bar (ROPS) wear the seat belt?",options:[{text:"To stay inside the protected zone if the tractor overturns",correct:!0},{text:"To avoid a fine when the tractor is driven on a main road",correct:!1},{text:"Because the seat belt makes the engine use less fuel",correct:!1},{text:"It is not needed, because the roll bar alone is enough",correct:!1}],explanation:"The roll bar protects only the space around the seat. Without the seat belt, the driver can be thrown out and crushed by the tractor."},{question:"What must you do before you unblock a machine driven by the PTO shaft?",options:[{text:"Stop the engine and wait until every part has stopped",correct:!0},{text:"Slow the engine down and keep your hands near the guard",correct:!1},{text:"Take off the guard so that you can reach the blockage",correct:!1},{text:"Ask a child to watch the shaft and shout if it speeds up",correct:!1}],explanation:"A turning shaft can catch clothing or hair in a moment. Stop the engine, wait until everything has stopped and keep every guard in place."},{question:"While plowing, you uncover a rusty metal object that looks like an old shell. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop work, keep everyone away and report it to 115",correct:!0},{text:"Carry it to the edge of the field so that work can go on",correct:!1},{text:"Plow around it, because an old rusty shell cannot explode",correct:!1},{text:"Tap it with a spade to check whether it is empty inside",correct:!1}],explanation:"Old and rusty ordnance can still explode if it is moved or struck. Do not touch it: stop work, keep everyone away, move well away before you phone and report it."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_farm_safety.webp",title:"Safe work on the farm",description:"A farmer wearing a seat belt drives a tractor with a roll bar while his children watch from behind the yard fence; pesticides stand in their labeled containers in a locked store."}]},{id:29,icon:"Mountain",accent:"#15803d",title:"Outdoor and mountain recreation",subtitle:"Picnics, barbecues, hiking and camping without fires, falls or getting lost",summary:"Many families enjoy picnics, hiking and camping in the mountains and valleys in spring and summer. Most accidents outdoors can be prevented with a little planning: a fire lit in the wrong place, a fall from rocks, a person who gets lost, a bite or sting, a sudden flood or a storm. This lesson explains how to plan a safe trip and what to do if something goes wrong far from help.",blocks:[{heading:"Before you go",points:["Tell someone who is not coming with you where you are going, which way you will go and when you will be back. Ask them to call for help if you are not back on time.","Check the weather forecast for the place you are going, not only for your town, and change your plans if storms or heavy rain are expected.","Take enough drinking water, food, a charged phone, a flashlight, a first aid kit, warm clothes and a whistle for every person, especially children.",'Stay on well-used paths and roads. In areas where there was fighting, unused paths and overgrown ground may hide mines or explosive ordnance: respect every warning sign (see the lesson "Mines and unexploded ordnance: stay safe").',"Always stay together as a group, and keep children where you can see them."]},{heading:"Picnic fires and barbecues",points:["Light a fire or a barbecue only where it is allowed. Ask about the local rules before you go.","Never light a fire on dry grass or on a dry, windy day. Clear away dry grass and leaves for at least 3 meters (10 feet) around it, and keep it at least 8 meters (25 feet) from trees, tents, cars and anything else that can burn.","Keep the fire small and never leave it alone, even for a few minutes. Keep children at least 1 meter (3 feet) from it, and never use gasoline or other fuels to light it or bring it back to life.","Keep water, or a shovel and soil, nearby. Before you leave, pour water on all the coals, stir and pour again, until there is no heat, glow, flame or smoke.","Never take a barbecue, a charcoal fire or hot coals into a tent, a car or a house, even to keep warm: they can cause a fire or carbon monoxide poisoning, which kills."]},{heading:"If you get lost",body:`Rescue can take hours, so make yourself easy to find:

1. Stop and stay calm. Stay where you are: moving makes it harder for rescuers to find you. Move only if you are in immediate danger, for example from rising water or a fire.
2. Stay together; nobody goes off alone to look for the way.
3. If you have a signal, call 115 and say where you started, where you last knew where you were, what you can see around you, how many you are and whether anyone is hurt or ill. Share your location from your phone if you can.
4. Blow your whistle three times, rest and repeat: a whistle carries further than your voice and saves your energy. Wear or spread out bright clothing so that you can be seen.
5. Find shelter from the sun, rain or cold and wait for help. Do not light a fire if there is any risk of a wildfire.

Teach children: if you get lost, stay where you are, "hug a tree" (or a big rock) and blow your whistle.`},{heading:"Falls and rocks",points:["Stay on the path and watch where you walk, especially near cliffs and on slippery ground.","Do not climb on rocks near waterfalls or streams: spray and algae make them slippery, and people have fallen to their deaths there.","Keep children close and watch them all the time near edges and water.","If someone falls and may have hurt the head, neck or back, do not move them unless the place is unsafe. Call 122, keep them still and warm, and wait for help."]},{heading:"Snakes and scorpions",points:["Wear closed shoes or boots and long pants. Do not put your hands under stones or into piles of wood or holes, and keep away from tall grass and piles of leaves where you can.","Shake out shoes, clothes and bedding before you use them.","Never touch, catch or try to kill a snake.","Snake bite: keep the person calm and still, take off rings, watches and anything tight before swelling starts, and get them to the hospital fast: call 122, and do not let them drive. Do not cut the bite, suck it, tie a tight band, put ice on it or use traditional remedies.","Scorpion sting: keep the person calm. You may put a cold pack wrapped in a cloth on the sting, but do not put the limb in ice water, and give no medicines. Get medical help for every sting, and call 122 at once if the person has trouble breathing, muscle twitching, drooling, slurred speech or strange restlessness."]},{heading:"Floods, lightning and heat",points:["Never camp, picnic or park in a dry riverbed or on low ground next to a stream: a flash flood can come in minutes, even if the rain falls far away. If the water starts to rise, climb to higher ground at once.",'Never walk or drive into flowing water (see the lesson "Floods for families").',"When thunder roars, go indoors: there is no safe place outside. Shelter in a solid building or a car with a metal roof; a tent or a picnic shelter gives no protection. Wait 30 minutes after the last thunder.","If you cannot reach shelter, keep away from hilltops, ridges, open fields, lone tall trees, water and metal fences, and spread out if you are in a group.",'Take plenty of water, rest in the shade and avoid hard walking in the hottest hours. Confusion or collapse in the heat can be heat stroke: call 122 and cool the person at once (see the lesson "Summer safety").']}],tips:["Tell someone where you are going and when you will be back.","Give every child a whistle and teach them to stay where they are if they get lost.","Light fires only where it is allowed, and put them out completely with water before you leave.","Shake out shoes and clothes before you put them on.","Check the forecast for the place you are going, and turn back when a storm comes."],warnings:["Never leave a fire or a barbecue burning, and never light one on dry grass or on a windy day.","Never take a barbecue or hot coals into a tent, a car or a house.","Never camp or park in a dry riverbed or next to a stream when rain is possible."],activity:{prompt:"You are hiking in the mountains with two friends and realize that you have lost the path. The sun will set in two hours and your phone has a weak signal. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We stop, stay together where we are, call 115 with our last known location and what we can see, and blow a whistle from time to time.",correct:!0,feedback:"Staying in one place makes it easier for rescuers to find you, and a call with clear location details and a whistle that carries far help them reach you."},{text:"We split up to look for the path in different directions and meet again later.",correct:!1,feedback:"Splitting up can turn one lost group into several lost people. Stay together and stay where you are."},{text:"We walk downhill as fast as we can, even after dark, until we find a road.",correct:!1,feedback:"Walking fast on unknown ground in the dark can lead to falls, and moving makes you harder to find. Stop, call for help and wait."},{text:"We light a big fire in the dry grass so that people can see the smoke.",correct:!1,feedback:"A fire in dry grass can spread and trap you. Signal with a whistle, a light or bright clothing, and call 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"When can you leave a picnic fire?",options:[{text:"When it has been soaked and stirred and no heat or smoke remains",correct:!0},{text:"When the flames have died down and only red coals are left",correct:!1},{text:"When the burning wood is covered with a layer of dry leaves",correct:!1},{text:"When the wind has turned and blows the smoke away from you",correct:!1}],explanation:"Coals can stay hot for hours, and the wind can carry them into dry grass. Pour water on all the coals, stir, pour again and check that no heat, glow, flame or smoke remains."},{question:"Your family is lost on a mountain. What is the best thing to do?",options:[{text:"Stay together where you are, call 115 and blow a whistle",correct:!0},{text:"Walk quickly in one direction until you reach a road or a village",correct:!1},{text:"Send the older children ahead alone to look for the path",correct:!1},{text:"Keep climbing to the highest point to wave, even after dark",correct:!1}],explanation:"Moving makes it harder for rescuers to find you and can lead to falls. Stay together where you are, call 115 with your last known location and signal with a whistle."},{question:"Someone is bitten on the leg by a snake. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Keep them still, remove tight items and get them to the hospital fast",correct:!0},{text:"Cut the bite and suck out the venom before you go to the hospital",correct:!1},{text:"Tie a tight band above the bite and put ice on the wound",correct:!1},{text:"Let them walk home quickly and treat the bite with a home remedy",correct:!1}],explanation:"Keeping still slows the spread of the venom, and hospital treatment is what helps. Cutting, sucking, tight bands, ice and traditional remedies only cause more harm."},{question:"Rain is forecast in the mountains. Which is the most dangerous place for your tent?",options:[{text:"In a dry riverbed or on low ground beside a stream",correct:!0},{text:"On a flat, raised area well away from the water",correct:!1},{text:"Next to your car in a gravel parking area on high ground",correct:!1},{text:"In a campsite on a hillside, well above the valley floor",correct:!1}],explanation:"A flash flood can fill a dry riverbed in minutes, even when the rain falls far away. Camp and picnic on higher ground, and climb up at once if water starts to rise."},{question:"Thunder starts while you are camping. Where is the safest place?",options:[{text:"In a solid building or a car with a metal roof",correct:!0},{text:"Inside your tent, with the door zipped shut",correct:!1},{text:"Under the tallest tree standing alone in the field",correct:!1},{text:"On the hilltop, where you can watch the storm",correct:!1}],explanation:"There is no safe place outside in a thunderstorm, and a tent gives no protection. Go to a solid building or a hard-topped car and stay there until 30 minutes after the last thunder."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_outdoor_safety.webp",title:"A safe family picnic in the mountains",description:"A family picnics on high ground, well away from a dry riverbed; the father pours water on the barbecue coals while a child with a whistle around his neck stays close by."}]},{id:30,icon:"Construction",accent:"#a16207",title:"After a building collapse: what citizens can do",subtitle:"Helping safely from outside, reporting trapped people and protecting yourself if you are trapped",summary:"A building can collapse after an earthquake or an explosion, in a fire, or because it is old or badly built. The first people to help are usually those nearby, before rescue teams arrive. But untrained people who rush into damaged buildings or onto rubble are often seriously hurt, and some have died. This lesson explains what you can safely do from outside, what to tell 115 and how to protect yourself if you are trapped.",blocks:[{heading:"Your safety comes first",points:["Never go into a damaged building and never climb onto rubble, even to save someone: rubble can shift and collapse, and an aftershock can bring down more.","If you are in a damaged building after the shaking stops, go out and move quickly away from it, to an open place away from buildings, trees, streetlights and power lines.","Signs that a building may fall: it leans or has moved off its base, part of it has already collapsed, there are large cracks in the foundation, or you hear creaking or groaning. Keep everyone away from it.","Expect aftershocks, sometimes for weeks. If you feel one: Drop, Cover, Hold On if you are inside, or stay in the open if you are outside.","If you smell gas, do not use switches, lighters or phones near the building: get away, warn others and call 115 from a safe place. Never touch fallen power lines or anything touching them."]},{heading:"Helping from outside",points:["Help people who can walk to get away from the building to a safe, open place, and keep onlookers back.","Give first aid to injured people you can reach without going into the building or onto the rubble, and call 122. Do not move a person who may have a neck or back injury unless the place is unsafe.","Do not pull a trapped person out by force and do not lift heavy pieces of rubble: other pieces can fall on them and on you. Leave this to the trained rescue team.","Do not dig into the rubble and do not crawl into gaps: they can close at any moment.",'From a safe place, call out and listen: "Can anyone hear me? Tap or call out." Note exactly where any voice or tapping comes from.',"When the rescue team arrives, tell them what you know and follow their instructions. Help only if they ask you to."]},{heading:"Calling 115: what to say",body:`Call 115 (and 122 for the injured) from a safe place and say:

1. Where: the area, the street, the building and a landmark; which side of the building and which floor.
2. What happened: a collapse, and any fire, smell of gas or fallen power lines.
3. People: how many may be trapped, where you heard voices or tapping, and how many are injured.
4. Your name and phone number. Do not hang up until you are told to.

When the rescue team arrives, tell them who lived in the building, who has been seen safe and who is still missing.`},{heading:"If you are trapped",body:`1. Move as little as possible, so that you do not kick up dust or bring down more debris.
2. Cover your nose and mouth with a cloth or your clothing.
3. Do not light a match or a lighter: there may be leaking gas.
4. Tap on a pipe or a wall, or blow a whistle, so that rescuers can find you. If your phone works, call 115 or send a message with your location.
5. Shout only as a last resort: shouting makes you breathe in dangerous amounts of dust.

Keep a whistle and a flashlight next to your bed and in your emergency kit.`},{heading:"In the hours and days after",points:["Use text messages rather than calls when you can, to keep the phone network free for emergencies.","Keep the streets clear for ambulances and rescue vehicles: do not drive into the area to look.","Children do not take part in clearing rubble or cleaning up.","Do not go back into a damaged building until engineers or the authorities say it is safe.","Follow official announcements from Civil Defense and the authorities, and do not spread rumors."]}],tips:["Keep a whistle and a flashlight next to your bed and in your emergency kit.","Agree with your family on a meeting place outside, away from buildings.","Save 115 and 122 in your phone, and know your street name and nearby landmarks.","After a collapse, call out and listen from a safe place, and tell the rescue team exactly where you heard someone.","Use text messages after a disaster to keep the phone lines free."],warnings:["Never go into a damaged building or climb onto rubble, even to rescue someone.","If you smell gas, do not use switches, lighters or phones there: get away and call 115 from a safe place.","Never touch fallen power lines or anything touching them."],activity:{prompt:"After an earthquake, part of a neighbor's house has collapsed. You hear a weak voice under the rubble, and several young men are starting to climb onto it to dig. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I call 115 with the exact place and where the voice is, ask the young men to come down and keep people back, and keep talking to the person from a safe place.",correct:!0,feedback:"Rubble can shift and aftershocks can bring down more. Exact information helps the rescue team, and talking to the trapped person from a safe place keeps them calm without putting more people in danger."},{text:"I climb up with them, because every minute counts and more hands dig faster.",correct:!1,feedback:"More weight on the rubble can make it collapse onto the trapped person and onto the people digging. Stay off the rubble and call 115."},{text:"I pull out the large concrete pieces at the edge to open a way in.",correct:!1,feedback:"Pulling out pieces can bring down the debris that holds up the space where the person is breathing. Leave heavy debris to the trained team."},{text:"I wait until the morning to report it, because the rescue teams are busy now.",correct:!1,feedback:"Trapped people need help as early as possible. Call 115 at once and give the exact location."}]},quiz:[{question:"You are trapped under debris after an earthquake. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Cover your mouth, and tap on a pipe or wall or use a whistle",correct:!0},{text:"Light a match so that you can see the space around you",correct:!1},{text:"Shout as loudly as you can, without stopping, until help comes",correct:!1},{text:"Push hard on the debris above you to make a way out quickly",correct:!1}],explanation:"A flame can ignite leaking gas, constant shouting makes you breathe in dust, and pushing can bring down more debris. Tapping and a whistle help rescuers find you; shout only as a last resort."},{question:"What is the safest way for a citizen to help after a building collapse?",options:[{text:"Help people outside the building and report trapped people to 115",correct:!0},{text:"Go into the damaged building and check every room for survivors",correct:!1},{text:"Climb onto the rubble and lift the heavy pieces one by one",correct:!1},{text:"Drive your car into the street so that you can see what is happening",correct:!1}],explanation:"Untrained people who go into damaged buildings or onto rubble are often hurt or killed. Help from outside, keep the streets clear for rescue vehicles and report trapped people to 115."},{question:"What should you tell 115 first when you report people trapped in a collapse?",options:[{text:"The exact location: the area, the street, the building and a landmark",correct:!0},{text:"Your opinion about who built the building and why it fell down",correct:!1},{text:"Only that something has happened somewhere in your neighborhood",correct:!1},{text:"The full names of everyone who has ever lived in the building",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rescue teams must find the place quickly. Give the location first, then what happened, how many may be trapped, where you heard them and any dangers, and do not hang up until you are told to."},{question:"Why should a trapped person shout only as a last resort?",options:[{text:"Shouting makes you breathe in dangerous amounts of dust",correct:!0},{text:"Rescue teams are trained to listen only for tapping sounds",correct:!1},{text:"The noise of shouting can make the rubble above you collapse",correct:!1},{text:"Shouting quickly uses up the battery of your mobile phone",correct:!1}],explanation:"Dust in a collapsed building can harm your lungs, and shouting makes you breathe in more of it. Tapping on a pipe or wall and a whistle carry well and protect your breathing."},{question:"You smell gas near a collapsed building. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Use no switches or flames there, get away and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Go in quickly and close the gas valve in the kitchen",correct:!1},{text:"Switch on the lights in the hallway to see the leak",correct:!1},{text:"Hold a lighter near the pipes to find where the leak is",correct:!1}],explanation:"A spark or a flame can ignite the gas and cause an explosion. Never go into the building to reach a valve: get away, warn others and call 115 from a safe place."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_collapse_citizen_help.webp",title:"Helping safely after a collapse",description:"Neighbors stay back from a partly collapsed house; one woman calls 115 and points to where a voice was heard, while others help an injured man to an open area away from buildings and power lines."}]},{id:31,icon:"Users",accent:"#0f766e",title:"Crowd safety at gatherings",subtitle:"Weddings, festivals, religious occasions, stadiums and markets",summary:"Weddings, festivals, religious occasions, matches and busy markets bring many people together. When too many people crowd into a small space, or everyone rushes toward one point, people can be crushed or trampled. In crowd disasters, most people die because they are squeezed so hard that they cannot breathe. This lesson explains how to recognize a dangerous crowd early, what to do if you are caught in one, how to protect children and what organizers must do.",blocks:[{heading:"When you arrive",points:["Look for all the exits as soon as you arrive, and find at least two ways out: in an emergency the door you came in by may be blocked.","Check that the exits are not locked, chained or blocked. If there are not at least two ways out, tell the organizers, and leave if it is not fixed at once.","Note where the first aid point is, and the information or lost-children point if there is one.","Agree with your family or friends on a meeting place in case you are separated.","Stay near the edges of the crowd or in less crowded areas, on level, dry ground.","If the place feels overcrowded or unsafe, leave early."]},{heading:"Signs of a dangerous crowd",body:"Leave early, while you can still move freely, if you notice:",points:["There is no space left between you and the people around you, and more people keep pushing in.","The crowd starts to sway or surge in waves, and you are moved where you do not want to go.","People are pushing or rushing, for example toward an entrance, a stage or a free distribution.","People are being pressed against walls, barriers or fences.","It becomes hard to breathe."]},{heading:"If you are caught in a crowd surge",points:["Keep your arms up in front of your chest, like a boxer, to keep space to breathe.","Stay on your feet: plant them firmly and try not to fall. Do not bend down to pick anything up.","Do not push against the crowd. If the people in front of you stop, do not push: hold on and ask those behind you to hold back too.","When the pressure eases for a moment, move diagonally across the crowd toward its edge.","Keep away from walls, barriers and fences, where people can be crushed.","If you fall, curl into a ball, protect your head and get up as soon as you can. If someone near you falls, ask the people around you to hold back and help them up."]},{heading:"Children, older people and people with disabilities",points:["Keep small children close, hold their hands and make sure they have enough space.","Put a card in your child's pocket with your name and phone number.","Show children the meeting place, and teach them to go there if they lose you.","Stay with older people and people with disabilities near the edges of the crowd and close to an exit."]},{heading:"What organizers must do",points:["Know the safe capacity of the place, including the areas where people queue, gather and leave, and never let in more people than it can hold safely.","Keep every exit unlocked and every route to it clear for the whole event; exit doors must open from the inside without a key.","Have enough trained stewards to guide people, watch for pushing and crowd build-up, and stop entry when an area is full.","Plan first aid and medical help for the expected crowd, and agree in advance with Civil Defense and the ambulance service how they will reach the place.","Have a public address system and clear emergency instructions, and report signs of a dangerous build-up at once.","Plan entry and exit so that people do not rush toward one point, such as a single gate or a free distribution.","Follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the local authorities for permits and safety arrangements."]},{heading:"If something goes wrong",points:["If an alarm sounds, or you see smoke, fire or a sudden disturbance, leave calmly at once by the nearest exit.","Move at the pace of the crowd; do not run and do not push.","Once you are out, stay out. Go to your meeting place and tell the rescue teams if someone is missing.","Do not spread rumors; follow the instructions of the stewards and Civil Defense."]}],tips:["Find two ways out as soon as you arrive.","Agree on a meeting place with your family before you go in.","Stay near the edges of the crowd, especially with children.","Leave early if the crowd keeps growing and you can no longer move freely.","Put a card with your phone number in your child's pocket."],warnings:["Never push in a crowd; if the people in front stop, hold back and ask those behind you to stop.","Organizers: never lock or block an exit while people are inside.","Never let in more people than the place can hold safely."],activity:{prompt:"At a crowded festival, the crowd suddenly surges toward the stage. You are pushed forward and can barely move your arms. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep my arms up in front of my chest, stay on my feet, do not push back, and when the pressure eases I move diagonally toward the edge.",correct:!0,feedback:"Arms in front of the chest keep space to breathe, staying on your feet keeps you from being trampled, and moving diagonally during a lull takes you out of the strongest pressure."},{text:"I push back hard against the people behind me to make more space.",correct:!1,feedback:"Pushing adds to the pressure on everyone and can make people fall. Do not resist the force of the crowd; wait for a lull."},{text:"I bend down to pick up my phone, which has fallen to the ground.",correct:!1,feedback:"Bending down in a surge can make you fall and be trampled. Stay on your feet and leave the phone."},{text:"I move toward the barrier at the front, where I can hold on to something.",correct:!1,feedback:"People are crushed against barriers and walls. Move away from them, diagonally toward the edge of the crowd."}]},quiz:[{question:"Why should you find two exits when you arrive at a wedding hall or a stadium?",options:[{text:"The door you came in by may be blocked in an emergency",correct:!0},{text:"The organizers may ask you to sit near one of the exits",correct:!1},{text:"It is polite for guests to know where the doors of a hall are",correct:!1},{text:"The second exit is always closer to the parking area outside",correct:!1}],explanation:"In emergencies most people head for the door they came in by, and it can quickly become blocked. A second way out lets you leave by the nearest safe exit."},{question:"What should you do with your arms if you are caught in a crowd surge?",options:[{text:"Keep them up in front of your chest, like a boxer",correct:!0},{text:"Hold them straight down by your sides to take less room",correct:!1},{text:"Raise them high above your head so that people can see you",correct:!1},{text:"Use them to push the people in front of you out of the way",correct:!1}],explanation:"Arms in front of the chest keep a little space for your lungs. In crowd crushes, most people die because they are squeezed so hard that they cannot breathe."},{question:"During a crowd surge, the pressure eases for a moment. Which way should you move?",options:[{text:"Diagonally across the crowd toward its edge",correct:!0},{text:"Straight back, against the flow of the crowd",correct:!1},{text:"Forward, toward the barrier at the front",correct:!1},{text:"Down to the ground, to rest until it is over",correct:!1}],explanation:"Moving diagonally toward the edge takes you out of the strongest pressure without fighting the crowd. Barriers and the ground are where people are crushed or trampled."},{question:"Which of these is an organizer's duty at a large wedding or festival?",options:[{text:"Keep every exit unlocked and clear while guests are inside",correct:!0},{text:"Lock the side doors so that nobody enters without an invitation",correct:!1},{text:"Let in everyone who comes, even after the hall is full",correct:!1},{text:"Hand out free gifts at one narrow gate to please the guests",correct:!1}],explanation:"Exits must open from the inside without a key, and the routes to them must stay clear. Organizers must also keep to the safe capacity and avoid anything that makes people rush to one point."},{question:"What helps most if your child gets separated from you in a crowd?",options:[{text:"A meeting place agreed in advance and a card with your phone number",correct:!0},{text:"A promise that you will search every corner of the festival ground",correct:!1},{text:"A rule that the child always walks alone at the front of the family",correct:!1},{text:"A plan for the child to go home alone by bus if they cannot find you",correct:!1}],explanation:"A meeting place the child knows and a card with your phone number help stewards and other adults bring you together quickly. A child should never leave alone."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_crowd_safety.webp",title:"Staying safe in a crowd",description:"Families at a festival stand near the edge of the crowd beside a clearly marked, open exit; a steward in a bright vest guides people, and a child has a card with a parent's phone number in a pocket."}]},{id:32,icon:"PlugZap",accent:"#a16207",title:"Home electrical and battery safety",subtitle:"Outlets and extension cords, chargers, e-bikes and e-scooters, lithium-ion batteries and first aid for electric shock",summary:"Every home is full of electrical equipment and rechargeable batteries: outlets and extension cords, phone chargers, power banks, e-bikes and e-scooters. An overloaded outlet, a damaged cable or a failing battery can start a fire, and an electric shock can kill. This lesson explains how to use them safely, how to recognize the warning signs, what to do if a battery overheats or catches fire, and how to help a person who has had an electric shock.",blocks:[{heading:"Outlets, extension cords and cables",points:["Do not overload outlets, extension cords or power strips: too many appliances on one outlet can overheat it and start a fire.","Never plug an extension cord or a power strip into another one (daisy-chaining).","Extension cords are for temporary use only. If you need one all the time, have a qualified electrician check whether more outlets are needed.","Plug heaters, refrigerators, washing machines and other large appliances directly into a wall outlet, never into an extension cord or a power strip.","Do not run cables under carpets or rugs, across doorways or where furniture can crush them.","Replace cracked or damaged cables and extension cords; do not try to repair them yourself.","Do not touch outlets, switches or appliances with wet hands."]},{heading:"Warning signs in outlets and wiring",body:"If you notice any of these signs, stop using the outlet or appliance, switch it off and unplug it if that is safe, and have a qualified electrician find and fix the cause. If there is smoke or fire, get everyone out, close the door and call 115:",points:["Outlets or switches that are warm or discolored, or plugs that get hot.","A smell of burning or hot plastic near an outlet or an appliance.","Sparks, buzzing or crackling sounds, or scorch marks around an outlet or a plug.","Lights that flicker, or fuses and circuit breakers that keep blowing or tripping.","A tingling feeling when you touch an appliance or a metal sink."]},{heading:"Chargers, e-bikes and e-scooters",points:["Phones, laptops, power banks, e-bikes and e-scooters have lithium-ion (Li-ion) batteries. They store a lot of energy, and a damaged battery or the wrong charger can make them overheat, catch fire or explode.","Use the charger that came with the device, or one that the manufacturer recommends. Buy chargers and batteries from reliable sellers: cheap copies may not meet safety standards.","Charge on a hard, flat surface such as a table or the floor, never on a bed, a sofa or under a pillow, where the heat cannot escape.","Do not leave devices charging while you sleep or when nobody is at home, and unplug the charger when the battery is full.","Charge e-bikes and e-scooters outside the home if you can. Never charge or keep them in a hallway, by the front door or on the stairs, where a fire would block the way out.","Fit a smoke alarm where you charge e-bikes and e-scooters, and let the battery cool down after a ride before you charge it.","Do not use a battery that is damaged, or one that has been modified or repaired by someone who is not qualified."]},{heading:"A battery that overheats, smokes or burns",body:"Warning signs: the battery gets very hot, swells or changes shape or color, leaks, smells strange, hisses, crackles or pops, or gives off smoke. Lithium-ion battery fires grow fast, are hard to put out and give off toxic smoke and gases.",points:["If there is no smoke or fire, stop using the battery at once. If it is safe, switch the device off and unplug the charger, then ask the manufacturer or the seller what to do.","If the battery smokes or burns, get everyone out at once, close the door behind you and call 115 from outside. Stay outside.","Do not try to put out or cool a burning battery yourself unless you are trained: leave it to the firefighters.","Do not throw old or damaged batteries in the household trash; ask where they can be collected safely."]},{heading:"After floods and earthquakes, and neighborhood generator cables",points:["Electricity and water together can kill: never touch switches, outlets or appliances while you are wet or standing in water.","After a flood, keep the electricity off until a qualified electrician has checked the house. If you would have to step into water to reach the main switch, call an electrician instead.","Do not use appliances, chargers, e-bikes or e-scooters that have been in water until a qualified person has checked them.","After an earthquake, watch out for fallen power lines and damaged wiring and stay well away from them. Treat a fallen line, and anything touching it, as live, and call 115.","If you smell gas, do not switch any light or appliance on or off: get everyone out, warn the neighbors and call 115 from outside.","Neighborhood generator cables: do not touch them or connect anything to them yourself. Report loose or damaged cables, sparks or a burning smell to the generator operator, and call 115 in an emergency. Only a qualified electrician should connect your home to the neighborhood line."]},{heading:"Electric shock: first aid",body:`1. Do not touch the person while they may still be in contact with electricity: you could get a shock too.
2. Switch off the power first: pull out the plug or switch off the main switch at the fuse box, if you can do it safely.
3. If you cannot switch off the power, or a high-voltage line or a fallen power line is involved, keep well away, keep others away and call 115 and 122.
4. When the power is off, call 122. If the person is not breathing normally, start CPR (compressions only if you are not trained) and follow the dispatcher's instructions; use an AED if one is available.
5. Even if the person seems well, keep watching them and get medical advice from 122: an electric shock can cause deep burns and internal injuries that cannot be seen.`}],tips:["Know where the main switch (the fuse box) of your home is, so that you can switch off the power quickly and safely.","Plug heaters and other large appliances directly into a wall outlet.","Unplug chargers and small appliances when you are not using them.","Fit a smoke alarm where you charge e-bikes or e-scooters, and test the smoke alarms in your home every month.","Buy chargers, batteries and extension cords from reliable sellers, and use only the charger that the manufacturer recommends."],warnings:["Never touch a person who may still be in contact with electricity: switch off the power first.","Never charge an e-bike or e-scooter in the hallway or by the front door, and never leave it charging while everyone is asleep.","If a battery smokes or catches fire, get out, close the door and call 115; do not try to put it out yourself."],activity:{prompt:"It is late at night. Your son's e-scooter is charging in the hallway next to the front door. You hear the battery hissing and notice a strong chemical smell. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I wake everyone and get them out at once, we close the doors behind us and I call 115 from outside; nobody stays behind to deal with the scooter.",correct:!0,feedback:"Hissing and a chemical smell are signs that the battery is failing and may catch fire very quickly. In the hallway a fire would block the way out, so getting everyone out first and calling 115 is right."},{text:"I pour a bucket of water over the battery to cool it down and stay next to it to watch.",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not try to cool or put out a failing battery yourself unless you are trained: it can suddenly burst into flames and give off toxic smoke. Get everyone out and call 115."},{text:"I carry the scooter out through the front door to the street so that it cannot set the house on fire.",correct:!1,feedback:"A hissing battery can catch fire at any moment: carrying it puts you in danger, and a fire in the hallway would block the way out. Get everyone out first and call 115."},{text:"I unplug the charger, open a window and go back to bed, and I check the battery in the morning.",correct:!1,feedback:"These are the warning signs of a battery that may catch fire. Leaving it in the hallway while everyone sleeps puts the whole family at risk: get out and call 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"What is the safe way to use extension cords and power strips at home?",options:[{text:"For a short time only, and never plugged into another cord or strip",correct:!0},{text:"Plug a second power strip into the first when you need more outlets",correct:!1},{text:"Run the cord under the carpet so that nobody trips over the cable",correct:!1},{text:"Use one for the heater and the refrigerator together to save outlets",correct:!1}],explanation:"Extension cords are for temporary use. Plugging one into another can overload the outlet and start a fire, cables under carpets can be crushed and damaged, and heaters and large appliances go directly into a wall outlet."},{question:"Where is the safest place to charge a phone or a power bank?",options:[{text:"On a hard, flat surface such as a table, unplugged once it is full",correct:!0},{text:"On the bed under a pillow, so that it stays out of the way at night",correct:!1},{text:"On the sofa all night, so that the battery is full in the morning",correct:!1},{text:"Next to a heater, where the warm air helps the battery charge faster",correct:!1}],explanation:"Soft surfaces such as beds, pillows and sofas trap heat, and a battery that cannot cool down can overheat and catch fire. Charge on a hard, flat surface, not while you sleep, and unplug the charger when the battery is full."},{question:"Which of these is a warning sign that a lithium-ion battery is failing?",options:[{text:"It swells, gets very hot or makes a hissing sound",correct:!0},{text:"It takes a little longer to charge than when it was new",correct:!1},{text:"Its charging cable is a different color from the device",correct:!1},{text:"The phone screen gets dimmer by itself in the evening",correct:!1}],explanation:"Strong heat, swelling or a change of shape or color, leaks, a strange smell, hissing or popping sounds and smoke are warning signs. Stop using the battery at once; if it smokes or burns, get out and call 115."},{question:"A power bank on the table starts to smoke. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Get everyone out, close the door and call 115 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"Pour a glass of water on it and stay until you see it go out",correct:!1},{text:"Put it in the refrigerator so that it cools down more quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Pick it up and carry it quickly to the kitchen sink",correct:!1}],explanation:"Lithium-ion battery fires grow fast and give off toxic smoke and gases. Do not try to put out, cool or carry a burning battery unless you are trained: get out, stay out and call 115 from outside."},{question:"After an electric shock, a man lies on the floor still holding the cable of an appliance. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Switch off the power at the plug or the main switch, without touching him",correct:!0},{text:"Grab his arm and pull him away from the cable as quickly as you possibly can",correct:!1},{text:"Pour water on his hands to cool the burns before doing anything else",correct:!1},{text:"Start CPR at once, even while he is still holding the cable",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never touch a person who may still be in contact with electricity: you could get a shock too. Switch off the power first, then call 122, and start CPR if he is not breathing normally."}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_home_electrical_battery.webp",title:"Safe charging and outlets at home",description:"A phone charging on a table, an e-scooter charging outside the home away from the door, a wall outlet with only one appliance plugged in and a smoke alarm on the ceiling."}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
